TI  - An optimal control algorithm for borehole thermal energy storage systems
T2  - Energy and Buildings
VL  - 43
PY  - 2011
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2011
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enbuild.2011.07.015
AB  - The aim of this paper is to present an optimal control algorithm to manage borehole thermal energy storage systems (BTES). Such a system gets exhausted, if it is employed intensively, i.e. outlet fluid is outside acceptable temperature ranges, and can no longer provide the desired heat or cold. To avoid this problem a control algorithm is proposed, which simultaneously optimizes the operation costs. This algorithm is based on a dynamic programming technique and results in an array which provides the optimal heat flux for a given field temperature, date and demand. The controller is illustrated on a simulation of an existing building. Several weather scenarios have been examined and the system remained robust under all situations.
KW  - Optimal control
KW  - dynamic programming
KW  - HVAC
KW  - heat flux
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. The aim of this paper is to present an optimal control algorithm to manage borehole thermal energy storage systems (BTES). 
N1  - C2. Such a system gets exhausted, if it is employed intensively, i.e. outlet fluid is outside acceptable temperature ranges, and can no longer provide the desired heat or cold. To avoid this problem a control algorithm is proposed, which simultaneously optimizes the operation costs. This algorithm is based on a dynamic programming technique and results in an array which provides the optimal heat flux for a given field temperature, date and demand. 
N1  - C3. A dynamic programming controller is able to operate the system within a given set of constraints. 
N1  - C4. The uncontrolled BTES installation operates at a lower cost. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. In most buildings more than 40% of the energy consumed is used for heating and cooling purposes. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The aim of this paper is to present an optimal control algorithm to manage borehole thermal energy storage systems. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The aim of this paper is to present an optimal control algorithm to manage borehole thermal energy storage systems (BTES). 
N1  - E5. Such a system gets exhausted, if it is employed intensively, i.e. outlet fluid is outside acceptable temperature ranges, and can no longer provide the desired heat or cold. To avoid this problem a control algorithm is proposed, which simultaneously optimizes the operation costs. This algorithm is based on a dynamic programming technique and results in an array which provides the optimal heat flux for a given field temperature, date and demand. 
N1  - E6. A dynamic programming controller is able to operate the system within a given set of constraints. 
N1  - E7. The uncontrolled BTES installation operates at a lower cost. 
N1  - E8. Objectives. 
N1  - E9. The aim of this paper is to present an optimal control algorithm to manage borehole thermal energy storage systems (BTES). 
N1  - E10. Introduction To Optimal Control. 
N1  - E11. The aim of this paper is to present an optimal control algorithm to manage borehole thermal energy storage systems (BTES) [1].. 
N1  - E12. Such a system gets exhausted if it is employed intensively, and can no longer provide the desired heat or cold.. 
N1  - E13. The proposed algorithm is based on a dynamic programming technique and results in an array which provides the optimal heat flux for a given field temperature, date and demand.. 
N1  - E14. Key Components Of The Algorithm. 
N1  - E15. The algorithm takes into account the dynamics of the underground storage system, which is described by a first order response model [11].. 
N1  - E16. The model enables the prediction of underground temperature changes due to a given operation strategy over relatively short periods.. 
N1  - E17. The algorithm also considers the constraints, including temperature constraints, energy fluxes from the field, and guarantees towards the client about the delivered heat and cold.. 
N1  - E18. Application And Benefits. 
N1  - E19. The system used for this study is based on a scheme which has been installed in Flanders, Belgium, and is in operation since 2007 [6,7].. 
N1  - E20. The algorithm is illustrated on a simulation of an existing building, and several weather scenarios have been examined.. 
N1  - E21. The system remained robust under all situations, and the algorithm can guarantee to supply all needed heat and cold while reducing the investment cost [8,9].. 
N1  - E22. The algorithm can also optimize the choice between the classical heating and cooling installation and the heat pump coupled to the storage field, resulting in lower operation costs [4,5].. 
N1  - E23. Model. 
N1  - E24. The duct ground heat storage model (DST) is used to simulate the response of the real underground [12].. 
N1  - E25. A simple model (Eq (1)) is optimized to predict the water temperature computed by the DST module, with a root-mean-square error of 0.0485 ◦C and a maximum deviation of 0.0857 ◦C.. 
N1  - E26. Constraints. 
N1  - E27. The system has several constraints, including:. 
N1  - E28. Pump limitations: the pumps are limited to 78 m3/h, corresponding to a maximum heat flux of 343 kW [12].. 
N1  - E29. Temperature bounds: the underground temperature should remain above 0◦C and below 12 ◦C.. 
N1  - E30. Heat and cold supply: the system should be able to deliver continuously 200 kW heat or cold, if desired, during winter and summer respectively.. 
N1  - E31. Control Algorithm. 
N1  - E32. A dynamic programming algorithm is used to find the optimal control parameters, taking into account the constraints and the cost function [10].. 
N1  - E33. The algorithm discretizes time in steps of one week and divides the demand and delivered heat or cold into intervals.. 
N1  - E34. The optimal control parameters are those values that minimize the cost, and the cost function is updated recursively until it converges to the optimal value.. 
N1  - E35. Control Policy. 
N1  - E36. The control policy for the underground heat storage field is based on the field's temperature, with different actions taken depending on the temperature range [12].. 
N1  - E37. Between 0.5 and 1.5 ◦C, additional heat is supplied to drive the field towards optimal temperatures.. 
N1  - E38. Between 1.5 and 3.6 ◦C, the temperature is optimal and the demanded cold is supplied.. 
N1  - E39. If the temperature exceeds 3.6 ◦C, heat is extracted and drained.. 
N1  - E40. The control policy is shown in Fig. 7 for a typical week in every month of the year.. 
N1  - E41. Simulation Results. 
N1  - E42. The TRNSYS simulation environment combined with the DST module [12] was used to simulate a real underground heat storage field.. 
N1  - E43. The results of seven scenarios are summarized in Table 3, with the optimal control algorithm optimizing towards minimal operation costs.. 
N1  - E44. The estimated heating and cooling values for the building are used, with adjustments made for unpredictable weather.. 
N1  - E45. The BTES is cheaper than the classical installation under all combinations.. 
N1  - E46. Controller Evaluation. 
N1  - E47. The optimal control policy is compared to the classical controller, with caution taken due to the classical controller not accounting for minimum delivery constraints [12].. 
N1  - E48. The costs of both control policies are summarized in Table 3, with the optimal control policy being slightly cheaper in some scenarios.. 
N1  - E49. The optimal control policy guarantees a continuous heat and/or cold supply, with no constraints violated in any scenario.. 
N1  - E50. The controller's performance is evaluated, with suggestions for future improvements, such as taking into account the outlet temperature. 
