AU  - Lundqvist, Per 
TI  - Capacity control in ground source heat pump systems part II: Comparative analysis between on/off controlled and variable capacity systems
T2  - International Journal of Refrigeration
VL  - 34
PY  - 2011
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2011
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.ijrefrig.2011.05.012
AB  - In the present paper, as the second part of two, modeling and simulation was carried out for a Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) system in the presence of all the most important interacting sub-systems such as building, ground heat source, electrical auxiliary heater, and the heat pump unit in order to make a fair and comprehensive comparison between the annual performance of on/off controlled and variable capacity systems. The annual modeling showed that dimensioning of the on/off controlled GSHP based on the peak heat demand of the building plays a significant role when the two control strategies are compared: if the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover only 55% of the peak heat demand of the building, the electrical auxiliary, which then covers about 10% of the annual heating demand of the building, makes the SPF of the on/off controlled GSHP to be lower than the one of the variable speed system. On the contrary, when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building’s peak heat demand, i.e. more than 95% of the annual heat demand of the building, there is no considerable difference between the SPFs of the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems.
KW  - heat pump
KW  - Ground Source Heat Pumps
KW  - Control
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Brine to water heat pump systems like Ground Source Heat Pumps (GSHPs) are one of the common and fast growing systems for heating the buildings in USA and European countries. 
N1  - C2. The presents paper aims at making a fair comparison between the annual performance of an on/off and variable capacity GSHP systems, using the generic model developed by Madani et al (2011). 
N1  - C3. The current paper compares the annual performance of the on/off controlled and inverter-driven variable capacity heat pump systems, using the generic model developed by Madani et al (2011). 
N1  - C4. The results from the annual modeling for both systems indicate: C When the annual performance of on/off controlled GSHP system is compared with the one of the variable speed GSHP system, it must be noticed that dimensioning of the on/off controlled GSHP based on the peak heat demand of the building plays a significant role: if the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover only 55% of the peak heat demand of the building, the important role of the electrical auxiliary heater makes the Seasonal Performance Factor (SPF) of the on/off controlled GSHP be lower than the one of the variable speed system. 
N1  - C5. On the contrary, when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building’s peak heat demand, i.e. more than 95% of the annual heat demand of the building, there is no considerable difference between the SPFs of the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems. 
N1  - C6. C The energy use by liquid pumps in the variable speed GSHP system can be 5e30% higher compared to the single speed system. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. As of 2005, there are over a million units installed worldwide providing about 15 GW of thermal capacity (Rybach, 2005) and the annual global increase has been more than 10% over the last ten years (Lund et al, 2004). 
N1  - D2. As may be seen from Fig. 9, the variable capacity system has higher efficiency than unit A due to avoiding the electrical auxiliary heater; Furthermore, the modeling results show that there is no significant difference between the COP of system B, system C, and the variable speed system. 
N1  - D3. As shown in Fig. 12, the SPF of unit A is about 13% lower than the SPF of the variable speed system whereas the SPF of system B, C, and D are almost the same as the one of variable speed system. 
N1  - D4. On the contrary, when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building’s peak heat demand, i.e. more than 95% of the annual heat demand of the building, there is no considerable difference between the SPFs of the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems. 
N1  - D5. C The energy use by liquid pumps in the variable speed GSHP system can be 5e30% higher compared to the single speed system. 
N1  - D6. As a final remark, in order to obtain a higher efficiency for on/off controlled systems, it is important to dimension them in a way that electrical auxiliary heater covers less than 5% of annual heat demand. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The presents paper aims at making a fair comparison between the annual performance of an on/off and variable capacity Ground Source Heat Pump systems, using the generic model developed by previous research. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The presents paper aims at making a fair comparison between the annual performance of an on/off and variable capacity GSHP systems, using the generic model developed by Madani et al (2011). 
N1  - E5. The current paper compares the annual performance of the on/off controlled and inverter-driven variable capacity heat pump systems, using the generic model developed by Madani et al (2011). 
N1  - E6. The results from the annual modeling for both systems indicate: C When the annual performance of on/off controlled GSHP system is compared with the one of the variable speed GSHP system, it must be noticed that dimensioning of the on/off controlled GSHP based on the peak heat demand of the building plays a significant role: if the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover only 55% of the peak heat demand of the building, the important role of the electrical auxiliary heater makes the Seasonal Performance Factor (SPF) of the on/off controlled GSHP be lower than the one of the variable speed system. 
N1  - E7. On the contrary, when the on/off controlled system is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building’s peak heat demand, i.e. more than 95% of the annual heat demand of the building, there is no considerable difference between the SPFs of the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems. 
N1  - E8. C The energy use by liquid pumps in the variable speed GSHP system can be 5e30% higher compared to the single speed system. 
N1  - E9. Control Strategies. 
N1  - E10. The study compares the annual performance of on/off controlled and variable capacity Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) systems (Madani et al, 2011; Zhao et al, 2003; Karlsson and Fahlen, 2007).. 
N1  - E11. The on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover 55-70% of the peak heat demand, while the variable capacity system modulates the compressor speed to match the building heat demand.. 
N1  - E12. The results show that if the on/off controlled GSHP covers only 55% of the peak heat demand, the electrical auxiliary heater covers about 10% of the annual heating demand, resulting in a lower Seasonal Performance Factor (SPF) compared to the variable capacity system.. 
N1  - E13. System Modeling. 
N1  - E14. The study uses a detailed model of the GSHP system, including the building, ground heat exchanger, and climate (Klein, 2005; Madani et al, 2011).. 
N1  - E15. The building model is created using TRNBUILD, an interface in TRNSYS, and takes into account heat losses, infiltration, ventilation, internal gains, and solar gains.. 
N1  - E16. The heat pump unit model is also detailed, with individual models for the compressor, evaporator, and condenser.. 
N1  - E17. The system is simulated over a year to evaluate the annual performance of the two control strategies.. 
N1  - E18. Efficiency Comparison. 
N1  - E19. The study finds that when the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building's peak heat demand, there is no significant difference in the SPF between the on/off controlled and variable capacity systems (Karlsson, 2007; Cuevas and Lebrun, 2009).. 
N1  - E20. However, if the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover only 55% of the peak heat demand, the variable capacity system has a higher SPF due to the reduced need for electrical auxiliary heating.. 
N1  - E21. The results highlight the importance of proper sizing of the GSHP system to optimize its efficiency (Rybach, 2005; Lund et al, 2004; Forsen and Nowacki, 2011).. 
N1  - E22. Balance Point. 
N1  - E23. The balance point temperature is the ambient temperature at which the heat demand of the building equals the heat capacity of the HP unit (Madani et al, 2011).. 
N1  - E24. The balance point temperatures for the studied HP units are given in Table 2.. 
N1  - E25. On the right side of the balance point, the HP unit's capacity is higher than the building's heat demand, and the compressor turns on and off intermittently.. 
N1  - E26. On the left side, the building's heat demand is higher than the HP unit's capacity, and an electrical auxiliary heater is activated to compensate for the deficit.. 
N1  - E27. Variable Speed Unit. 
N1  - E28. A variable speed GSHP unit was modeled with a frequency inverter to change the compressor speed and heat capacity (Madani et al, 2011).. 
N1  - E29. The balance point temperature for the variable speed unit is about -1°C, which is 4K higher than the smallest on/off controlled HP unit.. 
N1  - E30. The variable speed unit's compressor speed varies between 30 Hz and 90 Hz to meet the building's heat demand.. 
N1  - E31. The unit's COP is affected by the compressor speed, and the inverter losses reduce the unit's performance compared to the single speed unit.. 
N1  - E32. System Performance. 
N1  - E33. The performance of the single speed and variable speed HP units was analyzed over a year using climatic data for Stockholm (Meteotest, 2009).. 
N1  - E34. The results show that the variable speed unit can have a higher COP than the single speed unit at low frequencies, but the single speed unit can have a better performance due to inverter, motor, or compressor losses (Wemhoener and Afjei, 2006).. 
N1  - E35. The variable speed unit's annual energy use is lower than the single speed units, except for unit D, which is oversized.. 
N1  - E36. The variable speed system has higher efficiency than unit A due to avoiding the electrical auxiliary heater.. 
N1  - E37. Dimensioning. 
N1  - E38. The dimensioning of on/off controlled Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) systems plays a significant role in their annual performance, with systems dimensioned to cover more than 65% of the building's peak heat demand showing no considerable difference in Seasonal Performance Factor (SPF) compared to variable speed systems (Madani et al, 2011).. 
N1  - E39. If the on/off controlled GSHP is dimensioned to cover only 55% of the peak heat demand, the electrical auxiliary heater's role becomes more important, making the SPF of the on/off controlled GSHP lower than the variable speed system.. 
N1  - E40. Energy Consumption. 
N1  - E41. The energy use by liquid pumps in the variable speed GSHP system can be 5-30% higher compared to the single speed system, due to the longer operating time of the pump in the ground heat exchanger (Madani et al, 2011).. 
N1  - E42. The larger use of the electrical auxiliary heater in on/off controlled systems results in higher total energy use, suggesting that a well-dimensioned single speed heat pump unit can make the SPF of the system very close to that of the variable speed system.. 
N1  - E43. System Efficiency. 
N1  - E44. To obtain a higher efficiency for on/off controlled systems, it is essential to dimension them so that the electrical auxiliary heater covers less than 5% of the annual heat demand (Madani et al, 2011).. 
N1  - E45. Retrofitting an inverter to the current single speed HP systems can increase the annual efficiency of existing on/off controlled systems, by eliminating the need for the electrical auxiliary heater and improving the system's Coefficient of Performance (COP) when the ambient temperature is lower than the balance point. 
