TI  - Scientific achievements and regulation of shallow geothermal systems in six European countries – A review
T2  - Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews
VL  - 68
PY  - 2016
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2016
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.rser.2016.02.014
AB  - A legislative framework is suggested on a European Union level that gives a unified definition of shallow geothermal systems, preferably on enthalpy basis, and lays down the principles of permitting process and criteria for sustainable GHP systems based on scientific results tolerating differences due to local geological and hydrogeological properties. A clear distinction of low-risk systems is desirable to aid the simplification of administrative procedure.
KW  - Borehole Heat Exchangers
KW  - GSHP
KW  - Ground source heat pump
KW  - ground heat exchanger
KW  - Groundwater heat pump
KW  - Minimum distance
KW  - Legislation
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. It has to be noted that not all articles that were found relevant are cited : the focus was placed on ground source heat pumps and groundwater heat pumps. 
N1  - C2. A major conclusion of reviewing both the scientific literature and the legislation of different countries is that the regulations do not take advantage of the researches or at least there is a lag in implementation. 
N1  - C3. There are some good examples found online such as the ThermoMap project for very shallow geothermal energy and the GSHP screening tool to assess viability of open-loop (4100 kW) systems for England and Wales. 
N1  - C4. The definition of shallow geothermal energy differs; Italy uses temperature categories, while other countries set the boundaries based on different depths. 
N1  - C5. In situ optimization method was developed for the water circulation pumps of a single stage heat pump unit and a system of two compressors working in tandem resulting in 28%  and 31%  of energy saving respectively. This was later improved to ensure user comfort along with 33% energy saving potential. 
N1  - C6. The authors suggest a legislative framework on a transnational level that sets the definition of shallow geothermal systems, the principles of permitting process and criteria for permissible GHP systems based on scientific results taking local geological and hydrogeological properties into consideration. This does not mean that absolute values dedicated to regions should be defined but the minimum distances and or maximum temperature differences should correlate with the characteristics of the subsurface in question. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. As of 2008 0.7% of GHG in Europe was saved and this could be raised up to 30% with improved performance of heat pumps and use of low carbon electricity [107]. 
N1  - D2. For a system of 25 BHEs the temperature change was 18% less by optimizing the load of the individual boreholes [120]. 
N1  - D3. Under the conditions typical for European Mediterranean area the GSHP was proven to use 37 718% primary energy of a conventional air to water heat pump system in cooling mode in Valencia [151] while in Almería 31% less electrical energy was used compared to the cooling tower system on site, also saving 116 m3 water throughout the summer period [152]. 
N1  - D4. In situ optimization method was developed for the water circulation pumps of a single stage heat pump unit and a system of two compressors working in tandem resulting in 28% [156] and 31% [157] of energy saving respectively. This was later improved to ensure user comfort along with 33% energy saving potential [158]. 
N1  - D5. Based on numerical modelling of two typical house types the combined impact of low performance for light load periods of thermostatically-controlled GSHPs and heating system over-sizing (by up to 40%) increases energy use by up to 18–22% [191]. 
N1  - D6. Concerning life cycle impacts a study found that heat pumps (SPF 3.9) are advantageous considering only global warming potential, depletion of fossil resources and formation of photochemical oxidants, natural gas boilers being the benchmark while other indicators were 68% higher on average [196], contrary to other researches. 
N1  - D7. Two versions of GSHP with on/off control and electrical auxiliary heating was compared to variable capacity system and the results showed that if the GSHP is designed to cover more than 65% of the peak heat demand of the building the SPF is around the same but below 55% the SPF is around 13% lower as the one of the variable speed system [260]. 
N1  - D8. The heat pumps can be certified, besides fulfilling other criteria, if the hydraulic layout of the ground loop system is such that the overall pumping power at the lowest operating temperature is less than 3% of the heating capacity. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The aim of this paper is to provide information on the research concerning shallow geothermal energy and its sustainable utilization in the selected countries of the European Union and compare the results to the regulation of geothermal heat pumps. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. A major conclusion of reviewing both the scientific literature and the legislation of different countries is that the regulations do not take advantage of the researches or at least there is a lag in implementation. 
N1  - E5. There are some good examples found online such as the ThermoMap project for very shallow geothermal energy and the GSHP screening tool to assess viability of open-loop (4100 kW) systems for England and Wales. 
N1  - E6. The definition of shallow geothermal energy differs; Italy uses temperature categories, while other countries set the boundaries based on different depths. 
N1  - E7. In situ optimization method was developed for the water circulation pumps of a single stage heat pump unit and a system of two compressors working in tandem resulting in 28%  and 31%  of energy saving respectively. This was later improved to ensure user comfort along with 33% energy saving potential. 
N1  - E8. The authors suggest a legislative framework on a transnational level that sets the definition of shallow geothermal systems, the principles of permitting process and criteria for permissible GHP systems based on scientific results taking local geological and hydrogeological properties into consideration. This does not mean that absolute values dedicated to regions should be defined but the minimum distances and or maximum temperature differences should correlate with the characteristics of the subsurface in question. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The aim of this paper is to provide information on the research concerning shallow geothermal energy and its sustainable utilization in the selected countries of the European Union and compare the results to the regulation of geothermal heat pumps. 
N1  - E11. Regulation. 
N1  - E12. A legislative framework is suggested on a European Union level that gives a unified definition of shallow geothermal systems, preferably on enthalpy basis, and lays down the principles of permitting process and criteria for sustainable GHP systems based on scientific results tolerating differences due to local geological and hydrogeological properties [54].. 
N1  - E13. The authors suggest a legislative framework on a transnational level that sets the definition of shallow geothermal systems, the principles of permitting process and criteria for permissible GHP systems based on scientific results taking local geological and hydrogeological properties into consideration.. 
N1  - E14. Although the European Union provides a legislative framework on the use of geothermal energy, the national regulations are diverse at the moment.. 
N1  - E15. The definition of shallow geothermal energy differs; Italy uses temperature categories, while other countries set the boundaries based on different depths [46].. 
N1  - E16. Research. 
N1  - E17. The majority of the relevant articles studied borehole heat exchanger optimization, thermal response test, filling material properties and the geometry of borehole fields [43].. 
N1  - E18. Exergy analysis showed that the feasibility of the GSHP as opposed to air-source heat pumps depends on thermal properties of the ground and should be evaluated on a yearly basis considering both heating and cooling seasons [46].. 
N1  - E19. A study in 2011 [47] proved the feasibility of GSHP system for heating and cooling a residential building.. 
N1  - E20. Data envelopment analysis [48] showed that GSHP systems are economically feasible despite their high investment costs.. 
N1  - E21. Implementation. 
N1  - E22. A major conclusion of reviewing both the scientific literature and the legislation of different countries is that the regulations do not take advantage of the researches or at least there is a lag in implementation [112].. 
N1  - E23. While the thumb rule is that one system should not disturb another exists in all examined countries either implicitly or explicitly, only Germany (for systems above 30 kW) require calculation or numerical simulation to prove the design [114].. 
N1  - E24. There are some good examples found online such as the ThermoMap project for very shallow geothermal energy and the GSHP screening tool to assess viability of open-loop (4100 kW) systems for England and Wales [193].. 
N1  - E25. Design. 
N1  - E26. Guidelines on the design of geothermal heat pumps should be thorough and systematic, incorporating up-to-date scientific research [no citation], and flexible enough for various applications.. 
N1  - E27. A European standard [no citation] could also be implemented.. 
N1  - E28. Applicability. 
N1  - E29. The design should be applicable for different climatic and hydrogeological situations [no citation] on the long term.. 
N1  - E30. Purpose. 
N1  - E31. The purpose of such guidelines is to propagate the spread of heat pumps while avoiding adverse effects and performance deterioration [no citation]. 
