AU  - Zarrella, Angelo 
AU  - Emmi, Giuseppe 
AU  - De Carli, Michele 
TI  - A simulation-based analysis of variable flow pumping in ground source heat pump systems with different types of borehole heat exchangers: A case study
T2  - Energy Conversion and Management
VL  - 131
PY  - 2016
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2016
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enconman.2016.10.061
AB  - A simulation model of ground source heat pump systems has been used to investigate to what extent a variable flow of the heat-carrier fluid of the ground loop affects the energy efficiency of the entire system. The model contemporaneously considers the borehole heat exchangers, the heat pump, the building load, and the control strategies for the circulation pumps of the ground loop. A constant speed of the circulation pumps of the ground loop was compared with a variable flow controlled by means of a constant temperature difference across the heat pump on the ground side considering the load profile of an office building located in North Italy. The analysis was carried out for a single U-tube, double U-tube and coaxial pipe heat exchangers. The control strategies adopted to manage the flow rate of the heat-carrier fluid of the ground loop affect both the heat exchange rate of the borehole field and the heat pump’s long-term energy efficiency. The simulations show considerable differences in the system’s seasonal energy efficiency. The constant speed of the circulation pumps leads to the best results as far as the heat pump’s energy performance was concerned, but this advantage was lost because of the greater amount of electrical energy used by the circulation pumps; this, of course, affects the energy efficiency of the entire system. The optimal solution appears then to be a constant temperature difference in the heat-carrier fluid across the heat pump.
KW  - Flow rate
KW  - borehole heat exchanger
KW  - Energy efficiency
KW  - ground loop
KW  - circulation pump
KW  - Reynolds number
KW  - Ground source heat pumps
KW  - GSHP
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Efforts to reduce CO2 emissions have become a pivotal environmental priority of this century. 
N1  - C2. Case A: this represents the reference solution which is without a variable flow; nominal mass flow rates were assumed constant throughout time and the temperature difference across the heat pump changed depending on the ground load. 
N1  - C3. The study focuses on the effects of the control strategy of the mass flow rate of the ground loop on the system’s long-term energy efficiency. 
N1  - C4. In the considered case study, the seasonal cooling energy performance of the heat pump decreased by about 9–14% with a constant temperature difference control across the heat pump equal to 5 °C in the cooling mode; whereas the degradation of the energy performance of the heat pump was less than 4% when the constant temperature difference control of 3 °C was applied. 
N1  - C5. Using small values of temperature difference control of the flow rate in the cooling operation mode as 3 °C, both the heating and cooling operations, uncovered significant variations in flow rate percentage distribution over the seasonal operations that led to an annual energy saving range of the electrical consumption of the heat pump and ground circulation pump between 5 and 7% with respect to the constant speed. 
N1  - C6. Ground properties did not significantly modify the flows of the heat-carrier fluid because the flow rates were determined by the control strategy, but the system’s energy efficiency was without doubt affected. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. A large difference was found between the SEERhp values of Cases A and B; at the tenth year, the SEERhp value of the Case B was approximately 9, 12 and 14% lower than that of the reference Case A for the single U-tube, double U-tube and coaxial pipes respectively. 
N1  - D2. On the other hand, the SEERhp value of the Case C was approximately 3–4% lower for each type of borehole heat exchanger than for the reference Case A. 
N1  - D3. For the double U-tube and coaxial pipes, the SEERsys of Case B was again about 7% lower than that in Cases A and C for Ground G1 and about 4% for Ground G2. 
N1  - D4. On the other hand, the contribution of the ground loop pump was less than 4% of the total energy demand of the system when the variable flow is considered in Cases B and C. 
N1  - D5. In the Scenario S2, the SEERsys values were approximately 13, 2 and 5% lower than SEERhp values in Cases A, B and C for each type of borehole heat exchanger and ground. 
N1  - D6. In the considered case study, the seasonal cooling energy performance of the heat pump decreased by about 9–14% with a constant temperature difference control across the heat pump equal to 5 °C in the cooling mode; whereas the degradation of the energy performance of the heat pump was less than 4% when the constant temperature difference control of 3 °C was applied. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. Ground properties did not significantly modify the flows of the heat-carrier fluid because the flow rates were determined by the control strategy, but the system’s energy efficiency was without doubt affected. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. Case A: this represents the reference solution which is without a variable flow; nominal mass flow rates were assumed constant throughout time and the temperature difference across the heat pump changed depending on the ground load. 
N1  - E5. The study focuses on the effects of the control strategy of the mass flow rate of the ground loop on the system’s long-term energy efficiency. 
N1  - E6. In the considered case study, the seasonal cooling energy performance of the heat pump decreased by about 9–14% with a constant temperature difference control across the heat pump equal to 5 °C in the cooling mode; whereas the degradation of the energy performance of the heat pump was less than 4% when the constant temperature difference control of 3 °C was applied. 
N1  - E7. Using small values of temperature difference control of the flow rate in the cooling operation mode as 3 °C, both the heating and cooling operations, uncovered significant variations in flow rate percentage distribution over the seasonal operations that led to an annual energy saving range of the electrical consumption of the heat pump and ground circulation pump between 5 and 7% with respect to the constant speed. 
N1  - E8. Ground properties did not significantly modify the flows of the heat-carrier fluid because the flow rates were determined by the control strategy, but the system’s energy efficiency was without doubt affected. 
N1  - E9. Energy Efficiency. 
N1  - E10. The study investigates the effect of variable flow of the heat-carrier fluid of the ground loop on the energy efficiency of a ground source heat pump system [10].. 
N1  - E11. The results show that the control strategy of the mass flow rate of the ground loop affects the system's long-term energy efficiency.. 
N1  - E12. A constant temperature difference control across the heat pump was found to be the optimal solution, as it leads to a better energy efficiency of the entire system.. 
N1  - E13. The study also found that the energy saving due to the variable flow was negligible in one scenario, whereas in another scenario it ranged between 5 and 7% with respect to the constant speed [36].. 
N1  - E14. The energy efficiency of a GSHP system is affected by the circulation pumps on the ground side [18].. 
N1  - E15. The power of a water or brine circulation pump depends on the cube of the mass flow rate.. 
N1  - E16. To reduce energy consumption, the pump can run on a variable flow instead of a constant speed one.. 
N1  - E17. Most annual operating hours occur during part load operations, and the mass flow rate of the heat-carrier fluid can be lower than the nominal value, resulting in energy savings.. 
N1  - E18. Control Strategies. 
N1  - E19. The study compares a constant speed of the circulation pumps of the ground loop with a variable flow controlled by means of a constant temperature difference across the heat pump [15].. 
N1  - E20. The results show that the constant speed leads to the best results as far as the heat pump's energy performance is concerned, but this advantage is lost due to the greater amount of electrical energy used by the circulation pumps.. 
N1  - E21. The study also investigates the effect of adjusting the mass flow rates of the heat-carrier fluids on both the building and ground loops at partial loads when the electrical power of the compressor is decreasing.. 
N1  - E22. A control strategy with a constant temperature difference across the heat pump on the ground side and its long-term effect was investigated using numerical simulations [10].. 
N1  - E23. The study focused on a control strategy based on a constant temperature difference across the heat pump with a consequent variable flow in the ground loop.. 
N1  - E24. The results showed that the fluid temperatures obtained with a single U-tube borehole heat exchanger were very close to those with a double U-tube.. 
N1  - E25. The double U-tube had a better thermal performance than coaxial pipes.. 
N1  - E26. System Performance. 
N1  - E27. The study analyzes the performance of the ground source heat pump system with different types of borehole heat exchangers, including single U-tube, double U-tube, and coaxial pipes [10].. 
N1  - E28. The results show that the pressure losses in the borehole heat exchangers affect the energy performance of the whole system.. 
N1  - E29. The study also found that the ground properties do not significantly modify the flows of the heat-carrier fluid, but the system's energy efficiency is affected by the control strategy.. 
N1  - E30. The simulation results led to the conclusion that the solution with a constant flow rate moves from the best choice to the worse one as the pressure losses increase.. 
N1  - E31. The energy efficiency of the whole system (i.e., the heat pump and the circulation pump of the ground loop) was evaluated for two types of ground in the Scenario S0 when only the pressure losses in the borehole heat exchangers were considered [27].. 
N1  - E32. The difference between Case A with a constant mass flow rate and other two cases (B and C) decreased.. 
N1  - E33. The annual energy consumption of the ground loop pump was about 8% of the total demand of the considered GSHP system in the Scenario S1 and about 14% in the Scenario S2, for each type of borehole heat exchanger [30].. 
N1  - E34. Energy Performance. 
N1  - E35. The energy performance of ground source heat pump systems is affected by the control strategy of the mass flow rate of the ground loop [36].. 
N1  - E36. The study found that the heat pump showed the best energy performance when a constant mass flow rate control of the ground loop was used, regardless of the type of borehole heat exchanger and ground.. 
N1  - E37. The seasonal cooling energy performance of the heat pump decreased by about 9-14% with a constant temperature difference control across the heat pump equal to 5 °C in the cooling mode.. 
N1  - E38. Control Strategy. 
N1  - E39. The control strategy of the ground loop affects the behaviour of the heat pump and the efficiency of the entire system on the long-term.. 
N1  - E40. Three different control strategies were investigated, including a constant mass flow rate, a constant temperature difference control across the heat pump, and a variable flow rate.. 
N1  - E41. The study found that using small values of temperature difference control of the flow rate in the cooling operation mode as 3 °C led to an annual energy saving range of the electrical consumption of the heat pump and ground circulation pump between 5 and 7% with respect to the constant speed.. 
N1  - E42. Ground Loop. 
N1  - E43. The ground loop affects the energy efficiency of the heat pump, with the convective thermal resistance inside the pipes of the double U-tube heat exchanger increasing to about 0.02, 0.04 and 0.07 (m K)/W in single U-tube, double U-tube and coaxial pipes respectively at 0.4Á Â mf c nom.. 
N1  - E44. The study found that the energy saving due to the variable flow was negligible in the Scenario S1, whereas in the Scenario S2 it ranged between 5 and 7% with respect to the constant speed.. 
N1  - E45. The total annual number of operating hours was 1543 in the heating and 1808 in the cooling modes, with the mass flow rate in the (0.4-0.5) Á mf h nom range for about 85% of the total hours in the heating mode. 
