TI  - In situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency of ground source heat pump systems
T2  - Energy and Buildings
VL  - 68
PY  - 2013
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2013
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enbuild.2013.09.030
AB  - This paper presents an experimental study of the influence of the water circulation pumps frequency at the indoor and outdoor loops in the overall system performance of a ground source heat pump monitored plant, and describes a three steps new methodology to optimize the system energy performance and obtain the values of the optimal frequencies when ON/OFF regulation is employed. The first step consists of several experimental tests of pseudo-random sequence of frequency steps for both, internal and external circulation pumps, carried out during a single day. The second step is the characterization of the quasi-steady state system performance as a function of circulation pumps frequencies. The final step consists of, by means of an analytical expression, the extrapolation of results to any partial load ratio, what allows finding the optimal frequency as a function of the instantaneous thermal load. The advantage of the proposed methodology is that it can be carried out on site and is able to consider the phenomena occurring at the heat pump and the ground source heat exchanger when the flow rates are varied. The methodology can be applied to any installation incorporating variable speed circulation pumps. Results indicate large energy savings potential for this kind of installation.
KW  - Ground source heat pump
KW  - energy performance
KW  - Energy efficiency
KW  - IDAE
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. According to data provided by IDAE [4], energy consumption in buildings in Spain accounts for 20% of final energy consumption, a figure that tends to increase. 
N1  - C2. The purpose of this paper is to examine the influence of the flow rate of the water circulation pumps on the overall performance of a ground source heat pump system for heating and cooling, as well as the optimization of their frequency in order to lead to the minimal energy consumption of the entire system. 
N1  - C3. Energy efficiency parameters are calculated from the power consumption readings and the heat transferred from the heat pump to the internal circuit, which is estimated from the values of TINIC, TOUTIC and the water flow rate m. 
N1  - C4. The first step consists in the experimental characterization of the system energy performance (COPSYSTEM) as a function of the circulation pump frequencies during one entire day for heating and one for cooling. 
N1  - C5. Further optimization is to be carried out in Geocool installation where appropriate control algorithms, including adequate control strategies to ensure users comfort will be implemented in order to optimize the daily energy performance of the system. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The results show that there is a high potential for energy savings which has been estimated as 28% of annual electricity consumption for the studied case. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The first step consists in the experimental characterization of the system energy performance as a function of the circulation pump frequencies during one entire day for heating and one for cooling. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. According to data provided by IDAE [4], energy consumption in buildings in Spain accounts for 20% of final energy consumption, a figure that tends to increase. 
N1  - E5. The purpose of this paper is to examine the influence of the flow rate of the water circulation pumps on the overall performance of a ground source heat pump system for heating and cooling, as well as the optimization of their frequency in order to lead to the minimal energy consumption of the entire system. 
N1  - E6. Energy efficiency parameters are calculated from the power consumption readings and the heat transferred from the heat pump to the internal circuit, which is estimated from the values of TINIC, TOUTIC and the water flow rate m. 
N1  - E7. The first step consists in the experimental characterization of the system energy performance (COPSYSTEM) as a function of the circulation pump frequencies during one entire day for heating and one for cooling. 
N1  - E8. Further optimization is to be carried out in Geocool installation where appropriate control algorithms, including adequate control strategies to ensure users comfort will be implemented in order to optimize the daily energy performance of the system. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The purpose of this paper is to examine the influence of the flow rate of the water circulation pumps on the overall performance of a ground source heat pump system for heating and cooling, as well as the optimization of their frequency in order to lead to the minimal energy consumption of the entire system. 
N1  - E11. Energy Efficiency. 
N1  - E12. The use of energy efficient and environmentally safe air conditioning systems is desirable, as building equipments are responsible for a significant percentage of all greenhouse gas emissions produced in developed countries [1,2,3].. 
N1  - E13. Ground source heat pump systems are recognized as one of the most efficient and comfortable heating and cooling systems available today [11].. 
N1  - E14. The optimization of water circulation pumps frequency in ground source heat pump systems can lead to large energy savings potential [5,6,7,8,9,10].. 
N1  - E15. The energy efficiency of the system is affected by the circulation pump frequencies.. 
N1  - E16. Increasing the water flow rate diminishes the compressor consumption but increases the circulation pumps consumption.. 
N1  - E17. Reducing the water flow diminishes the circulation pumps consumption but increases the heat pump consumption and reduces the heat transfer coefficient in the ground source heat exchanger.. 
N1  - E18. The system's energy consumption is calculated by numerically integrating the power consumption measured for each component.. 
N1  - E19. System Optimization. 
N1  - E20. A three-step methodology is proposed to optimize the system energy performance and obtain the values of the optimal frequencies when ON/OFF regulation is employed [16,17].. 
N1  - E21. The methodology consists of experimental tests, characterization of the quasi-steady state system performance, and extrapolation of results to any partial load ratio.. 
N1  - E22. The advantage of the proposed methodology is that it can be carried out on site and considers the phenomena occurring at the heat pump and the ground source heat exchanger when the flow rates are varied [18,19].. 
N1  - E23. Ground Source Heat Pumps. 
N1  - E24. Ground source heat pump systems are normally sized to cover the peak load, but often work at part load conditions during most of the time [20,21,22].. 
N1  - E25. The use of variable speed circulation pumps can help to minimize the auxiliary consumption and improve the system energy performance [23,24,25].. 
N1  - E26. The Geocool plant, a reversible water to water ground source heat pump installation, was used as a case study to examine the influence of the flow rate of the water circulation pumps on the overall performance of the system [26,27,28].. 
N1  - E27. The results showed that the higher the inverter frequency, the greater the circulating water flow rate, and the power consumption typically increases in a cubic manner with flow rate [29].. 
N1  - E28. Influence Of Flow Rates. 
N1  - E29. The influence of secondary fluid flow rates on condenser and evaporator performance is not linear and can be difficult to assess [23].. 
N1  - E30. Manufacturers do not provide this information as it requires precise laboratory work.. 
N1  - E31. A very low flow rate can lead to laminar flow and poor heat transfer to the ground.. 
N1  - E32. Experimental results show that at low frequencies (20 Hz), the ground source heat exchanger works in almost laminar conditions, resulting in a lower heat transfer coefficient and water temperature.. 
N1  - E33. System Performance. 
N1  - E34. The system's performance is characterized by a single ON/OFF compressor cycle, which represents the mean daily thermal demand.. 
N1  - E35. The energy performance factor is calculated as the ratio between the thermal load and the electric energy consumption during a time interval.. 
N1  - E36. The system coefficient of performance is calculated using expression (2) during the ON period for each cycle, corresponding to the performance of the unit and the system under quasi-steady state conditions [30].. 
N1  - E37. The system coefficient of performance, COPSYSTEM, includes all components required to provide hot or chilled water, such as heat pump compressor and parasitic losses, and both external and internal circulation pumps consumption [2].. 
N1  - E38. The internal circulation pump consumption needs to be added to the heat pump capacity during heating mode and extracted during cooling mode.. 
N1  - E39. The electrical efficiency of the pump is considered, and the extra heat supplied to the water is taken into account [3].. 
N1  - E40. The system's performance factor degrades with the partial load ratio, leading to lower optimal circulation pump frequencies at lower loads.. 
N1  - E41. The optimal frequencies for heating and cooling modes are different, with approximately 40 Hz for the internal pump and 30 Hz for the external one in heating mode, and approximately 30 Hz for the internal pump and 40 Hz for the external one in cooling mode.. 
N1  - E42. The performance factor of the system can be calculated using equations such as (9), which takes into account the partial load ratio and the quasi-steady state performance maps.. 
N1  - E43. Optimization Methodology. 
N1  - E44. A methodology for in situ optimization of the frequency of each water circulation pump in a geothermal system with ON/OFF regulation was developed and tested [23, 32, 33].. 
N1  - E45. The methodology consists of three steps: characterization of the system performance during the ON time of the compressor at different frequencies, estimation of the system COP maps, and estimation of the system performance maps for any thermal load.. 
N1  - E46. The tests are recommended to be carried out during four days a year, two during the heating season and two during the cooling season.. 
N1  - E47. Performance Maps. 
N1  - E48. The performance maps of the unit are constructed as a function of both the external and internal circulation pumps frequency [5, 6].. 
N1  - E49. The optimal pair of frequencies corresponds to low pump frequencies, with the maximum COPSYSTEM value corresponding to an internal frequency of around 40 Hz and an external frequency of around 30 Hz for heating mode [6].. 
N1  - E50. The optimal frequency for the internal circulation pump results in a higher value for heating mode operation, as it contributes to the heating by adding useful heat.. 
N1  - E51. Methodology. 
N1  - E52. The proposed methodology for optimizing the circulation pump frequencies of a ground source heat pump (GSHP) system consists of three steps [30].. 
N1  - E53. The first step involves experimental characterization of the system's energy performance as a function of circulation pump frequencies.. 
N1  - E54. The second step involves constructing quasi-steady state performance maps, and the third step allows extrapolating these maps to any load ratio.. 
N1  - E55. The methodology uses equations such as (5), (7), (8), and (9) to calculate the optimal frequencies.. 
N1  - E56. Energy Savings. 
N1  - E57. The proposed methodology has the potential to lead to significant energy savings, with an estimated 28% annual electricity consumption savings for the studied case.. 
N1  - E58. The optimal frequencies for different load ratios are presented in Table 1, and the potential energy savings for each month are shown in Fig. 9.. 
N1  - E59. The methodology can be used to optimize the daily energy performance of the system, and further optimization is to be carried out in Geocool installation where appropriate control algorithms will be implemented to ensure users' comfort [30]. 
