AU  - Madani, Hatef 
AU  - Claesson, Joachim 
AU  - Lundqvist, Per 
TI  - A descriptive and comparative analysis of three common control techniques for an on/off controlled Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) system
T2  - Energy and Buildings
VL  - 65
PY  - 2013
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2013
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enbuild.2013.05.006
AB  - In the present paper, three common methods in order to control an on/off controlled Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) system called “Constant hysteresis”, “Floating hysteresis”, and “Degree–Minute” methods are comprehensively described. Then, the generic model already developed by the authors is used in order to do the dynamic simulation of the systems with three different control methods over a year and making the comparison between them. The results from annual modeling of the systems show that the mean temperature of the heating water supplied to the building for the system controlled with degree–minute method is always close to the required temperature, regardless of the climatic boundary conditions over a typical year, whereas, the average supply temperature for the system with constant hysteresis method is mostly higher or lower than the required temperature, depending on the boundary condition. Regarding the annual energy use, the degree–minute and constant hysteresis methods have the lowest and highest annual energy use respectively. Switching from constant hysteresis to floating hysteresis method, the annual energy use will become lower and the mean temperature of the heating water supplied to the building will be closer to the required one.
KW  - Control
KW  - hysteresis
KW  - Heat pump
KW  - Ground Source Heat Pumps
KW  - Capacity
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Brine to water heat pump systems like Ground Source Heat Pumps (GSHPs) are one of the common and fast growing systems for heating the buildings in USA and European countries. 
N1  - C2. The present paper aims at describing and comparing three common methods in order to control an on/off controlled GSHP system. 
N1  - C3. The generic model presented by Madani et al [6,8] is used in order to simulate the performance of the heat pump system over a year when either method A, B or C is used as the control method. 
N1  - C4. When the ambient temperature is considerably higher than the balance point temperature, the on-time for each cycle of the system controlled with the constant hysteresis method is so short that the average supply temperature is lower than the required temperature. 
N1  - C5. When the ambient temperature is slightly higher than the balance point temperature, the system controlled with the hysteresis method operates almost continuously without any stop; so the average supply temperature for the hysteresis method is higher than the required supply temperature. 
N1  - C6. Concerning the temperature control of the heating water supplied to the building, the average supply temperature for the system controlled with degree–minute method is always close to the required supply temperature; whereas, the average supply temperature for the system with constant hysteresis method can be mostly higher or lower than the required temperature. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The annual energy use of compressor for the system controlled with degree–minute method is about 6% lower than the one with constant hysteresis method. 
N1  - D2. Degree–minute method has the lowest annual energy use of the electrical auxiliary heater (approximately 50% lower than constant hysteresis method) whereas floating hysteresis method has the highest use of auxiliary heater over a year. 
N1  - D3. The lowest total energy use over a year belongs to the degree–minute method (9% lower than the constant hysteresis method). 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The present paper aims at description and evaluation of three common control techniques in order to control an on/off controlled Ground Source Heat Pump system. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The present paper aims at describing and comparing three common methods in order to control an on/off controlled GSHP system. 
N1  - E5. The generic model presented by Madani et al [6,8] is used in order to simulate the performance of the heat pump system over a year when either method A, B or C is used as the control method. 
N1  - E6. When the ambient temperature is considerably higher than the balance point temperature, the on-time for each cycle of the system controlled with the constant hysteresis method is so short that the average supply temperature is lower than the required temperature. 
N1  - E7. When the ambient temperature is slightly higher than the balance point temperature, the system controlled with the hysteresis method operates almost continuously without any stop; so the average supply temperature for the hysteresis method is higher than the required supply temperature. 
N1  - E8. Concerning the temperature control of the heating water supplied to the building, the average supply temperature for the system controlled with degree–minute method is always close to the required supply temperature; whereas, the average supply temperature for the system with constant hysteresis method can be mostly higher or lower than the required temperature. 
N1  - E9. Control Techniques. 
N1  - E10. The paper describes three common control techniques for an on/off controlled Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) system: “Constant hysteresis”, “Floating hysteresis”, and “Degree–Minute” methods [1].. 
N1  - E11. The constant hysteresis method allows the actual return temperature to divert from the required return temperature within a certain extent, while the degree–minute method multiplies the difference between the actual supply temperature and the required supply temperature by time to calculate a parameter used to control the heat pump and electrical auxiliary heater.. 
N1  - E12. Energy Efficiency. 
N1  - E13. The results of the annual modeling of the systems show that the degree–minute method has the lowest annual energy use, while the constant hysteresis method has the highest [1].. 
N1  - E14. Switching from constant hysteresis to floating hysteresis method can lower the annual energy use and bring the mean temperature of the heating water supplied to the building closer to the required temperature.. 
N1  - E15. GSHP Systems. 
N1  - E16. GSHP systems are a common and fast-growing system for heating buildings, with a worldwide installed capacity of 33 GWt and an annual energy use of over 55 TWh [1].. 
N1  - E17. In Sweden, approximately 414,000 GSHPs have been installed, providing about 4.5 GW of thermal capacity [2].. 
N1  - E18. Improving the efficiency of these systems can save a considerable amount of energy and reduce greenhouse emissions [3].. 
N1  - E19. Control Methods. 
N1  - E20. The study compares three control methods for a ground source heat pump (GSHP) system: method A, method B (degree-minute method), and method C (floating hysteresis method) [6,8].. 
N1  - E21. Method B turns on the electrical auxiliary heater based on the sum of degree-minutes, while method C uses a variable hysteresis that changes based on the heat pump status.. 
N1  - E22. The degree-minute method can lead to a high temperature swing in the supply temperature, potentially deteriorating the comfort condition for the building inhabitants [17].. 
N1  - E23. The degree–minute method is shown to be a robust method to control the operation of the electrical auxiliary heater, providing a supply temperature close to the required temperature.. 
N1  - E24. The constant hysteresis method can result in a supply temperature that is either higher or lower than the required temperature, depending on the ambient temperature.. 
N1  - E25. System Modeling. 
N1  - E26. The study uses a generic model to simulate the GSHP system with the three control methods over a year [6,8].. 
N1  - E27. The model includes sub-models for the building, heat pump unit, borehole, and liquid pumps.. 
N1  - E28. The building is modeled using TRNBUILD, an interface in TRNSYS, and the heat pump unit is modeled as a black box model that processes input data such as approach heat sink temperature and approach heat source temperature [16].. 
N1  - E29. Performance Comparison. 
N1  - E30. The study compares the performance of the GSHP system with the three control methods, including the supply temperature, indoor temperature, energy consumption, and seasonal performance factor.. 
N1  - E31. The results show that the degree-minute method can lead to a high temperature swing in the supply temperature, while the floating hysteresis method can provide better control over the water temperature [9].. 
N1  - E32. The study also highlights the importance of considering the dynamic interaction between the heat pump unit, building, ground heat exchanger, and climate in evaluating the performance of the GSHP system [17].. 
N1  - E33. Energy Use. 
N1  - E34. The annual energy use of the system components is presented in Fig. 10, showing that the degree–minute method has the lowest total energy use, while the constant hysteresis method has the highest total energy use over a year [6,8].. 
N1  - E35. The degree–minute method has the lowest annual energy use of the compressor and electrical auxiliary heater, resulting in a higher Seasonal Performance Factor (SPF) compared to the constant and floating hysteresis methods.. 
N1  - E36. Temperature Control. 
N1  - E37. The study shows that the average supply temperature for the system controlled with the degree–minute method is always close to the required supply temperature, whereas the average supply temperature for the system with constant hysteresis method can be mostly higher or lower than the required temperature.. 
N1  - E38. The floating hysteresis method can result in a higher supply temperature than required when the ambient temperature is lower than the balance point temperature, due to the high hysteresis used in this method. 
