TY  - JOUR
AU  - Atam, Ercan 
AU  - Helsen, Lieve 
TI  - Ground-coupled heat pumps: Part 2—Literature review and research challenges in optimal design
T2  - Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews
VL  - 54
PY  - 2015
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2015
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.rser.2015.07.009
AB  - This paper is the second part of the paper on literature review and challenges in modelling and optimal control/optimal design of Ground-Coupled Heat Pump (GCHP) systems. The first part was “GroundCoupled Heat Pumps: Part 1—Literature Review and Research Challenges in Modelling and Optimal Control”. In this second part, a detailed literature review of optimal design of GCHP systems and the associated research challenges are given. Since optimal design may require a model, a very short summary of modelling approaches is also given. More details on modelling and on research challenges can be found in the first part and hence these details will not be repeated here. As in the first part, we adopt the adage “a picture is worth a thousand words” and include a minimal number of representative schematics and result figures for some of the reviewed studies for clarity and a better understanding of the presented material. In addition to the literature review, we included our comments, points of view, alternative solutions and some potential future directions. This review paper is useful both for engineers and researchers involved in optimal design of GCHP systems.
KW  - borehole heat exchanger
KW  - Optimal design
KW  - Reynolds number
KW  - Co-design
KW  - literature review
KW  - Artificial Neural Network
KW  - state space model
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Future worries on finite fossil energy resources and necessity of environmental friendliness have made renewable energy use popular and widespread in recent years. 
N1  - C2. Mathematical model based optimal design or co-design depends on an accurate mathematical model of the underlying borefield subsystem and other subcomponents of a Ground-Coupled Heat Pump (GCHP) or Hybrid Ground-Coupled Heat Pump (HyGCHP) system. 
N1  - C3. The bottleneck here is borefield modelling to predict the thermal behaviour of a general borefield system accounting for the thermal interaction between boreholes, the effect of groundwater flow, the effect of a dynamic ground surface boundary condition, multi-layer ground characteristics, combination of short-term and longterm effects, etc. 
N1  - C4. Thermal response factor models and statespace models can be used for borefield modelling. 
N1  - C5. Thermal response factor models can be used in approaches based on rules of thumb and state-space models can be used in a numerical optimization approach. 
N1  - C6. A nice and interesting exercise would be to compare different categories of optimal design, and if possible co-design, on a benchmark case to have an idea of the comparative performances of the different approaches presented. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The results are shown in Fig. 15(b) as a comparison to the normal grid with equal distributed BHE loads. They observed that the optimization based on load optimization leads to a relative improvement of 30% compared to the reference case. 
N1  - D2. Mass flow rate (kg/s) load ratio and to the configuration of the borefield. At the end, they claimed that the use of GLHEPro results in an over-sized borefield with a 35% higher total cost. 
N1  - D3. Compared to Yavuzturk and Spitler [30], they achieved an improvement of 17% in life cycle cost, 10% in annual energy usage but 7% increase in total investment cost. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. Thermal response factor models can be used in approaches based on rules of thumb and state-space models can be used in a numerical optimization approach. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. Mathematical model based optimal design or co-design depends on an accurate mathematical model of the underlying borefield subsystem and other subcomponents of a Ground-Coupled Heat Pump (GCHP) or Hybrid Ground-Coupled Heat Pump (HyGCHP) system. 
N1  - E5. The bottleneck here is borefield modelling to predict the thermal behaviour of a general borefield system accounting for the thermal interaction between boreholes, the effect of groundwater flow, the effect of a dynamic ground surface boundary condition, multi-layer ground characteristics, combination of short-term and longterm effects, etc. 
N1  - E6. Thermal response factor models and statespace models can be used for borefield modelling. 
N1  - E7. Thermal response factor models can be used in approaches based on rules of thumb and state-space models can be used in a numerical optimization approach. 
N1  - E8. A nice and interesting exercise would be to compare different categories of optimal design, and if possible co-design, on a benchmark case to have an idea of the comparative performances of the different approaches presented. 
N1  - E9. Modelling. 
N1  - E10. The paper discusses the challenges in modelling Ground-Coupled Heat Pump (GCHP) systems, particularly in borefield modelling, which is the most challenging part [1].. 
N1  - E11. Thermal response factor models and state-space models can be used for borefield modelling, with thermal response factor models being easily constructed for many borefield configurations and state-space models obtainable from heat transfer relations [2].. 
N1  - E12. Artificial Neural Network (ANN) based methods are not feasible for long-term prediction due to the requirement of long-term training data [3].. 
N1  - E13. Optimal Design. 
N1  - E14. The paper reviews various optimal design approaches for GCHP systems, including rule/chart-based optimal design, mathematical model-based optimal design, and co-design [4].. 
N1  - E15. The co-design approach is considered the best strategy for achieving optimal global system performance, but the underlying optimization problem formulation can be complex and difficult to solve [5].. 
N1  - E16. The paper also discusses the limitations of current optimal design approaches, including the fact that most reviewed literature considers only one borehole, making them inapplicable for complex borefield systems [6].. 
N1  - E17. Future Directions. 
N1  - E18. The paper identifies the need for intensified research on optimal design using mathematical models and numerical optimization schemes to obtain global optimal solutions [7].. 
N1  - E19. Future directions should focus on developing generic approaches that are general and applicable, and a comparative study of different optimal design approaches on a benchmark case would be a useful exercise [8].. 
N1  - E20. The development of co-design approaches that integrate optimal design and control is also expected to be an area of future research [9]. 
