AB  - An important consideration with regard to ground source heat pump (GSHP) systems is ensuring high energy efficiency performance throughout the years. Multi-year analyses are thus exceedingly important to evaluate the seasonal oscillations in the ground temperature and its long-term drift.As only a limited number of results based on real cases are available in the literature, this work aims to address this paucity of information. Seasonal and long-term computer simulations were conducted in order to investigate the thermal performance of two office buildings in Italy with unbalanced load profiles. The study focuses on the evaluation of ground temperature drift which was essentially due to an imbalance of the buildings’ load profile.
KW  - Borehole
KW  - Heat pump
KW  - ground source heat pump
KW  - coefficient of performance
KW  - borehole heat exchangers
KW  - ASHRAE
KW  - Ground heat exchanger
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Over recent decades, ground source heat pump (GSHP) systems, which are used worldwide for space heating and cooling of buildings, have been shown to be an attractive as well as promising technology [1]. 
N1  - C2. The main disadvantage of GSHP systems compared to conventional ones is higher initial costs which include those covering the geotechnical investigations and inspections on the ground and linked to the thermal-physical characteristics of groundwater as well as of drilling a borehole ground heat exchanger or wells to extract groundwater. 
N1  - C3. A thermal drift can be tolerated if the seasonal oscillations are slight, but they cannot be if they are significant. 
N1  - C4. Great differences were instead found in carried out in the second building (Case B) with regard to the thermal performance of the GSHP system owing to the totally different design loads found during the monitoring activity. 
N1  - C5. From a strictly energetic viewpoint, an improvement in plant performance follows when the ratio between the heating and cooling loads to the heat pumps is rebalanced by hybrid GSHP systems, i.e. by auxiliary equipment improving the surplus cooling/heating loads: chillers in Case A, auxiliary heaters in Case B. 
N1  - C6. The energetic-economic evaluation did not justify adopting supplementary borehole heat exchangers (BHEs); load balancing could be appropriate if the auxiliary equipment was already installed in the plant and if its cost was negligible and lowered operating expenses. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. At optimum conditions, the average coefficient of performance (COP) of the hybrid GSHP system increased by approximately 7.2% compared with that of the pure one. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The results showed that rebalancing the ratio between the heat extracted from and injected into the ground led to better results from an energetic viewpoint almost certainly synonymous with an energeticeconomic optimization. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The main disadvantage of GSHP systems compared to conventional ones is higher initial costs which include those covering the geotechnical investigations and inspections on the ground and linked to the thermal-physical characteristics of groundwater as well as of drilling a borehole ground heat exchanger or wells to extract groundwater. 
N1  - E5. A thermal drift can be tolerated if the seasonal oscillations are slight, but they cannot be if they are significant. 
N1  - E6. Great differences were instead found in carried out in the second building (Case B) with regard to the thermal performance of the GSHP system owing to the totally different design loads found during the monitoring activity. 
N1  - E7. From a strictly energetic viewpoint, an improvement in plant performance follows when the ratio between the heating and cooling loads to the heat pumps is rebalanced by hybrid GSHP systems, i.e. by auxiliary equipment improving the surplus cooling/heating loads: chillers in Case A, auxiliary heaters in Case B. 
N1  - E8. The energetic-economic evaluation did not justify adopting supplementary borehole heat exchangers (BHEs); load balancing could be appropriate if the auxiliary equipment was already installed in the plant and if its cost was negligible and lowered operating expenses. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. As only a limited number of results based on real cases are available in the literature, this work aims to address this paucity of information. 
N1  - E11. Energy Efficiency. 
N1  - E12. The energy efficiency of ground source heat pump (GSHP) systems depends on the temperature of thermal sources, i.e., ground and building characteristics [1, 2].. 
N1  - E13. Multi-year analyses are necessary to evaluate the long-term thermal behavior of GSHP systems and assess the effects of seasonal oscillations in ground temperature and its long-term drift.. 
N1  - E14. The study found that rebalancing the ratio between the heat extracted from and injected into the ground led to better results from an energetic viewpoint [1].. 
N1  - E15. GSHP Systems. 
N1  - E16. GSHP systems are commonly used in office buildings due to their greater energy efficiency [1].. 
N1  - E17. The main disadvantage of GSHP systems is the high initial cost, which includes geotechnical investigations, drilling a borehole ground heat exchanger, and thermal-physical characteristics of groundwater [1, 2].. 
N1  - E18. Hybrid GSHP systems can help reduce the high initial investment by decreasing the payback time and making the system more economically viable [25, 26, 27].. 
N1  - E19. Modeling And Simulation. 
N1  - E20. Several analytical and numerical models are available to analyze the thermal behavior of borehole heat exchangers, including the infinite [3, 4] and finite-line [5, 6] source models, and the cylindrical heat source model [3, 7].. 
N1  - E21. Simulation tools such as TRNSYS [9, 10, 11], EnergyPlus [14], and Earth Energy Designer (EED) [17] can be used to assess the thermal behavior of borehole heat exchangers.. 
N1  - E22. The study used a detailed numerical model to investigate the thermal performance of two office buildings in Italy with unbalanced load profiles [1].. 
N1  - E23. Introduction To GSHP Systems. 
N1  - E24. The lack of detailed, quantitative data on real experiences in the field of Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) systems hinders awareness of the potential of the computational approach [1].. 
N1  - E25. This work addresses this paucity of information by demonstrating how a coordinated use of assessment and best estimate models can point the way to retrofits of the layout or reconceived operation procedures that may improve a system’s long-term behaviour [2].. 
N1  - E26. Two real GSHP systems, both located in the Po Valley of Italy, were taken into consideration [3].. 
N1  - E27. Methodology. 
N1  - E28. The long-term behaviour of GSHP systems can be analyzed using numerical or analytical approaches [4].. 
N1  - E29. The CaRM simulation tool, based on electrical analogy, was used as the numerical approach to solve the unsteady state heat transfer problem [5].. 
N1  - E30. The CaRM tool has been highly improved since it was originally devised [33]; its latest revision [34] was used in the present work.. 
N1  - E31. The two GSHP systems were also analyzed utilizing the approach proposed by Kavanaugh and Rafferty [36,37], known in the literature as the ASHRAE method.. 
N1  - E32. Case Studies. 
N1  - E33. The first building is located in the city of Padova, and its GSHP system consists of a heat pump and 16 boreholes [6].. 
N1  - E34. The second building is located near Milan, and its GSHP system consists of three heat pumps running in parallel; each heat pump is characterized by nominal heating and cooling thermal powers of about 100 kW and 135 kW, respectively, with COP value of 4.1 and EER value of 5.5 [7].. 
N1  - E35. The characteristics of the ground heat exchangers are outlined in Table 1 [8].. 
N1  - E36. Thermal response tests were extensively carried out during the design phase on 10 out of the 51 ground heat exchangers [9].. 
N1  - E37. The undisturbed ground temperature was assumed to be 13.4 ◦C [10].. 
N1  - E38. The groundwater flow can be considered negligible [11].. 
N1  - E39. The heat loads on the ground side were measured and used as data for the CaRM simulations [12].. 
N1  - E40. Simulation Types. 
N1  - E41. The study involved three types of simulations: Type 1, where the borehole field was simulated as built and ground heat load measurements were considered [14, 41]; Type 2, where ground heat loads were modified to decrease the long-term effect of thermal drift, effectively considering a hybrid GSHP system [42]; and Type 3, where the total ground heat load measurements from Type 1 were used, but the number of boreholes was increased.. 
N1  - E42. Thermal Drift Analysis. 
N1  - E43. The CaRM simulations showed negligible amplitude oscillations and a slow but persistent drift to the average value in both case studies [14, 41].. 
N1  - E44. The annual minimum and maximum values of the heat pump entering fluid temperature (EFT) are outlined in Fig. 6a and b for Cases A and B, respectively.. 
N1  - E45. Reducing the heat imbalance of the ground load profile (Type 2) limited the temperature change in the heat carrier fluid inside the ground heat exchangers to about 1 ◦C over ten years in both case studies.. 
N1  - E46. Borefield Design Evaluation. 
N1  - E47. The ASHRAE procedure was used to assess the compliance of the borefield design with accepted good design practices [36, 37, 43].. 
N1  - E48. The results showed that the existing borehole field was consistent with good design practices for Case B, but only when the thermal loads to the heat pump group corresponded to the design values [43].. 
N1  - E49. The LENGTH computer application was also used to evaluate the thermal drift and penalty temperature, providing a more detailed evaluation of the thermal exchange with the borehole exchanger cluster [44, 45].. 
N1  - E50. Ground Temperature. 
N1  - E51. The ground temperature drift depends primarily on the annual heat imbalance between heating and cooling loads [45].. 
N1  - E52. If the heat rate injected into the ground is greater than that extracted from it, a temperature increase is to be expected over the years.. 
N1  - E53. Reducing the heat imbalance on the ground side is the key factor to limiting the effect of the thermal drift.. 
N1  - E54. The time-history of the temperatures computed is characterized by an oscillating seasonal behaviour around an average value which can slowly drift over long-term periods (years).. 
N1  - E55. System Performance. 
N1  - E56. The results of the ASHRAE method were comparable to those of CaRM when the same heat loads were used [45].. 
N1  - E57. From a strictly energetic viewpoint, an improvement in plant performance follows when the ratio between the heating and cooling loads to the heat pumps is rebalanced by hybrid GSHP systems.. 
N1  - E58. Adding supplementary borehole heat exchangers (BHEs) led to less remarkable improvements.. 
N1  - E59. Evaluating the costs for auxiliary equipment (heaters, chillers, possible solar panels, etc.) and for supplementary BHEs (if and where possible) also addresses the issues of improvement and optimization.. 
N1  - E60. Economic Evaluation. 
N1  - E61. The energetic-economic evaluation did not justify adopting supplementary BHEs; load balancing could be appropriate if the auxiliary equipment was already installed in the plant (as in Case B) and if its cost was negligible and lowered operating expenses [45].. 
N1  - E62. The LENGTH/CaRM package, an easy-to-use and accurate tool that can assess many aspects of GSHP systems, is available in English free upon request to the authors.. 
