AB  - In order to match the output capacity of a geothermal heat pump system (GHPS) with the actual load requirement, research has been conducted in searching for suitable adjusting methods for the GHPS and on the subsequent effects on the working parameters of the GHPS. Firstly, several methods are compared, and it is found that the method of adjusting the compressor’s rotation speed using a transducer is better than the others. Secondly, this method is experimented on a small scale GHPS at frequencies ranging from 30 to 55 Hz. The analysis of the experimental data reveals the relationships between the compressor frequency and other important parameters, such as coefficient of performance, heat capacity, cool capacity and compressor power input. The conclusions in the paper can serve as some guidance to the load adjustment of GHPS. Ó 2002 Elsevier Science Ltd.
KW  - coefficient of performance
KW  - rotation speed
KW  - Geothermal heat pump
KW  - Frequency conversion
KW  - Experimental study
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. The geothermal heat pump technology is one of energy conversion that uses geothermal discharge water as a low temperature heat source and heating network water as a high temperature heat sink. 
N1  - C2. Geothermal heat pump systems (GHPS) are able to lower the temperature of the geothermal discharge water and, raise the efficiency of geothermal energy and, at the same time, reduce thermal pollution on the earth surface. 
N1  - C3. At a water temperature of 30 °C, the coefficient of performance (COP) decreases with the frequency increasing, and the maximum COP occurs at 30 Hz. 
N1  - C4. When the water temperature is further increased to 45 °C, the COP fluctuates between 3 and 4 with the frequency changing, and the maximum COP is at 40 Hz. 
N1  - C5. Based on the theoretical and experimental analyses, we arrive at the following conclusions on applying a transducer to the GHPS: Using an AC transducer is a suitable way in the GHPS to adjust the rotation speed of a compressor. 
N1  - C6. Since the GHPS operates under different working conditions than conventional air conditioning or heat pump systems, the results from previous researches on the latter systems should be examined and tailored to the GHPS. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The partial load working condition is achieved by closing the suction valve. This method can continuously reduce the output by up to 50%. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. Based on the theoretical and experimental analyses, we arrive at the following conclusions on applying a transducer to the Geothermal heat pump systems: Using an AC transducer is a suitable way in the GHPS to adjust the rotation speed of a compressor. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. Geothermal heat pump systems (GHPS) are able to lower the temperature of the geothermal discharge water and, raise the efficiency of geothermal energy and, at the same time, reduce thermal pollution on the earth surface. 
N1  - E5. At a water temperature of 30 °C, the coefficient of performance (COP) decreases with the frequency increasing, and the maximum COP occurs at 30 Hz. 
N1  - E6. When the water temperature is further increased to 45 °C, the COP fluctuates between 3 and 4 with the frequency changing, and the maximum COP is at 40 Hz. 
N1  - E7. Based on the theoretical and experimental analyses, we arrive at the following conclusions on applying a transducer to the GHPS: Using an AC transducer is a suitable way in the GHPS to adjust the rotation speed of a compressor. 
N1  - E8. Since the GHPS operates under different working conditions than conventional air conditioning or heat pump systems, the results from previous researches on the latter systems should be examined and tailored to the GHPS. 
N1  - E9. Introduction. 
N1  - E10. The geothermal heat pump system (GHPS) is a technology that utilizes geothermal discharge water as a low-temperature heat source and heating network water as a high-temperature heat sink [1,2].. 
N1  - E11. GHPSs can lower the temperature of the geothermal discharge water, increasing the efficiency of geothermal energy and reducing thermal pollution [3].. 
N1  - E12. Load Adjustment Methods. 
N1  - E13. Several load adjustment methods for vapor compression GHPS have been explored, including on/off control, inlet throttling, concentration adjustment, and frequency control [4,5].. 
N1  - E14. The method of changing the compressor’s rotation speed with an AC transducer is preferred due to its ability to smoothly adjust to the load and improve working conditions [1,2].. 
N1  - E15. Experimental Results. 
N1  - E16. Experimental data were collected from 25 groups of experiments on the GHPS at different frequencies and water temperatures [1,2].. 
N1  - E17. COP. 
N1  - E18. The coefficient of performance (COP) increases with the condenser inlet water temperature [1].. 
N1  - E19. A higher frequency causes a larger working fluid flow rate and a higher condensation temperature, which affects the COP.. 
N1  - E20. The frequency at which the maximum COP occurs increases with the water temperature in tank (1), for example, at 30 or 35 °C the maximum COP is at 30 Hz, while at 50 °C it is at 50 Hz.. 
N1  - E21. Operating Conditions. 
N1  - E22. The GHPS operates under different working conditions than conventional air conditioning or heat pump systems [1].. 
N1  - E23. When the water temperature in water tank (1) is lower than 40 °C, a higher COP can be obtained by reducing the frequency.. 
N1  - E24. However, when the water temperature is at 40–45 °C, reducing the frequency cannot always increase the COP obviously, and when the water temperature is over 45 °C, it can worsen the cycle condition and result in a drastically lowered COP.. 
N1  - E25. Scalability. 
N1  - E26. The conclusions drawn from this study are based on a small scale GHPS using a specific refrigerant [1].. 
N1  - E27. Therefore, care should be taken when applying these conclusions to other GHPS with larger or smaller scales and different refrigerants, and the results should be verified by experiments and theories. 
