TI  - Generalised water flow rate control strategy for optimal part load operation of ground source heat pump systems
T2  - Applied Energy
VL  - 150
PY  - 2015
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2015
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.apenergy.2015.03.134
AB  - A control strategy was developed to predict optimal ground source heat pump water flow rates under part load operation. Using this strategy, optimal flow rates are calculated from available design data and minimal on-line measurements. The optimal control strategy was evaluated with validated single speed and tandem speed ground source heat pump simulation models in both heating and cooling mode. A range of building load profiles were implemented when analysing heat pump system performance. The optimal strategy was shown to result in an improvement in system performance when compared to nominal flow rate operation, particularly for low load conditions. Due to the prevalence of low load operation over a heating or cooling season, the seasonal system performance (SPF3) was shown to increase by between 20% and 40% when using the optimal strategy.
KW  - coefficient of performance
KW  - Optimal
KW  - COP3
KW  - Control
KW  - Ground source heat pumps
KW  - control strategy
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Frequencies predicted by the optimal control strategy are compared to the simulated optimal frequencies, identified using the full system simulation models, over a range of fixed part loads. 
N1  - C2. The developed control strategy predicts steady state optimal internal and external circuit water flow rate, which is used to estimate optimal part load flow rate depending on current load conditions. 
N1  - C3. In steady state, the optimal control strategy results in COP3 values close to the simulated maximum over a range of tested part loads. In both steady and quasi-steady state the optimal strategy produces a significant improvement in COP3 when compared to nominal pump frequency operation, for low part load conditions. 
N1  - C4. This translates to an increase in SPF3 of over 20% in heating and 40% in cooling when the optimal control strategy is implemented over nominal frequency operation, as the system experiences low part load conditions for the majority of a season. 
N1  - C5. This simulation study demonstrates the performance improvements possible using the optimal flow rate control strategy, which will be evaluated in future work through experimental trials. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. Due to the prevalence of low load operation over a heating or cooling season, the seasonal system performance (SPF3) was shown to increase by between 20% and 40% when using the optimal strategy. 
N1  - D2. The Carnot efficiency can be affected by up to 1% per K, giving kEF a value of À0.01 or less. 
N1  - D3. The reduction in COP3 from the simulated maximum is less than 1% when using the optimal strategy. 
N1  - D4. COP3 decreases significantly, by up to 60%, when using the nominal frequency for decreasing part loads. 
N1  - D5. flow control is implemented over the nominal pump frequency. This increases to 38% and 40% for the single and tandem speed heat pumps in cooling mode. 
N1  - D6. However, in steady state, the optimal control strategy results in COP3 values close to the simulated maximum over a range of tested part loads. In both steady and quasi-steady state the optimal strategy produces a significant improvement in COP3 when compared to nominal pump frequency operation, particularly for low part load conditions. This translates to an increase in SPF3 of over 20% in heating and 40% in cooling when the optimal control strategy is implemented over nominal frequency operation, as the system experiences low part load conditions for the majority of a season. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. Frequencies predicted by the optimal control strategy are compared to the simulated optimal frequencies, identified using the full system simulation models, over a range of fixed part loads. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. Frequencies predicted by the optimal control strategy are compared to the simulated optimal frequencies, identified using the full system simulation models, over a range of fixed part loads. 
N1  - E5. The developed control strategy predicts steady state optimal internal and external circuit water flow rate, which is used to estimate optimal part load flow rate depending on current load conditions. 
N1  - E6. In steady state, the optimal control strategy results in COP3 values close to the simulated maximum over a range of tested part loads. In both steady and quasi-steady state the optimal strategy produces a significant improvement in COP3 when compared to nominal pump frequency operation, for low part load conditions. 
N1  - E7. This translates to an increase in SPF3 of over 20% in heating and 40% in cooling when the optimal control strategy is implemented over nominal frequency operation, as the system experiences low part load conditions for the majority of a season. 
N1  - E8. This simulation study demonstrates the performance improvements possible using the optimal flow rate control strategy, which will be evaluated in future work through experimental trials. 
N1  - E9. Introduction To GSHPs. 
N1  - E10. The building sector accounts for 40% of primary energy use in the US and Europe [3], with space heating, cooling, and hot water production responsible for half of this value [4].. 
N1  - E11. Ground source heat pumps (GSHPs) are a renewable energy option for the building sector, widely implemented in northern European countries for space heating [5].. 
N1  - E12. GSHP systems predominantly operate with single speed compressors [6] and often under unsteady, part load conditions [9], which can significantly degrade system performance [10].. 
N1  - E13. Control Strategy Development. 
N1  - E14. A control strategy was developed to predict optimal GSHP water flow rates under part load operation [1,2].. 
N1  - E15. The strategy requires minimal on-line inputs and design data, and was evaluated against nominal flow using GSHP simulation models.. 
N1  - E16. The strategy incorporates a simplified analytical control model, which can be applied to a range of tandem and fixed speed heat pump systems with variable speed secondary circuit pump control.. 
N1  - E17. Seasonal Performance Evaluation. 
N1  - E18. The seasonal performance factor (SPF) of the GSHP system was estimated using daily simulation results from sample days [31,32].. 
N1  - E19. Low average daily part loads were observed for the majority of the heating and cooling seasons, with part loads below 0.4 observed for 86% of the heating season and 79% of the cooling season [33].. 
N1  - E20. The SPF3 of the GSHP system was calculated using Eqs. (3) and (4) for the heating and cooling seasons, and the total power and capacity for a given sample day were multiplied by the percentage of the season that day represents [33].. 
N1  - E21. The development of a control strategy for optimising internal and external water flow rate under part load conditions was described, and optimal flow rates were identified over a range of part loads using the simulation models [1,2].. 
N1  - E22. Optimization. 
N1  - E23. The optimal internal frequency decreases with part loads, while the optimal external frequency remains relatively constant [1].. 
N1  - E24. The observed optimal flow rates are different for the tandem and single speed heat pump systems in heating and cooling mode.. 
N1  - E25. An analytical equation can be derived for calculating COP3 when operating at part load [2].. 
N1  - E26. The single speed heat pump part load can be calculated by dividing the heat pump cycle ‘on’ time by the total cycle time, as shown in Eq (5) [3].. 
N1  - E27. Part Load Calculation. 
N1  - E28. For the tandem speed heat pump, the same is true when operating with one compressor (a &lt; 0.5), referred to as ‘mode 1’ operation [4].. 
N1  - E29. When a &gt; 0.5 the heat pump cycles between one and two compressors, referred to as ‘mode 2’ [5].. 
N1  - E30. The heat pump part load (a) can be expressed in terms of the compressor specific part load (a0), as shown in Eqs. (7) and (8) [6].. 
N1  - E31. Controller Strategy. 
N1  - E32. A method for calculating optimal steady state water flow rate has previously been outlined by Granryd [29].. 
N1  - E33. The optimal steady state flow rate is first predicted for both the internal and external circuits using Eqs. (19) and (20) [30].. 
N1  - E34. The coefficients C1, C2 and C3 are determined by implementing Eqs. (16)–(18) [31].. 
N1  - E35. The internal circulation pump coefficient (aI) is calculated using Eq (14) with design pump power and flow rate as inputs [32].. 
N1  - E36. Part load is then calculated, using Eq (5), from the recorded compressor operation signal over the previous on/off cycle [33].. 
N1  - E37. Control Strategy. 
N1  - E38. The developed control strategy predicts steady state optimal internal and external circuit water flow rate, which is used to estimate optimal part load flow rate depending on current load conditions [9].. 
N1  - E39. Optimal flow rate is calculated using available design data and minimal on-line measurements.. 
N1  - E40. The control operates under the assumption that building load will not change significantly from one on/off cycle to the next.. 
N1  - E41. Performance Evaluation. 
N1  - E42. The optimal control strategy was evaluated using the single speed and tandem speed heat pump system models.. 
N1  - E43. Frequencies predicted by the optimal control strategy are compared to the simulated optimal frequencies, identified using the full system simulation models, over a range of fixed part loads.. 
N1  - E44. The optimal control strategy results in COP3 values close to the simulated maximum over a range of tested part loads.. 
N1  - E45. In both steady and quasi-steady state the optimal strategy produces a significant improvement in COP3 when compared to nominal pump frequency operation, particularly for low part load conditions.. 
N1  - E46. Seasonal Performance. 
N1  - E47. SPF3 was calculated for a heating and cooling season.. 
N1  - E48. An improvement in SPF3 of 23% and 20% is predicted, for the single and tandem speed heat pump systems, when optimal control is used in heating mode [9].. 
N1  - E49. In cooling mode, the improvement in SPF3 is 38% and 40% for the single and tandem speed heat pumps.. 
N1  - E50. The improvement in SPF3 is primarily due to high daily COP3 for low load days, which are more prevalent than high load operation over the course of a season. 
