TI  - An optimization strategy for the control of small capacity heat pump integrated air-conditioning system
T2  - Energy Conversion and Management
VL  - 119
PY  - 2016
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2016
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enconman.2016.04.027
AB  - This paper studies the optimization of a small-scale central air-conditioning system, in which the cooling is provided by a ground source heat pump (GSHP) equipped with an on/off capacity control. The optimization strategy aims to optimize the overall system energy consumption and simultaneously guarantee the robustness of the space air temperature control without violating the allowed GSHP maximum start-ups number per hour specified by customers. The set-point of the chilled water return temperature and the width of the water temperature control band are used as the decision variables for the optimization. The performance of the proposed strategy was tested on a simulation platform. Results show that the optimization strategy can save the energy consumption by 9.59% in a typical spring day and 2.97% in a typical summer day. Meanwhile it is able to enhance the space air temperature control robustness when compared with a basic control strategy without optimization.
KW  - Ground source heat pump
KW  - Optimization
KW  - control strategy
KW  - Air-conditioning system
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. This paper studies the optimization of a small-scale A/C system integrated with a ground source heat pump (GSHP). 
N1  - C2. This paper presents an optimization strategy for a GSHPintegrated A/C system in order to optimize the overall system energy consumption without violating the allowed GSHP maximum start-ups number and degrading the space air temperature control performance. 
N1  - C3. The performance of the optimization strategy is compared with the basic control strategy. 
N1  - C4. The results show that the optimization strategy can improve the robustness of the space air temperature control significantly and reduce the system energy consumption obviously in spring days, i.e. under light load conditions. 
N1  - C5. The energy consumption of the GSHP was reduced by 21.15% and the overall system was reduced by 9.59%. 
N1  - C6. Both the optimization strategy and the base control strategy can achieve a robust control of the space air temperature, while the energy consumption of the overall system is slightly smaller when the optimization strategy is used. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. It has been shown that more than 40% of the building energy consumptions are mainly used for the heating, ventilation, and air-conditioning (HVAC) systems [1,2]. 
N1  - D2. The energy consumption of the GSHP was reduced by 21.15% and the overall system was reduced by 9.59%. 
N1  - D3. In the summer day, both the optimization strategy and the base control strategy can achieve a robust control of the space air temperature, while the energy consumption of the overall system is slightly smaller (reduced by 2.97%) when the optimization strategy is used. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The results show that the optimization strategy can improve the robustness of the space air temperature control significantly and reduce the system energy consumption obviously in spring days, i.e. under light load conditions. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. This paper presents an optimization strategy for a GSHPintegrated A/C system in order to optimize the overall system energy consumption without violating the allowed GSHP maximum start-ups number and degrading the space air temperature control performance. 
N1  - E5. The performance of the optimization strategy is compared with the basic control strategy. 
N1  - E6. The results show that the optimization strategy can improve the robustness of the space air temperature control significantly and reduce the system energy consumption obviously in spring days, i.e. under light load conditions. 
N1  - E7. The energy consumption of the GSHP was reduced by 21.15% and the overall system was reduced by 9.59%. 
N1  - E8. Both the optimization strategy and the base control strategy can achieve a robust control of the space air temperature, while the energy consumption of the overall system is slightly smaller when the optimization strategy is used. 
N1  - E9. Optimization. 
N1  - E10. The optimization strategy aims to minimize the overall system energy consumption of a small-scale central air-conditioning system integrated with a ground source heat pump (GSHP) while guaranteeing the robustness of the space air temperature control [1,2].. 
N1  - E11. The set-point of the chilled water return temperature and the width of the water temperature control band are used as the decision variables for the optimization [22].. 
N1  - E12. The performance of the proposed strategy was tested on a simulation platform, showing that it can save energy consumption by 9.59% in a typical spring day and 2.97% in a typical summer day [3,4].. 
N1  - E13. Control. 
N1  - E14. The on/off control of the GSHP is based on the chilled water return temperature, with the compressor switching on when the temperature is higher than a pre-defined upper threshold and switching off when it is lower than a pre-defined lower threshold [17].. 
N1  - E15. The width of the CHWR temperature control band and the set-point of the CHWR temperature affect the number of GSHP start-ups per hour and the overall system energy consumption [22].. 
N1  - E16. A larger set-point of the CHWR temperature can reduce the on time operation of the heat pump, but may increase the mean supply air temperature and the energy consumption of the fan [17,22].. 
N1  - E17. Energy Efficiency. 
N1  - E18. The energy efficiency of the GSHP-integrated A/C system is affected by the water volume inside the chilled water loop, with a smaller water volume resulting in a higher on/off frequency of the compressor [28].. 
N1  - E19. The specification of the allowed maximum start-ups number of compressor per hour poses a constraint on the water volume inside the chilled water loop, and a water tank may be needed to increase the water volume to guarantee the allowed maximum start-ups number [28].. 
N1  - E20. The optimization strategy takes into account the allowed maximum start-ups number per hour specified by customers and the system operating load condition to minimize the overall system energy consumption [5,6].. 
N1  - E21. Optimization Scheme. 
N1  - E22. The proposed optimization scheme aims to improve the energy performance of a GSHP-integrated A/C system by optimizing Tw,set and DTw [22].. 
N1  - E23. The scheme involves checking the water volume inside the chilled water loop to ensure it can satisfy the allowed GSHP maximum start-ups number per hour.. 
N1  - E24. If the actual water volume is smaller than the required minimum water volume, a water tank is used to increase the water volume.. 
N1  - E25. The optimization is formulated as an objective function to minimize the overall energy consumption of the system, subject to constraints such as the GSHP maximum start-ups per hour and the allowed variation of the chilled water return temperature.. 
N1  - E26. Objective Function. 
N1  - E27. The objective function is defined as the average power consumption of the overall system in one GSHP on/off cycle, which is calculated using an empirical formula (Eq. 17) [29].. 
N1  - E28. The formula takes into account the CHWR temperature set-point, the space air temperature set-point, and the PLR.. 
N1  - E29. The coefficients of the empirical formula are identified using operation data.. 
N1  - E30. Optimization Algorithm. 
N1  - E31. The optimization algorithm involves offline initialization and online operation.. 
N1  - E32. The online operation includes measuring the real-time total cooling load and sensible heat load, calculating the mean total cooling load and the mean sensible heat load, and applying the optimal values of Tw;set and DTw to the on/off controller.. 
N1  - E33. The algorithm uses equations such as Eq. (28) to calculate the real-time total cooling load and Eq. (29) to calculate the real-time sensible heat load.. 
N1  - E34. The optimization strategy is tested using a simulated virtual system representing an actual GSHP-integrated central A/C system [22].. 
N1  - E35. The optimization algorithm was assessed based on room temperature control performance, GSHP on/off frequency, and total energy consumption [37].. 
N1  - E36. The algorithm was compared to a basic control strategy, and the results showed that the optimization strategy achieved good control robustness, with an average absolute tracking error of 0.030 °C.. 
N1  - E37. The optimization strategy adjusted Tw,set and DTw according to the cooling load to minimize overall energy consumption, and the variation of Tw,set and DTw were shown in Fig. 12(a) [30].. 
N1  - E38. System Description. 
N1  - E39. The building automation system (BAS) measures and records chilled/cooling water supply/return temperature and air supply/return temperature at specified time intervals [30].. 
N1  - E40. The simulation platform uses TRNSYS and includes models for the building, GSHP, AHU, water pump, water tank, GSHE, cooling coil control valve, temperature and humidity sensors, and actuators.. 
N1  - E41. The optimization algorithm is coded in MATLAB and embedded in TRNSYS using component Type 155.. 
N1  - E42. Two local controllers, an on/off controller and a bilinear feedback controller, control the heat pump and room temperature, respectively.. 
N1  - E43. Model Validation. 
N1  - E44. The heat pump model and GSHE model were validated using operation data collected from a test rig [23, 31, 32].. 
N1  - E45. The parameters of the heat pump model, including a, Wlo, UAev, and UAcd, were identified and listed in Table 3.. 
N1  - E46. The thermal capacitances of the water volume in the evaporator and condenser were calculated and listed in Table 4.. 
N1  - E47. The GSHE model was validated using operating data from the test rig, and the overall heat transfer coefficient UAGS was identified as 2670 W/K [33].. 
N1  - E48. The models were validated using data from different summer days, and the results showed that the models could predict the outlet water temperature and power consumption accurately [34, 35, 36].. 
N1  - E49. Optimization Strategy. 
N1  - E50. The optimization strategy for a GSHP-integrated A/C system aims to minimize the total power of the system while satisfying the allowed GSHP maximum start-ups number and maintaining space air temperature control performance [1].. 
N1  - E51. The strategy involves two steps: checking the water volume inside the chilled water loop and optimizing system settings (DTw and Tw,set) using an optimization algorithm [2].. 
N1  - E52. The results show that the optimization strategy can improve the robustness of space air temperature control and reduce system energy consumption, especially under light load conditions [3].. 
N1  - E53. Energy Performance. 
N1  - E54. The energy performance of the optimization strategy is compared with the basic control strategy, showing that the optimization strategy achieves better energy performance in both summer and spring days [4].. 
N1  - E55. The daily average energy consumption of the system is reduced by 2.97% in summer days and 9.59% in spring days [5].. 
N1  - E56. Implementation And Validation. 
N1  - E57. The proposed strategy can be easily implemented in an existing GSHP-integrated A/C system by calculating the required water volume and using a mean power model of GSHP, an AHU model, and a mean cooling capacity model [7].. 
N1  - E58. The models are validated using data collected from a simulation platform, showing that they can predict the overall energy consumption and cooling capacity accurately [8].. 
N1  - E59. The validation results show that the largest absolute prediction error is 2.63% for the mean power consumption model, 1.01% for the AHU model, and 0.18% for the mean cooling capacity model [9]. 
