TI  - Energy efficiency in a ground source heat pump with variable speed drives
T2  - Energy and Buildings
VL  - 91
PY  - 2015
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2015
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enbuild.2014.12.048
AB  - A variable capacity heat pump, with variable speed drives on the secondary circuits, needs an adequate control for achieving the maximum energy efficiency. In the present paper, experimental data and a modeling procedure of a ground source heat pump (GSHP) with variable speed compressor, variable speed water pumps and variable speed fans in the coils are reported. Both data and model are used to evaluate the operating conditions that lead to the maximum seasonal coefficient of performance of the system. The control parameters that can be varied are the followings: frequency of compressor, frequency of water pump to the borehole heat exchangers, frequency of the water pump to the user, velocity of the fans, water temperature to the user. From the experimental data, it was possible to define a control variable which is the ratio of compressor electric power consumption to the power absorbed by the water pumps. The model is also the baseline to develop a control strategy that is implemented in the present GSHP system, developed in the framework of the European Project Ground-Med, with the final objective of maximizing the seasonal coefficient of performance of the system.
KW  - Model
KW  - Control
KW  - COP3
KW  - Ground source heat pump
KW  - water pump
KW  - brushless DC
KW  - Experimental data
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. It was possible to define a control variable which is the ratio of compressor electric power consumption to the power absorbed by the water pumps. 
N1  - C2. The parameters that can be varied for the heat pump system control are: electric frequency of compressor, frequency of water pump in the external circuit, frequency of the water pump in the internal circuit, velocity of the fans and supply water temperature to the fan coils. 
N1  - C3. Experimental COP curves at constant load ratio are plotted as a function of the water temperature difference in the borehole heat exchangers and in the building loop. 
N1  - C4. (2) At higher load ratio the maximum COP3 is obtained for a water temperature difference at the condenser of about 5 K and at the evaporator of 2.8 K. 
N1  - C5. When decreasing the load ratio, the maximum COP3 value is obtained at the minimum values of water flow rate to the borehole heat exchangers and to the building because at partial loads the heat exchangers are somehow oversized. 
N1  - C6. The authors pointed out that in the variable speed heat pumps the energy consumptions of the water pumps were 5–30% higher than the on/off controlled heat pumps, due to longer working time of pumps over a year. 
N1  - C7. The main components of the system are modeled with ad-hoc lookup tables (LUTs) which have been calibrated using the experimental data taken from the heat pump installed at the demo site. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. Considering the seasonal coefficient of performance, this study indicates that if the on/off controlled systems are designed to cover more than 65% of the building peak demand, there is no considerable difference with the variable capacity system. 
N1  - D2. If the on/off controlled heat pump is oversized, covering more than 94% of the building peak heat demand, the annual energy consumption is the lowest but economic constraints prevent the system to be the best option; if the on/off controlled heat pump is dimensioned to cover only the 55% of the peak demand, it gives the highest annual energy consumption due to the large use of electrical auxiliary heaters. 
N1  - D3. The authors pointed out that in the variable speed heat pumps the energy consumptions of the water pumps were 5–30% higher than the on/off controlled heat pumps, due to longer working time of pumps over a year. 
N1  - D4. Comparing the calculated SCOP3, at the same operating conditions, an increase of about 30% could be achieved using a BLDC motor compressor instead of the inverter-driven asynchronous motor compressor. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The experimental in-situ performance of a ground source heat pump with variable speed drives, installed in the framework of the European Ground-Med project, is presented. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. Experimental COP curves at constant load ratio are plotted as a function of the water temperature difference in the borehole heat exchangers and in the building loop. 
N1  - E5. (2) At higher load ratio the maximum COP3 is obtained for a water temperature difference at the condenser of about 5 K and at the evaporator of 2.8 K. 
N1  - E6. When decreasing the load ratio, the maximum COP3 value is obtained at the minimum values of water flow rate to the borehole heat exchangers and to the building because at partial loads the heat exchangers are somehow oversized. 
N1  - E7. The authors pointed out that in the variable speed heat pumps the energy consumptions of the water pumps were 5–30% higher than the on/off controlled heat pumps, due to longer working time of pumps over a year. 
N1  - E8. The main components of the system are modeled with ad-hoc lookup tables (LUTs) which have been calibrated using the experimental data taken from the heat pump installed at the demo site. 
N1  - E9. Introduction To GSHP. 
N1  - E10. The paper discusses the energy efficiency of a ground source heat pump (GSHP) with variable speed drives, which requires adequate control to achieve maximum energy efficiency [1].. 
N1  - E11. Experimental data and a modeling procedure are reported to evaluate the operating conditions that lead to the maximum seasonal coefficient of performance of the system.. 
N1  - E12. The control parameters that can be varied include frequency of compressor, frequency of water pump, velocity of fans, and water temperature to the user.. 
N1  - E13. Control And Efficiency. 
N1  - E14. The control strategy is crucial in achieving the maximum seasonal coefficient of performance of the GSHP system [2].. 
N1  - E15. The paper compares the energy performance of variable-capacity geothermal heat pumps against the performance of on/off controlled equipment, considering aspects such as temperature level of heated water, performance of heat exchangers at partial loads, and compressor global efficiency [3].. 
N1  - E16. The use of a variable speed compressor can reduce the need for supplementary heating [4] and increase the energy efficiency of the system.. 
N1  - E17. Modeling And Comparison. 
N1  - E18. Several models of heat pump systems have been developed in literature, including a system model of an air-to-water reversible heat pump [6] and a model for capacity control in ground source heat pump systems [9].. 
N1  - E19. The paper presents a comparison between an inverter-driven scroll compressor and a brushless DC motor compressor in terms of coefficient of performance [10].. 
N1  - E20. The experimental data are compared with a model of the GSHP system with variable speed drives, which takes into account the compressor efficiency, performance of heat exchangers at partial loads, and influence of supply water temperature [11].. 
N1  - E21. System Components. 
N1  - E22. The heat pump system consists of an inverter-driven compressor with a scroll orbiting compressor, centrifugal pumps with brushless DC motors, and fan coils with brushless DC motors [1].. 
N1  - E23. The compressor is driven by an asynchronous electric motor controlled by a converter, and the expansion valve is an electronically commanded electric valve [2].. 
N1  - E24. The system also includes hydronic pumps driven by single-phase synchronous permanent magnet electric motors with inverter control [3].. 
N1  - E25. Performance Analysis. 
N1  - E26. The performance of the system was analyzed through a measurement campaign during the heating season, where data were collected for different water flow rates and supply water temperatures [4].. 
N1  - E27. The results show that the evaporation temperature increases when the heat flow rate at the evaporator is decreased and when the water mass flow rate increases [5].. 
N1  - E28. The condensation temperature also increases when the load ratio and the water mass flow rate are increased [6].. 
N1  - E29. The coefficient of performance (COP) of the system was calculated, and the results show that the maximum COP3 is obtained for a water temperature difference at the borehole heat exchanger in the range 2.4-3.2 K [7].. 
N1  - E30. Modeling And Control. 
N1  - E31. A numerical model of the heat pump system was implemented using lookup tables (LUTs) to model the evaporator, geothermal field, compressor, condenser, and fan coils [8].. 
N1  - E32. The LUTs were calibrated with experimental data, and the model was used to create iso-COP curves [9].. 
N1  - E33. The results show that the regulation of auxiliary water pumps is important to achieve the maximum COP3, and a control variable called the "parameter" was defined to optimize the system performance [10].. 
N1  - E34. The model can be used to optimize the system performance and control the water pumps and fan coils to achieve the maximum COP [11].. 
N1  - E35. Model Development. 
N1  - E36. The model of the heat pump system has been developed using ad-hoc lookup tables (LUTs) calibrated with experimental data [1,2,3,4,5,6].. 
N1  - E37. The model allows to predict the system behavior, with compressor power consumption values predicted in the range ±20% and COP3 values in the range ±10% [4].. 
N1  - E38. Any component of the heat pump can be easily replaced by modifying the corresponding lookup table.. 
N1  - E39. Control Strategy. 
N1  - E40. A control strategy with double regulation and a proportional integral action has been proposed to maximize the seasonal system performance [6].. 
N1  - E41. The primary regulation varies the frequency of the compressor as a function of the difference between the setpoint value of the supply water temperature and the water temperature in the tank.. 
N1  - E42. The secondary regulation varies the water flow rate in the internal and external circuits and the fan coils speed.. 
N1  - E43. Performance Optimization. 
N1  - E44. The adoption of a brushless DC motor compressor instead of an inverter-driven asynchronous motor compressor can increase the values of SCOP3 by 30% due to the higher efficiency of the brushless compressor at partial loads [5].. 
N1  - E45. The optimal range for the water mass flow rate has been found, and a parameter defined as the ratio of compressor electric power consumption to the power absorbed by the water pumps has been introduced, displaying a constant value of about 8.5 in the optimal working conditions [3]. 
