N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. In a “base case” the fluid velocity is chosen to a certain value and for a given application the operating temperatures depends on the performance of the evaporator and condenser. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. In a “base case” the fluid velocity is chosen to a certain value and for a given application the operating temperatures depends on the performance of the evaporator and condenser. 
N1  - E5. Using Eq 12 this reasoning gives for the evaporator side the following simple expression of fan power corresponding to maximum net capacity in a refrigeration system: Ep2 Qmax 1⁄4 nT $kq$q2Qmax nP þ 1 (23). 
N1  - E6. It is desirable to have a design with a small value for the “pump power ratio”. 
N1  - E7. Considering a base case of a refrigerating system where the design data for the evaporator and condenser are known, simple analytical relations are deduced for optimum flow rates that will result in highest overall COP of the system when energy demand for the compressor as well as fans are included. 
N1  - E8. Objectives. 
N1  - E9. The purpose of the paper is to derive useful and simple tools to find optimum flow conditions in the heat exchangers of refrigeration systems. 
N1  - E10. Introduction. 
N1  - E11. The paper discusses the optimization of flow rates in evaporators and condensers of heat pumping systems to achieve the highest overall Coefficient of Performance (COP) [1].. 
N1  - E12. The flow velocities on the air or liquid side of these components significantly affect the system performance, and the purpose of the paper is to derive analytical relations for optimum flow rates.. 
N1  - E13. Optimization. 
N1  - E14. The optimization of flow rates is mainly of a technical character, and the solutions are not influenced by investment strategies, interest rates, or energy prices [2].. 
N1  - E15. The paper assumes a given system design and derives simple analytical relations for optimum flow rates that result in the highest overall COP of the system when energy demand for the compressor as well as pumps or fans are included.. 
N1  - E16. The overall energy to operate the system is comprised of the energy used for the compressor and for the fans (or pumps), expressed as Etot = EC + Ep2 + Ep1 = Q2/COP2 + Ep2 + Ep1 [3].. 
N1  - E17. The Coefficient of Performance, COP2, is influenced by the evaporating and the condensing temperatures, t2 and t1.. 
N1  - E18. The overall total COP2tot, defined as Q2N/Etot, is of interest in refrigerating applications.. 
N1  - E19. To achieve a low "pump power ratio", a design should aim for a high analogy number A, high efficiency fan or pump and motor, minimal flow restrictions, and a high overall heat transfer coefficient U [3].. 
N1  - E20. The choice of velocity w and temperature difference qM is crucial in optimizing the design, with the goal of balancing first cost and operating cost.. 
N1  - E21. Applications. 
N1  - E22. The expressions derived in the paper can be used for design purposes as well as for checking suitable flow velocities in existing plants [1].. 
N1  - E23. The relations may also be incorporated in algorithms for optimal operation of systems with variable speed compressors.. 
N1  - E24. The paper provides examples of experimental results and simulations of a refrigerating system equipped with fan coils, demonstrating the influence of air velocity on the net refrigerating capacity and the overall COP of the system [2].. 
N1  - E25. Evaporator. 
N1  - E26. Considering the condenser side given, the derivative of 1/COP2tot with respect to air velocity can be expressed, and by setting this derivative to zero, an optimum value for the air velocity can be found, corresponding to an extreme value for the COP2tot [3].. 
N1  - E27. The result is w2COPmax ~ w20 * (nT * (T10 - T20 * (1 - hCt)) / (nT + np + 1))^(1/(nT+np+1)).. 
N1  - E28. Condenser. 
N1  - E29. Similarly, focusing on the condenser and considering the evaporator side given, the condenser temperature will only marginally influence the evaporator temperature, but it does influence the Carnot efficiency (hCt) [3].. 
N1  - E30. A parameter kE is introduced to account for the variation of the Carnot efficiency with the condenser temperature.. 
N1  - E31. The optimum velocity on the condenser side for maximum COP2tot can be derived, and the result is w1COPmax ~ w10 * (nT * (T10 - T20 * (1 - hCt)) / (nT + np + 1))^(1/(nT+np+1)).. 
N1  - E32. Design. 
N1  - E33. The "pump power ratio" (EpIs/Q) is influenced by w2=qM, the analogy number A, and fluid properties Pr and cp [1].. 
N1  - E34. The analogy number A is often less than 1 for compact heat exchangers, with values ranging from 0.4 to 0.15 or lower, and decreases slightly with Reynolds number [2].. 
N1  - E35. The design of the heat exchanger, including tube arrangements and flow restrictions, affects the analogy number A.. 
N1  - E36. Performance. 
N1  - E37. The "pump power ratio" is strongly influenced by operating parameters and fluid properties, and a simple general relation is given for forced convection heat exchangers [4].. 
N1  - E38. The relations deduced for optimum flow rates can be used for design purposes, checking flow velocities in existing plants, and incorporating in algorithms for optimal operation of systems with variable speed compressors. 
