N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. Micro Phase Change Materials slurries represent the best substitute to conventional heat transfer fluids at present, providing an increase in the Coefficient of Performance and in the heat load-to-pumping power ratio compared to water up to 5% and 34%. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. A scheme forheat these configurations iseach shown and ground-coupled heat pumps (GCHP),each onein characterized the other ground-source heat pumps (GSHPs) systems required the availability of water as a thermal source, the present study focuses on the GCHP technology, which consists of a reversible vapour compression cycle where heat is exchanged with the ground through the ground heat (GHE), a closed-loop with working fluid circulating, which can be installed either in vertical boreholes or in horizontal trenches, potentially in each type of ground. 
N1  - E5. Since the other GSHP systems required the availability of water as a thermal source, the present study focuses on the GCHP technology, which consists of a reversible vapour compression cycle where heat is exchanged with the ground through the GHE, a closed6 of 25 loop with working fluid circulating, which can be installed either in vertical boreholes or in horizontal trenches, potentially in each type of ground. 
N1  - E6. In order to exploit GSHP potential, several EU research projects are ongoing with the aim to make this technology cheaper and more cost-effective by working on more cost-effective heat pumps and more efficient borehole heat exchangers, with reduced length. 
N1  - E7. The low thermal conductivity of Phase change materials (PCM) has necessitated the introduction of thermally enhanced PCMs, whose performances, in terms of thermal conductivity, are still not comparable to those of controlled lowstrength materials (CLSM) and conventional spiked grouts. 
N1  - E8. Micro Phase Change Materials slurries (MPCMS) represent the best substitute to conventional heat transfer fluids at present, providing an increase in the Coefficient of Performance (COP) and in the heat load-to-pumping power ratio compared to water up to 5% and 34%. 
N1  - E9. GSHP Technology. 
N1  - E10. The building sector in the European Union is responsible for 40% of the overall final energy consumption [1], and ground-source heat pumps (GSHPs) have been recognized as one of the most cost-effective solutions to tackle the environmental issue of this sector.. 
N1  - E11. GSHPs have been proven to be the most efficient solution on equal boundary conditions, but they hold only a small market share with respect to air-source heat pumps.. 
N1  - E12. The present study focuses on the ground-coupled heat pump (GCHP) technology, which consists of a reversible vapour compression cycle where heat is exchanged with the ground through the ground heat exchanger (GHE).. 
N1  - E13. Borehole Heat Exchangers. 
N1  - E14. The state of art of borehole heat exchangers has been studied, focusing on the parameters characterizing the outside pipe and the pipe itself, i.e., pipe and grout materials [19].. 
N1  - E15. The minimum Rb values achievable with present-day best solutions in terms of pipe and grout materials range from 0.06 to 0.08 K/(m·W).. 
N1  - E16. The thermal conductivity of the backfill material has the greatest influence on the system performance, and the choice of its thermal conductivity and heat capacity is crucial for the efficiency of the GSHP [37,38].. 
N1  - E17. Design And Control Optimization. 
N1  - E18. The advantages of GSHP technology may be undone in the absence of design and control optimization procedures, which are key steps to guarantee high efficiency of the GSHP and avoid oversizing or downsizing of the system [113].. 
N1  - E19. Several procedures are available in the open literature to reduce the energy consumption of GSHP systems by optimizing design and control strategies, usually validated on the basis of simulations or simplified applications.. 
N1  - E20. Accurate design represents the first means of reducing energy consumption in HVAC systems, and the challenge of optimal design lies in optimally determining the configuration of the plant and the heat transfer mechanisms [110].. 
N1  - E21. Design Optimization. 
N1  - E22. The ASHRAE procedure is a widely used method for designing ground-source heat pump (GSHP) systems, but it has been shown to underestimate the length of the borehole heat exchanger (BHE) by more than 10% [30].. 
N1  - E23. Mathematical model-based methods, including numerical, analytical, and computational heat transfer approaches, can provide a more accurate design, but most proposed solutions are formulated for a single borehole and may not be efficient for complex systems [131].. 
N1  - E24. A co-design approach, which integrates design and control optimization, can provide a more optimal solution [129].. 
N1  - E25. Control Optimization. 
N1  - E26. Control optimization is necessary to optimize the operation of GSHP systems, considering factors such as thermal inertia, operating time, and objective functions like efficiency, cost saving, or comfort [113].. 
N1  - E27. Model-based control methods, which use mathematical models to guide control operations, are widely applied and can provide significant cost savings [130].. 
N1  - E28. Model Predictive Control (MPC) is a promising approach that can optimize multiple parameters and predict future system states [135].. 
N1  - E29. However, the choice of control method depends on the specific GSHP system and its components.. 
N1  - E30. Multi-Source Systems. 
N1  - E31. Multi-source systems, which combine GSHP with other energy sources like solar thermal collectors or air-to-water heat pumps, can provide better performance and energy storage capabilities [136].. 
N1  - E32. These systems can optimize energy storage and release, reducing the operating costs and improving the overall efficiency of the system.. 
N1  - E33. The use of hybrid solutions, such as dual-source heat pumps, can lead to better performance compared to undersized GSHP systems [136].. 
N1  - E34. GSHP Potential. 
N1  - E35. A great potential emerges for Ground Source Heat Pumps (GSHPs) due to their high efficiency level, with a potential reduction of 20–40% and 30–50% of the energy demand for heating and cooling respectively [136].. 
N1  - E36. However, this potential is still mostly unexpressed due to economic issues regarding drilling and installation costs.. 
N1  - E37. Technological Advances. 
N1  - E38. Current research on borehole heat exchangers focuses mainly on more compact and easier to install heat exchangers, more efficient materials, and more compact drilling machines to reduce the invasiveness of installation.. 
N1  - E39. Promising developments include the use of thermally enhanced HDPE for pipe materials, graphite thermally enhanced grouts for grout materials, and Micro Phase Change Materials slurries (MPCMS) as a substitute to conventional heat transfer fluids.. 
N1  - E40. Design And Control. 
N1  - E41. Great attention has to be paid to proper design and proper control of the GSHP plant, in order to guarantee low operational costs and limit investment cost [136].. 
N1  - E42. Mathematical model-based methods have a higher chance of solving the optimal designing issue, and the use of adaptive models such as MPCs have shown great potential for GSHP applications, providing a reduction of operating cost up to 8%. 
