TI  - Experimental Analysis of Optimal Operation Mode of a Ground Source Heat Pump System
T2  - Energy Procedia
VL  - 45
PY  - 2014
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2014
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.egypro.2014.01.142
AB  - This paper presents experimental data and modeling of a ground source heat pump (GSHP) with variable speed compressor, variable speed water pumps and variable speed fans in the coils, installed at Hiref Spa (Italy) in the framework of the European Project Ground-Med. The present model has been developed to evaluate the operating conditions that lead to the maximum seasonal coefficient of performance and to analyze the behavior of the system at partial loads since variable capacity heat pumps do not work at nominal power for most of the time. The control parameters of the model that can be varied are the followings: frequency of compressor, frequency of water pump to the borehole heat exchangers, frequency of water pump to the user, velocity of the fans and water temperature to the user. The model has been compared with experimental data taken during a heating season and it can be the baseline to develop a control strategy with the final objective of maximizing the seasonal coefficient of performance of the system.
KW  - model
KW  - COP3
KW  - ground source heat pump
KW  - experimental
KW  - water pump
KW  - borehole heat exchanger
KW  - GSHP
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. This study indicated that if the on/off controlled systems were dimensioned to cover more than the 65% of the building peak demand, there was no considerable difference with the variable capacity system. 
N1  - C2. The parameters that can be varied for the heat pump system control are: electric frequency of compressor, frequency of water pumps in the external circuit, frequency of the water pump in the internal circuit, velocity of the fans and supply water temperature to the fancoils. 
N1  - C3. When decreasing the load ratio, the maximum value of the system COP (COP3) is obtained at the minimum values of water flow rate to the borehole heat exchangers and to the building because at partial loads the heat exchangers are somehow oversized, reducing the energy consumption of the water pumps becomes important as it is demonstrated when keeping constant the β parameter (Eq 5). 
N1  - C4. At higher load ratio the maximum COP3 is obtained for a water temperature difference at the condenser of about 5 K. 
N1  - C5. The present model allows to predict the system behavior: values of COP3 are predicted in the range ±10%, so it can be used as the baseline to develop a proper control strategy. 
N1  - C6. Substituting the asynchronous motor compressor lookup tables (LUTs) with a brushless DC (BLDC) motor compressor LUT, the possible SCOP3 increase is about 30%, due to higher efficiency of the BLDC at partial loads. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. This study indicated that if the on/off controlled systems were dimensioned to cover more than the 65% of the building peak demand, there was no considerable difference with the variable capacity system. 
N1  - D2. At both ends, if the on/off controlled heat pump was oversized, thus covers more than 94% of the building peak load, economic constraints prevented the option to be the best solution and if it was dimensioned to cover the 55% of the peak demand, the electrical auxiliary heaters consumption would be extremely high. 
N1  - D3. In the variable speed heat pump due to almost continuous operations the energy consumptions of the water pumps were 5-30% higher than the on/off controlled heat pumps. 
N1  - D4. Comparing the SCOP3 calculated data, at the same operating conditions, an increase of about 30% could be achieved using a BLDC motor compressor instead of the the inverter-driven asynchronous motor compressor. 
N1  - D5. Substituting the asynchronous motor compressor LUT with a BLDC motor compressor LUT, the possible SCOP3 increase is about 30%, due to higher efficiency of the BLDC at partial loads. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. This paper presents experimental data and modeling of a ground source heat pump with variable speed compressor, variable speed water pumps and variable speed fans in the coils, installed at Hiref Spa in the framework of the European Project Ground-Med. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The parameters that can be varied for the heat pump system control are: electric frequency of compressor, frequency of water pumps in the external circuit, frequency of the water pump in the internal circuit, velocity of the fans and supply water temperature to the fancoils. 
N1  - E5. When decreasing the load ratio, the maximum value of the system COP (COP3) is obtained at the minimum values of water flow rate to the borehole heat exchangers and to the building because at partial loads the heat exchangers are somehow oversized, reducing the energy consumption of the water pumps becomes important as it is demonstrated when keeping constant the β parameter (Eq 5). 
N1  - E6. At higher load ratio the maximum COP3 is obtained for a water temperature difference at the condenser of about 5 K. 
N1  - E7. The present model allows to predict the system behavior: values of COP3 are predicted in the range ±10%, so it can be used as the baseline to develop a proper control strategy. 
N1  - E8. Substituting the asynchronous motor compressor lookup tables (LUTs) with a brushless DC (BLDC) motor compressor LUT, the possible SCOP3 increase is about 30%, due to higher efficiency of the BLDC at partial loads. 
N1  - E9. Introduction To GSHP Systems. 
N1  - E10. The 68th Conference of the Italian Thermal Machines Engineering Association, ATI2013, featured an experimental analysis of the optimal operation mode of a ground source heat pump (GSHP) system [1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9].. 
N1  - E11. GSHP systems usually operate in on/off mode, but this can lead to reduced efficiency.. 
N1  - E12. A variable speed heat pump can follow the load, increasing its efficiency by taking advantage of oversized heat exchangers at partial loads.. 
N1  - E13. GSHP System Components And Efficiency. 
N1  - E14. The GSHP system analyzed consists of a reversible water-to-water ground source heat pump, four vertical 80 m deep probes, and various components such as inverter-driven scroll compressor, centrifugal pumps, and fancoils [1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9].. 
N1  - E15. The system's coefficient of performance (COP) is influenced by the frequency of the compressor speed, water tank temperatures, and load ratio.. 
N1  - E16. The load ratio α is defined as the ratio of thermal power provided by the heat pump to the nominal power.. 
N1  - E17. Experimental data were taken for two different supply water temperatures and three different values of load ratio, varying the water flow rate to the building and to the borehole heat exchangers.. 
N1  - E18. Experimental Results And Control Strategies. 
N1  - E19. Experimental results show that the water temperature difference at the borehole heat exchangers that maximizes the COP is in the range 2.4 ÷ 3.1 K, while the water temperature difference to the building is in the range 4.2 ÷ 5.4 K [1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9].. 
N1  - E20. The ratio of compressor electric power consumption to the electric power absorbed by the water pumps, β, is an important parameter to achieve maximum COP.. 
N1  - E21. The E parameter, defined in Eq 5, is almost constant when varying the load ratio, showing the opportunity of using this parameter as a control variable in the regulation strategy.. 
N1  - E22. A numerical model composed of lookup tables (LUTs) modeling the main components of the system was developed to analyze the system's performance.. 
N1  - E23. Model Development. 
N1  - E24. The GSHP model was calibrated with experimental data from a heat pump installation [1], and its accuracy was verified by comparing predicted and experimental values of COP3, which were within a ±10% error band.. 
N1  - E25. The model uses three-dimensional matrices to represent the compressor, containing cooling and heating capacities as a function of evaporating temperature, condensing temperature, and frequency of the electric motor [2].. 
N1  - E26. The model also accounts for the behavior of the boreholes, simulated using the commercial software EED and the software distributed by Davide Del Col et al [3].. 
N1  - E27. System Performance. 
N1  - E28. The model was used to analyze the performance of the heat pump system with different system layouts, including the use of an inverter-driven asynchronous motor compressor and a brushless DC (BLDC) motor compressor [4].. 
N1  - E29. The results showed that the BLDC motor compressor allows for a significant increase in COP1 at partial loads (+27%, +33%, and +43% at α=0.8, 0.60, and 0.40, respectively) due to its higher efficiency [5].. 
N1  - E30. The model also predicted that the maximum seasonal performance factor SCOP3 can be achieved by optimizing the operating conditions, including the frequency of the compressor, water pump to the borehole heat exchangers, and water pump to the user [6].. 
N1  - E31. Optimization And Comparison. 
N1  - E32. The model was used to compare the performance of the heat pump system with different supply water temperatures and compressor types [7].. 
N1  - E33. The results showed that using a BLDC motor compressor instead of an inverter-driven asynchronous motor compressor can increase the SCOP3 by about 30% [8].. 
N1  - E34. The model also allows for the replacement of lookup tables (LUTs) of any component, enabling the analysis of different system layouts and optimization of the control strategy [9].. 
N1  - E35. The authors acknowledge the support of the European project GROUND-MED, co-funded by the European Commission within the Seventh Framework Programme [10]. 
