TY  - JOUR
AU  - Mader, Gunda 
AU  - Madani, Hatef 
TI  - Capacity control in air–water heat pumps: Total cost of ownership analysis
T2  - Energy and Buildings
VL  - 81
PY  - 2014
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2014
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enbuild.2014.06.029
AB  - Adjusting capacity to changing demand by variable speed control is known to offer efficiency improvement over classical on/off control. With a total cost of ownership analysis the economic viability of both control schemes is assessed for residential air–water heat pumps operating in different climate zones. Component sizes are optimized for both control methods individually. Results show optimal compressor displacement volumes to be smaller for variable speed than for on/off control. The optimal ratio of evaporator to condenser size is smaller for the variable speed system. Variable speed control is shown to be uneconomic for space heating in warmer climate while for average climate cost-effectiveness depends on the economic framework. For colder climate variable speed control is the more profitable choice in all considered cases; savings of up to 5000 EUR compared to on/off control can be achieved within 15 years of operation.
KW  - Heat pump
KW  - climate zone
KW  - total cost of ownership
KW  - Optimization
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Changes in the control scheme can reduce emissions associated with air source heat pumps operating over a wide range of conditions by more than a third [1]. 
N1  - C2. The gradient of the curves steepen, when electric heat is added. This occurs for the fixed speed on/off (FS) system below balance point temperature Ta,b and for variable speed (VS) control below the upper speed limit temperature Ta,u. 
N1  - C3. The general trend for differences in total cost of ownership (TCO) is not reversed: In the warm climate zone VS control is too costly, in the average climate zone the benefit depends on the economic framework and variable speed control pays off in the colder climate. 
N1  - C4. It can be assumed that effects of points above and below the linear curve will outbalance each other in an economic comparison of fixed and variable speed control. 
N1  - C5. Variable speed units optimized for colder climates are slightly more cost efficient when run in the average climate than fixed speed units optimized for colder climates. 
N1  - C6. In the warmer climate zone the most cost efficient option is a fixed speed unit optimized for the conditions encountered there. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The performance of the FS unit with electrical backup heat covering less than 5% of the annual demand was the same as the performance of the VS unit. 
N1  - D2. Deviations between seasonal power consumption calculated by Eq (3) and by the quadratic model (Eq (4)) are below 0.5% within the design region. 
N1  - D3. At higher ambient temperatures isentropic efficiency is up to 8% higher for FS operation. 
N1  - D4. Within the given size limits a maximum TCO increase of about 12% can be observed for undersized heat exchangers. 
N1  - D5. • No up to date values for prices of speed control of compressor and fan were available. 50% higher costs than assumed in this study increase payback time to 4 years for colder and 11 years for average climate (0.21 EUR kWh−1, interest rate 3%). 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. Optimal component sizes differ for fixed and variable speed control. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The gradient of the curves steepen, when electric heat is added. This occurs for the fixed speed on/off (FS) system below balance point temperature Ta,b and for variable speed (VS) control below the upper speed limit temperature Ta,u. 
N1  - E5. The general trend for differences in total cost of ownership (TCO) is not reversed: In the warm climate zone VS control is too costly, in the average climate zone the benefit depends on the economic framework and variable speed control pays off in the colder climate. 
N1  - E6. It can be assumed that effects of points above and below the linear curve will outbalance each other in an economic comparison of fixed and variable speed control. 
N1  - E7. Variable speed units optimized for colder climates are slightly more cost efficient when run in the average climate than fixed speed units optimized for colder climates. 
N1  - E8. In the warmer climate zone the most cost efficient option is a fixed speed unit optimized for the conditions encountered there. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. The goal of this study is to quantify the total cost of ownership (TCO) of VS and FS control in residential air–water heat pumps for different climate zones. 
N1  - E11. Control Methods. 
N1  - E12. Variable speed control is compared to on/off control for residential air–water heat pumps, with results showing that variable speed control can offer efficiency improvements [1, 2, 3].. 
N1  - E13. The optimal compressor displacement volume is smaller for variable speed control than for on/off control.. 
N1  - E14. The optimal ratio of evaporator to condenser size is also smaller for the variable speed system.. 
N1  - E15. Economic Viability. 
N1  - E16. The economic viability of variable speed control is assessed for different climate zones, with results showing that it is uneconomic for space heating in warmer climates, while in average climates cost-effectiveness depends on the economic framework [4, 5].. 
N1  - E17. In colder climates, variable speed control is the more profitable choice, with potential savings of up to 5000 EUR compared to on/off control within 15 years of operation.. 
N1  - E18. System Optimization. 
N1  - E19. The study optimizes air flow rate, compressor displacement volume, and heat exchanger sizes to give minimum total cost of ownership for both fixed speed and variable speed systems [6, 7, 8].. 
N1  - E20. A quadratic model is used to describe annual power consumption as a function of component sizes, allowing for the calculation of a new optimum for changing price information without requiring new system simulations [9, 10, 11].. 
N1  - E21. Methodology. 
N1  - E22. The study uses a heat pump model to calculate Qand COP at several part load conditions defined in EN14825 [1].. 
N1  - E23. Losses in on/off operation are taken into account by correcting COP with Eq (1) from EN14825 [1].. 
N1  - E24. The standard value of 0.9 for the cycling degradation coefficient cc is used [1].. 
N1  - E25. A quadratic model is used to describe the seasonal power consumption W of the system for each climate zone as a function of the inner heat transfer area of evaporator Ae and condenser Ac (Eq (4)) [23].. 
N1  - E26. Results. 
N1  - E27. Simulations show that variable speed control reduces operating costs of the heat pump below the balance point temperature of the fixed speed system [24].. 
N1  - E28. However, above the balance point temperature, the operating costs for the variable speed system become higher than for on/off control [24].. 
N1  - E29. The study finds that the optimal compressor size increases with lower minimum operating temperatures of the climate zones [25].. 
N1  - E30. Optimal compressor sizes for variable speed control are smaller than for fixed speed control [25].. 
N1  - E31. Economic Analysis. 
N1  - E32. The discounted net present value method is applied to calculate the total cost of ownership (TCO) [26].. 
N1  - E33. The study finds that the TCO difference between fixed and variable speed control is sensitive to different economic scenarios [24].. 
N1  - E34. In the warmer climate zone, variable speed control is more costly than fixed speed control for all considered scenarios [24].. 
N1  - E35. In the average climate zone, variable speed control is profitable for scenarios with higher electricity prices and low interest rate [24].. 
N1  - E36. The variable speed system is cost-efficient for the colder climate zone, independent of the considered economic scenario [24].. 
N1  - E37. Economic Comparison. 
N1  - E38. The study compares the total cost of ownership (TCO) of fixed speed (FS) and variable speed (VS) heat pumps, considering factors like investment costs, operating costs, and maintenance costs [27].. 
N1  - E39. Longer time periods favor VS systems by shifting the emphasis from investment costs to operating costs.. 
N1  - E40. The TCO difference between FS and VS systems decreases by about 20% when the cycle degradation coefficient during on/off control is increased from 0.9 to 0.95.. 
N1  - E41. System Design And Performance. 
N1  - E42. The design temperature and design building capacity determine the slope of the building demand curve, which affects the performance of FS and VS systems.. 
N1  - E43. VS systems are less beneficial for small and well-insulated buildings, as the capacity curve of a FS heat pump approximates the building demand curve more closely.. 
N1  - E44. The study also notes that building demand depends on factors like user behavior, solar radiation, and building dynamics, which can affect the economic comparison between FS and VS control [8].. 
N1  - E45. Climate And Operating Conditions. 
N1  - E46. The study finds that VS control is most promising for heat pumps operating in colder climate zones, where it can reduce electricity consumption at low ambient temperatures.. 
N1  - E47. The optimal component sizes differ for FS and VS control, with VS control requiring a smaller compressor displacement volume and a lower ratio of optimal evaporator to condenser area.. 
N1  - E48. The study also notes that frost and defrost effects are not taken into account, but could affect the annual performance of FS and VS systems, particularly in colder climate zones [27]. 
