TI  - In situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency of ground source heat pump systems: Analysis for multistage heat pump units
T2  - Energy and Buildings
VL  - 88
PY  - 2014
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2014
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enbuild.2014.12.008
AB  - In order to optimize the global energy performance of a ground source heat pump system, special attention needs to be paid to the auxiliaries as they stand for a considerable part of the total energy consumption. A new in situ experimental methodology based on the frequency variation of the water circulation pumps in order to optimize the energy performance of the system was previously published by the authors for a ground source heat pump system using a single stage heat pump with ON/OFF regulation. The original single stage heat pump was recently replaced with a multistage unit consisting of two compressors of the same capacity working in tandem. A new experimental campaign was carried out and a new study was performed in order to adapt the in situ optimization methodology to the performance of the tandem compressors unit, and, by extension, to the multistage case. This paper presents the in situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency adapted for multistage ground source heat pump systems. Results show that energy savings up to 32% can be obtained by applying this optimization methodology.
KW  - Ground source heat pump
KW  - energy performance
KW  - water circulation
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. In the current context of global warming concern, special attention should be paid to the efficient use of renewable energies. 
N1  - C2. The authors previously developed an experimental in situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency of ground source heat pump systems when single stage ON/OFF regulation is employed [12]. 
N1  - C3. This paper describes the in situ methodology to determine those optimum frequencies for a multistage heat pump unit. 
N1  - C4. When analyzing a multistage heat pump unit, which consists of a specific number of compressors (N) working in tandem, special attention should be paid to the thermal demand of the building, since it will determine the number of stages in operation and the compressor consumption. 
N1  - C5. The methodology previously developed and published by the authors for single stage heat pump units with ON/OFF regulation was adapted for multistage heat pump units. 
N1  - C6. Given that the in situ optimization methodology has been developed for both single stage and multistage heat pump units with ON/OFF regulation, further studies will focus on the adaptation of the methodology to Ground source heat pump (GSHP) installations with variable speed heat pump units. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. Results show that energy savings up to 32% can be obtained by applying this optimization methodology. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. Given that the in situ optimization methodology has been developed for both single stage and multistage heat pump units with ON/OFF regulation, further studies will focus on the adaptation of the methodology to Ground source heat pump installations with variable speed heat pump units. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The authors previously developed an experimental in situ optimization methodology for the water circulation pumps frequency of ground source heat pump systems when single stage ON/OFF regulation is employed [12]. 
N1  - E5. This paper describes the in situ methodology to determine those optimum frequencies for a multistage heat pump unit. 
N1  - E6. When analyzing a multistage heat pump unit, which consists of a specific number of compressors (N) working in tandem, special attention should be paid to the thermal demand of the building, since it will determine the number of stages in operation and the compressor consumption. 
N1  - E7. The methodology previously developed and published by the authors for single stage heat pump units with ON/OFF regulation was adapted for multistage heat pump units. 
N1  - E8. Given that the in situ optimization methodology has been developed for both single stage and multistage heat pump units with ON/OFF regulation, further studies will focus on the adaptation of the methodology to Ground source heat pump (GSHP) installations with variable speed heat pump units. 
N1  - E9. Optimization Methodology. 
N1  - E10. The authors developed an in situ experimental methodology to optimize the water circulation pumps frequency for ground source heat pump systems with multistage heat pump units [1, 2].. 
N1  - E11. The methodology aims to reduce energy consumption by optimizing the system's performance, considering the thermal demand of the building and the operation state of the heat pump unit.. 
N1  - E12. The study was carried out for a heat pump unit with two compressors of the same capacity working in tandem, and the results show that energy savings up to 32% can be obtained by applying this optimization methodology [12].. 
N1  - E13. An in situ experimental methodology is proposed to obtain the optimum circulation pumps frequency leading to the minimum energy consumption of a GSHP installation using a multistage heat pump unit with two compressors working in tandem [12].. 
N1  - E14. The methodology consists of three steps: (1) experimental tests of pseudorandom sequence of frequency steps for both internal and external circulation pumps, (2) analysis of the results and estimation of the system COP, and (3) extrapolation of the performance maps to any partial load ratio of the system.. 
N1  - E15. The methodology is based on experimental measurements, taking into consideration the real characteristics of the heat pump and the installation.. 
N1  - E16. System Performance. 
N1  - E17. The performance of the heat pump and the entire system is affected by the increase of the circulating water flow rate [12].. 
N1  - E18. A higher water flow rate enhances the heat transfer coefficient through the heat exchanger of the heat pump and diminishes the water temperature variation across it.. 
N1  - E19. However, operating at maximum flow rates results in a great reduction of the system COP due to the big influence of the circulation pumps consumption [12].. 
N1  - E20. The system performance factor (PFsys) is calculated as a function of the partial load ratio of each stage (̨) using Eqs (7) and (8), which relate the quasi-steady state performance maps to the partial load ratio [12].. 
N1  - E21. The resulting system COP maps for one typical heating and one typical cooling day are shown in Fig. 6, with the optimal pair of frequencies corresponding to quasisteady state working conditions.. 
N1  - E22. The system performance factor maps as a function of the water circulation pumps frequency for four different values of the partial load ratio of the system are shown in Figs 7 and 8 for heating and cooling mode, respectively.. 
N1  - E23. The system COP maps for the heat pump operating with one compressor show that the influence of the internal pump is higher than that of the external pump [12,13].. 
N1  - E24. The optimums correspond to low frequencies (between 30 Hz and 35 Hz) because the influence of the consumption of the circulation pumps is important in comparison to the total consumption.. 
N1  - E25. When the heat pump works with two compressors in tandem, the system COP maps become different and the influence of the circulation pumps is smaller.. 
N1  - E26. Thermal Demand. 
N1  - E27. The thermal demand of the building is a key factor in determining the optimal pair of frequencies for the water circulation pumps [12].. 
N1  - E28. The partial load ratio of the system, which is the relation between the instantaneous thermal load of the building and the heat pump capacity, is used to characterize the thermal demand of the building [12].. 
N1  - E29. For a multistage heat pump, the partial load ratio of each stage must be calculated separately, taking into account the operation state of the heat pump unit [12].. 
N1  - E30. Energy Efficiency. 
N1  - E31. The energy performance of the GSHP installation is characterized by the energy performance factor, defined as the ratio between the thermal load and the electric energy consumption during a time interval [12].. 
N1  - E32. The energy performance factor can be seasonal, monthly, daily, etc., with the most representative one being the seasonal performance factor (SPF), which estimates the system performance during each season.. 
N1  - E33. The system coefficient of performance is calculated using Eq (4), which includes the power consumption of the whole system, comprising the consumption of the heat pump compressors, external and internal circulation pumps, and parasitic losses [15].. 
N1  - E34. Methodology. 
N1  - E35. The experimental tests from step 1 of the methodology should be repeated at least three times: for the first stage (n = 1), for the maximum capacity of the heat pump (n = N) and also for an intermediate stage [12,13].. 
N1  - E36. The intermediate stage can be chosen by means of an expression provided by an equation.. 
N1  - E37. To save time, some experimental points can be removed from the test matrix, but considering a number of points less than 15 would result in a loss of resolution and the optimum not being accurately determined.. 
N1  - E38. The optimization methodology was adapted and proposed for multistage heat pump units with any number of compressors [no citation].. 
N1  - E39. The in situ optimization methodology has been developed for both single stage and multistage heat pump units with ON/OFF regulation [no citation].. 
N1  - E40. Optimization Results. 
N1  - E41. The optimization methodology developed allows the characterization of the system COP and the system performance factor, and the determination of the optimal pair of frequencies that maximize the system performance factor as a function of the building thermal load [12,13].. 
N1  - E42. Experimental tests showed that energy savings of (31.70 ± 0.07)% were obtained by applying the optimized control, with the variation of the circulation pumps accounting for most of the energy savings.. 
N1  - E43. Energy Savings. 
N1  - E44. The system showed energy savings of (29.4 ± 0.3)% for a typical heating day [no citation], with a source temperature of 16.2 ◦C for the standard day and 16.5 ◦C for the optimized day, and a sink temperature of 39.6 ◦C for the standard day and 35.2 ◦C for the optimized day [no citation].. 
N1  - E45. Considering the whole analyzed period, energy savings of (31.70 ± 0.07)% were obtained [no citation].. 
N1  - E46. Funding. 
N1  - E47. This work was supported by the “Programa de Ayudas de Investigación y Desarrollo (PAID)” of the Universitat Politècnica de València [no citation] and the European FP7 project “Advanced ground source heat pump systems for heating and cooling in Mediterranean climate” (GROUND-MED) [no citation]. 
