TY  - JOUR
AU  - Beaudry, Gabrielle 
AU  - Pasquier, Philippe 
AU  - Marcotte, Denis 
AU  - Zarrella, Angelo 
TI  - Flow rate control in standing column wells: A flexible solution for reducing the energy use and peak power demand of the built environment
T2  - Applied Energy
VL  - 313
PY  - 2022
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2022
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.apenergy.2022.118774
AB  - Standing column wells offer a generous and flexible solution to improve the energy efficiency of the built environment. As these systems behavior is strongly affected by groundwater advection, the acute challenge of predicting their thermal evolution with both accuracy and computational efficiency has so far hindered the development of design tools and optimized control strategies. In this work, a powerful simulation algorithm is implemented and applied to the performance assessment of multi-borehole standing column well systems located in a heterogeneous geological environment and operating under various constant and dynamic flow rate control strategies. The iterative algorithm relies on the non-stationary convolution technique to simulate the underground components, and the EnergyPlus approach to represent the heat pump efficiency at full and part loads. The findings suggest that using higher flow rates in peak conditions is a key element that minimizes auxiliary assistance and power demand. Complementary variable flow rate control aiming to maintain a 2 ◦C temperature difference across the plate heat exchanger has shown to alleviate groundwater usage and generate at least 8%–11% annual energy savings compared with constant flow. This operating strategy allowed the systems to deliver 197–246 W/m with a heating seasonal performance factor of 3.59. These results were achieved through 34 annual simulations having hourly time steps that were performed with the proposed algorithm in a total of 4 h 17 min, compared to 11 days for a single simulation using a numerical reference model.
KW  - Standing column well
KW  - Simulation
KW  - ground heat exchanger
KW  - Ground-source heat pump
KW  - Power demand
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. A powerful simulation algorithm is implemented and applied to the performance assessment of multi-borehole standing column well systems located in a heterogeneous geological environment and operating under various constant and dynamic flow rate control strategies. 
N1  - C2. The results of several annual simulations comparing various flow rate control strategies are presented to illustrate the beneficial effect of variable flow rate control for simultaneously reducing the systems’ energy consumption, power demand, operating costs and groundwater usage. 
N1  - C3. The results demonstrate that variable flow rate control aiming to maintain a 2 °C fluid temperature difference across the heat pump leads to an increased energy efficiency, nearzero auxiliary solicitation and 35 kW of peak power usage instead of the 131 kW that would require an electric resistance heating system having a 100% efficiency. 
N1  - C4. We proposed a powerful simulation algorithm allowing the fast computation of the operating temperatures and performance indicators of a standing column well (SCW) system. 
N1  - C5. We showed that this flexible modeling strategy allows considering important contributors to SCWs thermal efficiency, namely the presence of a multi-layered geological environment having heterogeneous hydraulic and thermal conductivity distributions, and the use of a variable flow rate control strategy. 
N1  - C6. ground-source heat pump (GSHP) have maintained an annual growth rate of around 10% over the last ten years and reached a 77.5 GWt worldwide installed capacity as of 2020 [3]. 
N1  - C7. These results reveal the potential of the proposed simulation algorithm for future design purposes and developing innovative control strategies, leading to improved performance of SCW systems. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. GSHPs have maintained an annual growth rate of around 10% over the last ten years and reached a 77.5 GWt worldwide installed capacity as of 2020 [3]. 
N1  - D2. Montagud et al [9] estimated that this strategy can lead up to 28% reductions in annual energy consumption. 
N1  - D3. Beurcq [13, 14] obtained similar results in a rare numerical study investigating the influence of control strategies on the operation of SCWs in cold climates. They demonstrated the interest of using a variable-speed submersible pump to control the pumping and discharge rates linearly as a function of the load on the building loop, with energy and costs savings going up to 20% compared to good practice scenarios. 
N1  - D4. In Case 3, the discharge ratio is increased at 30% for the highest flow rate level, in order to maximize the system’s efficiency during peak demand periods. 
N1  - D5. The process is repeated iteratively until the correction on the ground loads is less than 0.75% of the maximal load. 
N1  - D6. Note that the discharge flow rates increase from g1 to g8. The difference between both systems appears important at lower discharge ratios (g1 and g2 have ratios of 0% and 10%, respectively), while it gradually diminishes as this parameter increases (20% for g3 to g7, and 30% for g8). 
N1  - D7. The dynamic flow rate cases show more steady and resilient results, and use up to 11% (4-SCWs) and 8% (5-SCWs) less energy annually than the best constant flow rate scenarios. 
N1  - D8. Comparison of Case 3b with 4 SCWs and Case 4b with 5 SCWs, which are very similar in terms of annual energy use, peak demand and operating costs, also suggests that the better efficiency of the 5-SCWs system at lower discharge flow rates would allow reducing the annual volume of discharged groundwater by 32% compared with the 4-SCWs. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The findings suggest that using higher flow rates in peak conditions is a key element that minimizes auxiliary assistance and power demand. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The results demonstrate that variable flow rate control aiming to maintain a 2 °C fluid temperature difference across the heat pump leads to an increased energy efficiency, nearzero auxiliary solicitation and 35 kW of peak power usage instead of the 131 kW that would require an electric resistance heating system having a 100% efficiency. 
N1  - E5. We proposed a powerful simulation algorithm allowing the fast computation of the operating temperatures and performance indicators of a standing column well (SCW) system. 
N1  - E6. We showed that this flexible modeling strategy allows considering important contributors to SCWs thermal efficiency, namely the presence of a multi-layered geological environment having heterogeneous hydraulic and thermal conductivity distributions, and the use of a variable flow rate control strategy. 
N1  - E7. ground-source heat pump (GSHP) have maintained an annual growth rate of around 10% over the last ten years and reached a 77.5 GWt worldwide installed capacity as of 2020 [3]. 
N1  - E8. These results reveal the potential of the proposed simulation algorithm for future design purposes and developing innovative control strategies, leading to improved performance of SCW systems. 
N1  - E9. Introduction To GSHP. 
N1  - E10. The widespread electrification of the built environment is a key element on the road to achieving carbon neutrality [1].. 
N1  - E11. Ground-source heat pump (GSHP) systems represent an opportunity to support building decarbonization by mitigating the overall costs and impacts of space heating electrification [2].. 
N1  - E12. GSHPs have maintained an annual growth rate of around 10% over the last ten years and reached a 77.5 GWt worldwide installed capacity as of 2020 [3].. 
N1  - E13. Standing Column Wells. 
N1  - E14. Standing column wells (SCWs) offer a generous and flexible solution to improve the energy efficiency of the built environment [4].. 
N1  - E15. SCWs retrieve heat directly from the groundwater being drawn up in an uncased borehole, providing reductions of 49 to 78% of the size of the GHE compared with a conventional closed-loop system of similar capacity, along with significant cuts in capital and life-cycle costs [5].. 
N1  - E16. Optimal control strategies are crucial to maximize system operational efficiency and generate lifecycle savings [6,7,8].. 
N1  - E17. Flow rate control has shown to provide impressive energy gains through the reduction of circulation pumps power consumption [9,10].. 
N1  - E18. Simulation And Results. 
N1  - E19. A powerful simulation algorithm is implemented and applied to the performance assessment of multi-borehole SCW systems located in a heterogeneous geological environment and operating under various constant and dynamic flow rate control strategies [31].. 
N1  - E20. The findings suggest that using higher flow rates in peak conditions is a key element that minimizes auxiliary assistance and power demand [11].. 
N1  - E21. Complementary variable flow rate control aiming to maintain a 2 ◦C temperature difference across the plate heat exchanger has shown to alleviate groundwater usage and generate at least 8%–11% annual energy savings compared with constant flow [12].. 
N1  - E22. The proposed algorithm relies on the non-stationary convolution technique to simulate the underground components, and the EnergyPlus approach to represent the heat pump efficiency at full and part loads [32].. 
N1  - E23. Site Description. 
N1  - E24. The study site has three main geological units: unconsolidated deposits of till in the first 10 m, a sandy dolomite down to a depth of 99 m, and a quartz arenite at greater depths [36].. 
N1  - E25. The water table static level is at a 2.5 m depth with a 0.006 m/m hydraulic gradient.. 
N1  - E26. A constant undisturbed ground temperature of 8.5 °C was observed down to a 133 m depth.. 
N1  - E27. System Design. 
N1  - E28. The ground source heat pump (GSHP) system was designed to cover 80% of the peak power demand for heating, with an auxiliary electric resistance heating system to balance out the remaining loads [37,38].. 
N1  - E29. A water-to-water heat pump was selected, and two standing column well (SCW) systems composed of four and five boreholes were investigated.. 
N1  - E30. The boreholes are 133 m deep, with a 152 mm diameter, and are placed 6 m apart in a parallel arrangement to minimize thermal interaction.. 
N1  - E31. Control Strategies. 
N1  - E32. Several control strategies involving constant and variable flow rates were investigated to minimize the overall annual energy use and operating cost of the study system [12,13,14].. 
N1  - E33. The strategies considered include adjusting the flow rate levels to maintain constant temperature differences across the heat pump, and reducing the pumps' frequency at lower loads to minimize the system's overall energy use.. 
N1  - E34. The discharge flow rate was also considered as a ratio of the total pumping rate, with values ranging from 0% to 30% [13,14].. 
N1  - E35. Model Development. 
N1  - E36. The solution to the system is computed as the sum of the independent solutions to each individual impulse contained in the input function f(t) [31].. 
N1  - E37. A scaled correction function λ(t) is used to accurately represent the situation, which is dependent on the active transfer function at each time step: ΔT(t) = (f ∗ g)(t) − λ(t).. 
N1  - E38. The water-to-water heat pump is modeled following the EnergyPlus approach [32].. 
N1  - E39. The energy performance of the system is evaluated using equations (6) to (9) in the heating and cooling modes.. 
N1  - E40. System Evaluation. 
N1  - E41. The overall performance of the system is evaluated annually as a function of the total energy use, peak power demand, operating costs, and cumulative volume of groundwater discharged from the wells [34].. 
N1  - E42. The energy use of the system is determined by the heat pump compressor, auxiliary system, and pumping energy.. 
N1  - E43. The pumps' work is expressed as a function of the fluid flow rate, pressure loss, and pump's efficiency.. 
N1  - E44. The pressure loss is related to friction within the mechanical equipment and inside the GHE, and to the vertical lift due to the static and dynamic levels of the water table.. 
N1  - E45. Simulation Results. 
N1  - E46. The results of several annual simulations comparing various flow rate control strategies are presented, illustrating the beneficial effect of variable flow rate control for reducing the system's energy consumption, power demand, operating costs, and groundwater usage [12, 41].. 
N1  - E47. The dynamic flow rate cases show more steady and resilient results, using up to 11% (4-SCWs) and 8% (5-SCWs) less energy annually than the best constant flow rate scenarios.. 
N1  - E48. The results suggest that smaller temperature differences should be maintained to minimize peak power demand in SCW systems.. 
N1  - E49. Variable flow rate control is a cost-effective solution that allows minimizing both energy consumption and power usage [34].. 
N1  - E50. System Performance. 
N1  - E51. The study analyzed the performance of a school's heating and cooling system using a shallow geothermal system with 4 or 5 boreholes [24].. 
N1  - E52. The results showed that the system can provide the annual heating and cooling energy needs with heat exchange rates of 197–246 W/m and heating and cooling seasonal performance factors of 3.59 and 5.46, respectively.. 
N1  - E53. The system's operating costs were found to be in the range of $9,751-$10,143, with a total energy consumption of 71–76 MWh [42].. 
N1  - E54. Groundwater Usage. 
N1  - E55. The amount of groundwater discharged from the wells was found to be minimized when operating the system with variable flow rate control, reducing the pressure on groundwater resources [24].. 
N1  - E56. The results showed that the 5-SCW system can reduce the annual volume of discharged groundwater by 32% compared to the 4-SCW system.. 
N1  - E57. Simulation Algorithm. 
N1  - E58. A simulation algorithm was proposed to compute the operating temperatures and performance indicators of the SCW system, which was validated using a numerical reference model [27].. 
N1  - E59. The algorithm was found to be accurate, with a mean absolute error of 0.06 °C, and computationally efficient, allowing for 34 annual simulations to be completed in 4 hours and 17 minutes [39].. 
N1  - E60. The algorithm's flexibility and computing efficiency make it suitable for future design purposes and developing innovative control strategies to improve the performance of SCW systems. 
