TI  - Evaluation of short and long-term performance of a dual-source heat pump
T2  - 
VL  - 
PY  - 2024
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2024
UR  - 
DO  - 10.22488/okstate.24.000035
AB  - A dual-source heat pump is a heat pump coupled with two heat sources/sinks, such as the ground and the air. Such solutions allow to balance the thermal load to the ground, shifting part of the heating or cooling thermal load to the finned coil heat exchanger and preventing the possible degradation of the long-term ground-source heat pump performance. In addition, a similar configuration can be more economically convenient than a ground source heat pump. Depending on the plant configuration and the boundary conditions (mainly climatic area), the control strategy to switch between the two heat sources and sinks is crucial to ensure good heat pump performance in the short and long-term operation of the system. This work investigates a control strategy to choose the value of switch temperature, which establishes the most favorable heat source or sink to be used by the heat pump. A dynamic model is developed using TRNSYS to study and predict the thermal and electrical behavior of double-source heat pumps, which can be used in the context of retrofitted and new buildings under different weather conditions. The modeled system simulates an existing commercial building, where a dual-source heat pump is installed and whose operation data are monitored.
KW  - ground-source heat pump
KW  - Coefficient of Performance
KW  - heat exchanger
KW  - heat source
KW  - TRNSYS
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Towards the electrification of the building sector, using ground-source heat pumps can lead to an improvement in energy performance and a reduction in operating costs compared to traditional air-source heat pumps. 
N1  - C2. This work proposes a methodology for dynamically evaluating the best heat source or sink to use in a dual-source heat pump system. 
N1  - C3. The dynamic model was applied to a real case study, where boundary conditions, like the number of borehole heat exchangers and climatic data, were set. 
N1  - C4. The results of the parametric analysis applied to this case study show the economic inconvenience of fully balancing the thermal load to the ground. 
N1  - C5. Such operation leads to higher system performance in heating mode, it cannot compensate for the decrease in cooling performance. In this case, environmental aspects due to the ground thermal stress should be considered. This approach is based on a long-term performance evaluation, and allows to dynamically adjust the switch temperature to reduce the electrical consumption of the dual-source heat pump and to prevent the ground thermal drift. 
N1  - C6. The possibility to dynamically adapt the settings according to changes that might occur in real applications, such as an unexpected increase in thermal load due to extreme weather conditions, or increase/decrease in the heated or cooled areas, is extremely interesting. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The models are reported in Eq (3) and Eq (4) and presented a RMSE lower than 1% compared with catalog data. 
N1  - D2. The models presented a RMSE lower than 2%, Although the models still depend on the source and load sides entering and exiting water temperatures, a new term has been introduced, which is the partial load ratio (and its square), calculated as the heating (or cooling) capacity in partial load divided by the nominal heating (or cooling) capacity declared by the manufacturer. 
N1  - D3. In particular, the equation has the same shape as Eq 6, where SEFT is the temperature of the external ait. For this model, the RMSE is lower than 4% compared to manufacturet’s data. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. This work proposes a methodology for dynamically evaluating the best heat source or sink to use in a dual-source heat pump system. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. This work proposes a methodology for dynamically evaluating the best heat source or sink to use in a dual-source heat pump system. 
N1  - E5. The dynamic model was applied to a real case study, where boundary conditions, like the number of borehole heat exchangers and climatic data, were set. 
N1  - E6. The results of the parametric analysis applied to this case study show the economic inconvenience of fully balancing the thermal load to the ground. 
N1  - E7. Such operation leads to higher system performance in heating mode, it cannot compensate for the decrease in cooling performance. In this case, environmental aspects due to the ground thermal stress should be considered. This approach is based on a long-term performance evaluation, and allows to dynamically adjust the switch temperature to reduce the electrical consumption of the dual-source heat pump and to prevent the ground thermal drift. 
N1  - E8. The possibility to dynamically adapt the settings according to changes that might occur in real applications, such as an unexpected increase in thermal load due to extreme weather conditions, or increase/decrease in the heated or cooled areas, is extremely interesting. 
N1  - E9. Introduction. 
N1  - E10. A dual-source heat pump (DSHP) is a heat pump coupled with two heat sources/sinks, such as the ground and the air, to balance the thermal load to the ground and prevent degradation of the long-term ground-source heat pump performance (Grossi et al, 2018).. 
N1  - E11. The control strategy to switch between the two heat sources and sinks is crucial to ensure good heat pump performance in the short and long-term operation of the system.. 
N1  - E12. Control Strategy. 
N1  - E13. The switch between air and ground heat sources is usually decided based on different criteria, including the maximization of the system's instantaneous energy performance, its annual performance, or its energy costs (Grossi et al, 2018).. 
N1  - E14. A dynamic model is developed using TRNSYS to predict and evaluate the thermal and electrical behavior of a DSHP system, which can be used in retrofitted or new buildings under different weather conditions (Solar Energy Laboratory, 1975).. 
N1  - E15. The novelty of this study is that the value of Tswitch can be varied dynamically to minimize the thermal unbalance to the ground, using a new TRNSYS component.. 
N1  - E16. Modeling And Simulation. 
N1  - E17. The modeled system simulates an existing commercial building, where a dual-source heat pump is installed and whose operation data are monitored (Zarrella et al, 2020).. 
N1  - E18. The building's energy model is developed using EnergyPlus (EnergyPlus, 2016), and the heat pump models are calibrated using manufacturer's data (Bordignon et al, 2023).. 
N1  - E19. The TRNSYS plugin of GenOPT (Wetter, 2001) is used to perform a parametric analysis for the dynamic selection of Tswitch, and the simulations are carried out assuming the DSHP was operating continuously from 2005 to 2022 (Corberan et al, 2018).. 
N1  - E20. Models. 
N1  - E21. The study discusses the development of models for calculating the Coefficient of Performance (COP) of old Ground Source Heat Pumps (GSHP) and new Dual-Source Heat Pumps (DSHP) (Bordignon et al, 2023).. 
N1  - E22. For the old GSHP, two models (wh09 and wc01) were selected and calibrated using the generalized least squares method, with a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) lower than 1% compared to catalog data.. 
N1  - E23. For the new DSHP, water-source and air-source models were used, with the water-source model modified to consider partial load operation, and the air-source model having the same shape as the water-source model.. 
N1  - E24. Switch Temperature. 
N1  - E25. The study introduces a novel method for selecting the switch temperature (Tswitch) for the DSHP, which is based on the cumulative energy rejected to and extracted from the ground.. 
N1  - E26. The Tswitch is calculated separately for heating and cooling, and is adjusted based on the ground heat unbalance.. 
N1  - E27. The method allows for the correction of the switch temperature to balance the thermal load to the ground.. 
N1  - E28. Parametric Analysis. 
N1  - E29. A parametric analysis was performed to evaluate the best combination of maximum and minimum Tswitch, multiplier, and time interval that minimizes the total electrical consumption over 17 years of simulation (Bordignon et al, 2023).. 
N1  - E30. The results show that the variable Tswitch case leads to higher electrical consumption (+0.5%) compared to the baseline case, due to a lower Seasonal Performance Factor (SPF) in cooling, which is not compensated by the increased SPF in heating mode.. 
N1  - E31. The study concludes that fully balancing the thermal load to the ground may not be economically convenient, and that the proposed methodology can help evaluate the trade-offs between system performance and electrical consumption.. 
N1  - E32. Adaptability. 
N1  - E33. The approach allows for dynamic adjustment of the switch temperature to reduce electrical consumption and prevent ground thermal drift (no specific author mentioned).. 
N1  - E34. The possibility of adapting settings to changes in real applications, such as extreme weather conditions or changes in heated or cooled areas, is also noted (no specific author mentioned). 
