TI  - An optimal control method for small-scale GSHP-integrated air-conditioning system to improve indoor thermal environment control
T2  - Journal of Building Engineering
VL  - 59
PY  - 2022
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2022
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.jobe.2022.105140
AB  - ON/OFF controlled ground-source heat pump (GSHP) is often integrated with small-scale central air-conditioning system (ACS) for villas. Chilled-water temperature (CHWT) in this system usually oscillates in a large range due to the ON/OFF switch of the compressor. As it may be too high or too low, the cooling output of the air-handling unit (AHU) in its adjustable range may not be coincident with the indoor cooling demand, which may lead to unsatisfactory room temperature control performance. This study proposes an optimal control method for this system to improve the control robustness of the room temperature. Usually, the variation of indoor cooling-load is transferred to the chilled-water resulting in changing water temperature. In this method, the optimal chilled-water return (CHWR) temperature is calculated on the basis of the estimated sensible heat load and total cooling-load of the concerned rooms. Then, this CHWR temperature is reset following up the ON and OFF states of the compressor to regulate the variation range of the CHWT. Thus, the cooling output of the AHU in its adjustable range can always follow up the changing of indoor cooling demand. This method is tested on a simulation platform and a real ACS. The test results show good temperature control performance can be achieved. The experimental results demonstrate that the maximum deviation of room temperature is reduced by 2◦C and the energy consumption of the GSHP and fan can be reduced by 30.12% at light cooling-load. The application performance shows this method can be easily realized in a common building automation system.
KW  - Ground-source heat pump
KW  - air-handling unit
KW  - Optimal control
KW  - air-conditioning system
KW  - room temperature
KW  - temperature control
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Small-scale ground-source heat pump (i.e., GSHP) integrated central air-conditioning systems (i.e., ACSs) are well suited and widely used in the small-scale and low-density residential buildings, such as villas, rural houses, or some small offices [1,2,3]. 
N1  - C2. This study presents an optimal control method for an ON/OFF controlled GSHP-integrated central air-conditioning system (i.e., ACS) so as to improve the control performance of the indoor thermal environment. 
N1  - C3. The chilled-water return temperature (i.e., CHWR) is optimized based on estimated cooling-load. It can regulate the cooling output capacity of the air-handling unit (AHU) following up the ON and OFF states of the GSHP to always satisfy the changing of the indoor cooling demand. 
N1  - C4. The control performance of this optimal control method was tested in the simulation platform of a GSHP-integrated central ACS. 
N1  - C5. The test results show that a better control robustness and accuracy of room temperature can be achieved by using the optimal control method in a typical summer day as well as in a typical spring day. 
N1  - C6. The results show that the system energy use can be reduced significantly at light load condition by using the optimal control method. In this test, the energy consumption was reduced by 30.12% in total of GSHP and fan. 
N1  - C7. The experiment results show that the optimal control method can obtain much better room temperature control performance when comparing with the basic control method. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The experimental results demonstrate that the maximum deviation of room temperature is reduced by 2◦C and the energy consumption of the GSHP and fan can be reduced by 30.12% at light cooling-load. 
N1  - D2. The total energy consumption of GSHP and fan can be reduced by 7.63% in the spring day. 
N1  - D3. The results also show that the system energy use can be reduced significantly at light load condition by using the optimal control method. In this test, the energy consumption was reduced by 30.12% in total of GSHP and fan. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. This study presents an optimal control method for an ON/OFF controlled ground-source heat pump-integrated central air-conditioning system so as to improve the control performance of the indoor thermal environment. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The chilled-water return temperature (i.e., CHWR) is optimized based on estimated cooling-load. It can regulate the cooling output capacity of the air-handling unit (AHU) following up the ON and OFF states of the GSHP to always satisfy the changing of the indoor cooling demand. 
N1  - E5. The control performance of this optimal control method was tested in the simulation platform of a GSHP-integrated central ACS. 
N1  - E6. The test results show that a better control robustness and accuracy of room temperature can be achieved by using the optimal control method in a typical summer day as well as in a typical spring day. 
N1  - E7. The results show that the system energy use can be reduced significantly at light load condition by using the optimal control method. In this test, the energy consumption was reduced by 30.12% in total of GSHP and fan. 
N1  - E8. The experiment results show that the optimal control method can obtain much better room temperature control performance when comparing with the basic control method. 
N1  - E9. Control Method. 
N1  - E10. The study proposes an optimal control method for small-scale ground-source heat pump (GSHP) integrated central air-conditioning systems (ACSs) to improve room temperature control performance [1,2,3].. 
N1  - E11. The method calculates the optimal chilled-water return (CHWR) temperature set-point based on the estimated sensible heat load and total cooling-load of the concerned rooms [4,5,6].. 
N1  - E12. This set-point is then reset to regulate the variation range of the chilled-water temperature (CHWT) following the ON and OFF states of the compressor.. 
N1  - E13. The optimal control algorithm is developed to improve the control robustness of the room temperature under different cooling-load conditions [10,12].. 
N1  - E14. The set-point of CHWR temperature Tw,set is optimized according to the room sensible heat load qsen and total cooling-load ql.. 
N1  - E15. The algorithm is driven by the change in the states of the GSHP ON and OFF, and the set-point of CHWR temperature Tw,set is calculated by Eq (4) [28,30].. 
N1  - E16. Energy Efficiency. 
N1  - E17. The proposed control method aims to improve energy efficiency by reducing the energy consumption of the GSHP and fan [7,8,9].. 
N1  - E18. The experimental results demonstrate that the maximum deviation of room temperature is reduced by 2°C and the energy consumption of the GSHP and fan can be reduced by 30.12% at light cooling-load [10,11].. 
N1  - E19. The method can be easily implemented in a common building automation system, making it a practical solution for improving energy efficiency [12].. 
N1  - E20. System Performance. 
N1  - E21. The study tests the proposed control method on a simulation platform and a real ACS, and the results show good temperature control performance [13,14].. 
N1  - E22. The control algorithm can handle the uncertainty, nonlinearity, and constraint of HVAC systems, making it a robust solution for improving indoor thermal environment [15,16].. 
N1  - E23. The method is beneficial for the discussion on the management of small-scale GSHP-integrated central ACSs, particularly in regions with hot summer and cold winter [17,18].. 
N1  - E24. System Operation. 
N1  - E25. The system operates with a single AHU, and the optimal control algorithm is based on the design cooling capacity of the GSHP-integrated ACS fulfilling the cooling-load of the rooms served by the AHU.. 
N1  - E26. The CHWR temperature is limited to a range (Tw,set− δTw/2, Tw,set+ δTw/2) to meet the indoor cooling demand [31].. 
N1  - E27. The supply-air flow rate is adjustable, and the range of supply-air flow rate is [M a, Ma], corresponding to the range of the fan frequency [15Hz, 50Hz].. 
N1  - E28. Simulation Results. 
N1  - E29. A simulation platform of a GSHP-integrated central ACS was developed to validate the control performance of the optimal control method [31,32].. 
N1  - E30. The results show that the optimal control method can track the room temperature robustly and accurately, with a mean absolute tracking error of 0.07◦C and a maximum deviation of 0.4◦C at steady state phase.. 
N1  - E31. The basic control method, which uses a fixed CHWR temperature set-point, results in significant oscillations and a higher mean absolute tracking error of 0.15◦C [10,12].. 
N1  - E32. Control Performance. 
N1  - E33. The optimal control method achieved good control robustness and accuracy, with an average absolute tracking error of 0.09◦C and a maximum deviation of 1.6◦C at steady state phase [Fig. 11].. 
N1  - E34. In contrast, the basic control method had an average absolute tracking error of 0.21◦C and a maximum deviation of 2.8◦C [Fig. 11].. 
N1  - E35. The optimal control method also performed well in a real GSHP-integrated ACS, with an average absolute tracking error of 0.09◦C and a maximum deviation of 0.3◦C, compared to 1.47◦C and 2.3◦C for the basic control method [Fig. 15].. 
N1  - E36. Energy Consumption. 
N1  - E37. The optimal control method reduced energy consumption, especially at light load conditions.. 
N1  - E38. In the spring day simulation, the total energy consumption of GSHP and fan decreased by 7.63% [Table 1].. 
N1  - E39. In the real ACS test, the system energy consumption was reduced by 30.12% [Table 4], with the GSHP energy consumption decreasing by 38.54% and the fan energy consumption increasing by 71.24%.. 
N1  - E40. Practical Application. 
N1  - E41. The optimal control method was implemented in a real GSHP-integrated ACS and showed good application performance [33].. 
N1  - E42. The calculation process of the algorithm is simple, and the procedure can be conveniently written in the common controller of a BAS with no or low cost addition for the development of software.. 
N1  - E43. The method can be used for air-handling units and can be implemented in BAS systems.. 
N1  - E44. Funding. 
N1  - E45. The work is financially supported by a grant (No 51178201) of National Science Foundation of China [1].. 
N1  - E46. Data. 
N1  - E47. Data will be made available on request [2].. 
N1  - E48. Conflicts. 
N1  - E49. The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper [3]. 
