AB  - Deep geothermal energy is a sustainable and renewable spacing heating source. Although many studies have discussed the design optimization of deep borehole systems, few have accomplished optimization and in-depth analysis of system operation control. In this study, an analytical model of the U-type deep borehole heat exchanger is proposed, and the average relative error between the simulated outlet temperatures and experimental data is −3.2%. Then, this paper presents an integrated model for the operation optimization study of the U-type deep-borehole ground source heat pump system. The optimal control of flow rate is adopted to match the variation in heating load. Compared with the constant-flow rate (110 m3/h) operation mode, the variable flow rate method reduces the power consumption of the heat pump and circulating pump by 22.1%, from 288,423 kW·h to 224,592 kW·h, during 2112 h of operation. In addition, the system has a larger RHS and COP when the thermal conductivity of the backfill material increases. When the borehole depth increases by 200 m from 2300 m, the energy consumption of the circulating pump will drop from 85,844 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h. The COP of the heat pump unit will decrease approximately linearly as the heating load increases, and the total power consumption will increase accordingly. This work can provide guidance for the design and optimization of U-shaped GSHP systems.
KW  - coefficient of performance
KW  - ground source heat pump
KW  - flow rate
KW  - geothermal energy
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Geothermal energy is an important renewable energy source for power generation [1], spacing cooling/heating [2] and some additional preheating processes [3]. 
N1  - C2. In scenarios where the thermal load of buildings significantly exceeds the cooling load, shallow ground source heat pump (GSHP) systems often struggle to meet long-term heating demands, and prolonged heat extraction leads to lowered soil temperatures, resulting in decreased operational efficiency [7]. 
N1  - C3. In the design process of ground source heat pump systems with buried pipes, the drilling depth is typically calculated under the premise of meeting the most unfavorable conditions. 
N1  - C4. This model comprehensively considers the dynamic heat load of terminal buildings and the dynamic heat exchange process on the ground source side. It adopts a variable-flow method to match the dynamic changes in building heat load, while reducing the energy consumption of the ground source loop pump. This enables the calculation of water flow based on known building heat load, achieving optimization of ground source heat pump system operation. 
N1  - C5. Circulating pump power consumption from 117,332 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h, resulting in a total reduction inpower powerconsumption consumptionfrom of 22.1%. 
N1  - C6. If the flow rate is greater than the maximum value, it is necessary to further reduce the outlet temperature of the heat pump, i.e., the inlet temperature of the BHE at7 the of 15next moment. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. In terms of the long-term operation and thermal recovery performance for U-type BHE, the results show that the thermal recovery rate of deep rock and soil is over 91.2% after an eight-month thermal recovery period [18]. 
N1  - D2. Compared with this, variable-flow operation reduces the power consumption of the heat pump unit from 171,090 kW·h to 168,043 kW·h, and the circulating pump power consumption from 117,332 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h, resulting in a total reduction in power consumption of 22.1%. 
N1  - D3. Circulating pump power consumption from 117,332 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h, resulting in a total reduction inpower powerconsumption consumptionfrom of 22.1%. 
N1  - D4. 98.8%, and the heat pump unit m, the average RHS increases from 95.7% to 98.8%, and the heat pump unit COP increases increases from. 
N1  - D5. 20%, during thethe simulation peperiod, the average heat extraction capacity of the ground loop heat exchanger decreases riod, the average heat extraction capacity of the ground loop heat exchanger decreases riod, the average heat extraction capacity of the ground loop heat exchanger decreases from 320 kW to 265 kW, a reduction of 17.2%. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. In the design process of ground source heat pump systems with buried pipes, the drilling depth is typically calculated under the premise of meeting the most unfavorable conditions. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. In scenarios where the thermal load of buildings significantly exceeds the cooling load, shallow ground source heat pump (GSHP) systems often struggle to meet long-term heating demands, and prolonged heat extraction leads to lowered soil temperatures, resulting in decreased operational efficiency [7]. 
N1  - E5. In the design process of ground source heat pump systems with buried pipes, the drilling depth is typically calculated under the premise of meeting the most unfavorable conditions. 
N1  - E6. This model comprehensively considers the dynamic heat load of terminal buildings and the dynamic heat exchange process on the ground source side. It adopts a variable-flow method to match the dynamic changes in building heat load, while reducing the energy consumption of the ground source loop pump. This enables the calculation of water flow based on known building heat load, achieving optimization of ground source heat pump system operation. 
N1  - E7. Circulating pump power consumption from 117,332 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h, resulting in a total reduction inpower powerconsumption consumptionfrom of 22.1%. 
N1  - E8. If the flow rate is greater than the maximum value, it is necessary to further reduce the outlet temperature of the heat pump, i.e., the inlet temperature of the BHE at7 the of 15next moment. 
N1  - E9. Introduction. 
N1  - E10. Deep geothermal energy is a sustainable and renewable spacing heating source [1,2,3].. 
N1  - E11. The traditional ground source heat pump (GSHP) systems typically utilize geothermal energy from depths shallower than 200 m [4].. 
N1  - E12. However, shallow GSHP systems encounter limitations due to their small temperature differentials, necessitating substantial land area and soil thermal balance maintenance [5,6].. 
N1  - E13. In comparison to shallow GSHP systems, mid-deep GSHP systems, with borehole depths typically ranging from 2000 to 3000 m, exhibit higher bottom temperatures, significantly enhancing their heat extraction capabilities [8,9].. 
N1  - E14. Methodology. 
N1  - E15. To understand the thermal performance of the U-type deep borehole heat exchanger (DBHE), this study regularizes the geometry of the U-shaped buried pipe heat exchanger [23].. 
N1  - E16. The heat transfer process between the circulating fluid inside the underground pipes and the surrounding rock and soil is complex, involving heat convection between the fluid and the pipe, heat conduction between the inner and outer walls of the pipe, heat conduction between the outer wall of the pipe and the backfill material, heat conduction between the backfill material and the borehole wall, and heat conduction between the borehole wall and the rock and soil [25,26,27,28].. 
N1  - E17. The underground heat transfer process of the deep U-shaped buried pipe heat exchanger is divided into two parts, inside and outside the borehole, and energy control equations for the rock and soil and the circulating fluid are established [29,30,31].. 
N1  - E18. Results. 
N1  - E19. The optimal control of flow rate is adopted to match the variation in heating load, reducing the power consumption of the heat pump and circulating pump by 22.1%, from 288,423 kW·h to 224,592 kW·h, during 2112 h of operation [16,17].. 
N1  - E20. The system has a larger RHS and COP when the thermal conductivity of the backfill material increases [18,19].. 
N1  - E21. When the borehole depth increases by 200 m from 2300 m, the energy consumption of the circulating pump will drop from 85,844 kW·h to 56,548 kW·h [20].. 
N1  - E22. The COP of the heat pump unit will decrease approximately linearly as the heating load increases, and the total power consumption will increase accordingly [21,22].. 
N1  - E23. Model Development. 
N1  - E24. The energy equation of the circulating fluid in the U-shaped pipe is expressed as a function of the temperature of the circulating fluid, the temperature of the borehole wall, the mass flow rate of the circulating fluid, and the thermal capacitance of materials per unit length inside the borehole [34].. 
N1  - E25. The fluid temperature at time k + 1 can be determined using the fluid temperature and the borehole wall temperature at time k.. 
N1  - E26. The heat flow along the borehole can be calculated, followed by calculating the wall temperature at time k + 1 using Equations (1)–(3).. 
N1  - E27. Simulation And Validation. 
N1  - E28. The mathematical model was validated by comparing simulation results with experimental measurements reported in the literature [34].. 
N1  - E29. The experimental data were sourced from Li et al [34], derived on 28 February 2017, starting at 10:00 and concluding after 72 h.. 
N1  - E30. The inlet water temperature was maintained at 19.5 °C with a flow rate of 46.5 m3/h.. 
N1  - E31. The simulation results stabilized and exhibited consistent trends with experimental data, showing good agreement with relative errors ranging from −4% to −2%, and the average value of relative error was −3.2%.. 
N1  - E32. Variable-Flow Operation. 
N1  - E33. A variable-flow operation model for medium-depth U-type ground source heat pump systems was proposed, considering the dynamic heat load of terminal buildings and the dynamic heat exchange process on the ground source side [35, 36, 37].. 
N1  - E34. The model adopts a variable-flow method to match the dynamic changes in building heat load, reducing the energy consumption of the ground source loop pump.. 
N1  - E35. The simulation results showed that the variable-flow operation reduces the power consumption of the heat pump unit and the circulating pump power consumption, resulting in a total reduction in power consumption of 22.1% [35].. 
N1  - E36. The thermal conductivity of the backfill material was set to 1.0, 2.0, and 3.0 W/(m·K), and the results showed that as the thermal conductivity of the backfill material decreases, the occurrence time of insufficient heating advances, and the minimum value of the RHS also decreases [34].. 
N1  - E37. System Performance. 
N1  - E38. Increasing the drilling depth is beneficial for improving the heat extraction of the system [1].. 
N1  - E39. When the pipe length increases, the average RHS increases from 95.7% to 98.8%, and the heat pump unit COP increases from 4.929 to 5.135 [2].. 
N1  - E40. Increasing the length of the vertical pipe segment enhances the heat extraction capacity of the ground loop, reducing the circulation flow rate and power consumption of the circulation pump [3].. 
N1  - E41. Heat Load. 
N1  - E42. As the building heat load decreases, the heat extraction capacity of the ground loop heat exchanger decreases, and the heating guarantee rate increases to a certain extent [4].. 
N1  - E43. When the building heat load increases, the COP of the heat pump unit gradually decreases, while the energy consumption of the heat pump and circulation pump gradually increases [5].. 
N1  - E44. The demand for heat extraction from the ground loop heat exchanger increases, causing the surrounding rock and soil temperature to decrease more rapidly, resulting in a lower outlet temperature of the circulating fluid and consequently decreasing the COP of the unit [6].. 
N1  - E45. Modeling And Optimization. 
N1  - E46. An analytical solution model for the heat transfer analysis of U-shaped ground heat exchangers is proposed and validated by experimental data [7].. 
N1  - E47. A variable-flow operation model is established for the operation optimization research of ground source heat pump systems with known building thermal loads [8].. 
N1  - E48. The model shows that operating the system with variable fluid flow can reduce energy consumption, and increasing the thermal conductivity of the backfill materials enhances the underground heat transfer, increasing the RHS and COP of the system [9]. 
