AB  - E-mail address: hatef.madani@energy.kth.se (H. Madani). 1 Hatef Madani is a Website manager and contact person of IIR commission E2. 2 Per Lundqvist is the President of IIR commission E2. 0140-7007/$ e see front matter a 2011 Elsevier Ltd and IIR.
KW  - Heat pump
KW  - Control
KW  - Modeling
KW  - heat source
KW  - storage tank
KW  - Simulation
KW  - TRNSYS
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. In order to reach the full thermodynamic potential of heat pumps systems an appropriate capacity control technique must be developed and adjusted for each unique installation. 
N1  - C2. This paper describes the development of a generic model of a ground source heat pump system, covering the heat pump unit, the building, the ground as heat source, a thermal storage tank. 
N1  - C3. The developed computer model in the present study aims at facilitating a reasonable accurate comparative analysis of the seasonal performance of different control strategies or different system layouts by finding the balance between complexity of models and possible simplifications; so HP unit, heat source and heat sink are all modeled in detail and the models are located in the upper right corner of Zone A of Fig. 1, somewhere close to the crossing of the dashed lines. 
N1  - C4. By using the model developed in the present study, it is possible to compare some of methods and strategies used for capacity control of a heat pump system such as using a variable speed compressor and/or variable speed pumps in the HP system. 
N1  - C5. The present paper, as the first part of two, suggests a generic method to approach the challenge of further understanding capacity control in a ground source heat pump. 
N1  - C6. The paper describes the development of a computer model of ground source heat pump, covering the heat pump unit, the building, the heat source, a thermal storage tank, and the climate, in order to analyze existing or new control methods or strategies in the HP system. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. As may be seen in Table 2, the HP unit model is able to predict the evaporation and condensation temperatures fairly accurate, as well as the heating capacity, compressor power and COP of the unit. It is shown in Figs. 5 and 6 that the calculated COP and heating capacity by the developed HP unit model have less than 15% deviation from the measurements. 
N1  - D2. Furthermore, the calculated compressor power has less than 10% deviation from the measurements, as it is presented in Fig. 7. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The present paper, as the first part of two, suggests a generic method to approach the challenge of further understanding capacity control in a ground source heat pump. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. This paper describes the development of a generic model of a ground source heat pump system, covering the heat pump unit, the building, the ground as heat source, a thermal storage tank. 
N1  - E5. The developed computer model in the present study aims at facilitating a reasonable accurate comparative analysis of the seasonal performance of different control strategies or different system layouts by finding the balance between complexity of models and possible simplifications; so HP unit, heat source and heat sink are all modeled in detail and the models are located in the upper right corner of Zone A of Fig. 1, somewhere close to the crossing of the dashed lines. 
N1  - E6. By using the model developed in the present study, it is possible to compare some of methods and strategies used for capacity control of a heat pump system such as using a variable speed compressor and/or variable speed pumps in the HP system. 
N1  - E7. The present paper, as the first part of two, suggests a generic method to approach the challenge of further understanding capacity control in a ground source heat pump. 
N1  - E8. The paper describes the development of a computer model of ground source heat pump, covering the heat pump unit, the building, the heat source, a thermal storage tank, and the climate, in order to analyze existing or new control methods or strategies in the HP system. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. If the goal of the study is to investigate how the entire system and the components behave for a wide variety of boundary conditions or how a new technology, introduced in one of the sub-systems affect the other systems and the system as a whole, we may have to develop a model where all the models included are fairly detailed. 
N1  - E11. Introduction To Capacity Control. 
N1  - E12. The challenge of capacity control in Ground Source Heat Pumps (GSHPs) is complex due to the dynamic interaction among the components of the heat pump system (Neale et al, 1981; Lida, 1982; Wang and Wilson, 1983; Halozan, 1988; Zubair and Bahel, 1989; Sheldrake, 1991; Qureshi and Tassou, 1996; Vargas and Parise, 1995; Qureshi, 1998; Claesson and Forsen, 2002; Zhao et al, 2003; Karlsson, 2007; Parnitzki, 2007; Madani et al, 2008).. 
N1  - E13. Capacity control can be divided into three main categories: capacity control by components, cycle design for capacity control, and capacity control by advanced control algorithms.. 
N1  - E14. Modeling And Simulation. 
N1  - E15. A generic model of a ground source heat pump system has been developed, covering the heat pump unit, the building, the ground as heat source, and a thermal storage tank (Wimmer and Shafai, 1999; Bianchi et al, 2005; Sakellari et al, 2006).. 
N1  - E16. The model takes the local climate into consideration and aims to facilitate a comparative analysis between the annual performance of the systems with a variety of capacity control strategies.. 
N1  - E17. The model is developed in the two environments EES and TRNSYS utilizing a co-solving technique.. 
N1  - E18. Capacity Control Techniques. 
N1  - E19. The model can be used to evaluate on/off controlled and variable capacity GSHPs, with a single speed or variable speed pumps in the systems, under a wide range of operating conditions (He, 1996; Granryd, 2007; Karlsson and Fahlen, 2007; Karlsson and Fahlen, 2008; Finn et al, 2008).. 
N1  - E20. The study aims to investigate how different capacity control techniques affect the seasonal performance of the system, including the influence of the auxiliary electrical heater, the relative sizing of the heat pump unit capacity, and variations in the mass flow rate of brine in the ground heat exchanger and water in the heating system.. 
N1  - E21. Modeling Approach. 
N1  - E22. The study uses a bottom-up approach to develop semi-empirical sub-models of heat pump unit components, including the compressor, condenser, evaporator, and expansion valve (Bourdouxhe et al, 1994; Cuevas et al, 2010).. 
N1  - E23. The individual sub-models are linked together to form the model of the heat pump unit.. 
N1  - E24. The modeling procedure involves developing models of the heat source, heat sink, climate, and thermal storage tank, and then linking all the sub-models together to model the heat pump system as a whole.. 
N1  - E25. Component Modeling. 
N1  - E26. The compressor model includes the electromechanical losses, built-in volume ratio, and inverter loss (Chen and Halm, 2000; Winandy et al, 2002).. 
N1  - E27. The condenser model estimates the heat transfer coefficient correlations for desuperheating, condensation, and subcooling sections (Claesson, 2005a).. 
N1  - E28. The evaporator model considers both evaporation and superheating portions of the heat transfer surface (Claesson, 2005b; Longo and Gasparella, 2007).. 
N1  - E29. The expansion valve model assumes a constant superheat temperature through an isenthalpic expansion process (Madani et al, 2011).. 
N1  - E30. System Modeling. 
N1  - E31. The heat pump system model combines the individual sub-models and processes input data such as frequency, approach heat sink temperature, and approach heat source temperature (Klein et al, 2005; Klein, 2003).. 
N1  - E32. The model outputs include evaporation temperature, condensation temperature, heating capacity, compressor power, and COP heat.. 
N1  - E33. The study also models a single-family house and calculates the heating load based on parameters such as heat losses, infiltration losses, ventilation load, internal gains, and solar gain (Acuna et al, 2009; Jonasson, 2009).. 
N1  - E34. Model Development. 
N1  - E35. The heat pump unit model is developed using semi-empirical models of its components, including compressor, evaporator, condenser, and expansion valve, based on a limited set of experimental data (Qureshi and Tassou, 1996; Qureshi, 1998).. 
N1  - E36. The model is then validated with another set of experimental data from two variable speed GSHP systems in Stockholm, Sweden.. 
N1  - E37. The validation results show that the model can predict the evaporation and condensation temperatures, heating capacity, compressor power, and COP of the unit with reasonable accuracy (Madani et al., 2011).. 
N1  - E38. Validation And Accuracy. 
N1  - E39. The Root Mean Square errors for selected outputs from the HP unit model are presented in Table 2, showing that the model can predict the evaporation and condensation temperatures, as well as the heating capacity, compressor power, and COP of the unit with less than 15% deviation from the measurements (Voit et al, 1994; Acuna et al, 2009).. 
N1  - E40. The calculated compressor power has less than 10% deviation from the measurements.. 
N1  - E41. Application And Context. 
N1  - E42. The developed model is part of a National Research and Development Program in Sweden called “Effsysþ6”, a four-year program for applied R&amp;D in Refrigeration and Heat Pump Technology (Klein et al, 2005).. 
N1  - E43. The model can be used to compare different methods and strategies for capacity control of a heat pump system, such as using a variable speed compressor and/or variable speed pumps in the HP system (Parnitzki, 2007; Sakellari et al, 2006). 
