TI  - A quasi-steady state mathematical model of an integrated ground source heat pump for building space control
T2  - Energy and Buildings
VL  - 43
PY  - 2010
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2010
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.enbuild.2010.08.017
AB  - This paper is concerned with the development of a mathematical model, capable of describing the quasisteady state performance of an integrated ground source heat pump, which is used for heating and cooling of an institutional building located in a Mediterranean climate. The model is structured on functional basis according to the heat pump vapour compression or primary circuit, a secondary ground loop circuit and a secondary building loop circuit. Heat pump heating and cooling capacities, as well as COP, are considered to be dependent variables and are estimated in the model using performance fitted maps. Independent variables include: compressor speed, circulation pump speeds, ground loop return temperature and building circuit return temperature. The model is validated using data from a full-scale ground source heat pump installation. The validated model is used to examine system capacity and performance sensitivity to different control optimisation strategies, including set-point control of room air temperature, room air bandwidth temperature, building loop return water temperature and building loop return bandwidth temperature.
KW  - Ground source heat pump
KW  - Mathematical model
KW  - Engineering Equation Solver
KW  - steady state
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Research concerning control issues related to vapour compression systems has largely focused on refrigeration and air-conditioning and to a lesser extent on heat pumps. 
N1  - C2. This paper examines, for a ground source heat pump (GSHP), how control of building circuit variables affects overall system performance. 
N1  - C3. A system level mathematical model was developed from the GSHP heat pump system using Engineering Equation Solver (EES) [17]. 
N1  - C4. The vapour compression software package IMST-ART was used to model the behaviour of the GSHP as a standalone system [18]. 
N1  - C5. This paper examines, for ground source heat pumps, how control of building circuit variables affects overall system performance. 
N1  - C6. It would appear that higher water return temperatures reduces the ON time operation of the heat pump, and results in an increase of daily heat pump seasonal performance factor (SPF) of approximately 5% per each degree increase of the building water return temperature. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. As can be observed on the right hand axes of Figs. 3–5, experimental measurements for cooling capacity and power input are predicted by the polynomial correlations with a maximum mean deviation better than 5% for cooling capacity and 2.4% for power input. 
N1  - D2. Analysis shows that capacity increases by approximately 3.6% per degree increase of the building return water temperature. 
N1  - D3. Values for the SPF of the system and the heat pump are affected by less than 0.5% by return water temperature bandwidth. 
N1  - D4. Values for the SPF of the system and the heat pump are slightly affected, less than 1%, by space temperature bandwidth. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. This paper examines, for ground source heat pumps, how control of building circuit variables affects overall system performance. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. This paper examines, for a ground source heat pump (GSHP), how control of building circuit variables affects overall system performance. 
N1  - E5. A system level mathematical model was developed from the GSHP heat pump system using Engineering Equation Solver (EES) [17]. 
N1  - E6. The vapour compression software package IMST-ART was used to model the behaviour of the GSHP as a standalone system [18]. 
N1  - E7. This paper examines, for ground source heat pumps, how control of building circuit variables affects overall system performance. 
N1  - E8. It would appear that higher water return temperatures reduces the ON time operation of the heat pump, and results in an increase of daily heat pump seasonal performance factor (SPF) of approximately 5% per each degree increase of the building water return temperature. 
N1  - E9. Model Development. 
N1  - E10. The paper presents a quasi-steady state mathematical model of an integrated ground source heat pump for building space control [1,2,3].. 
N1  - E11. The model is structured on a functional basis, including the heat pump vapour compression or primary circuit, a secondary ground loop circuit, and a secondary building loop circuit.. 
N1  - E12. Heat pump heating and cooling capacities, as well as COP, are considered dependent variables and are estimated in the model using performance fitted maps [4,5].. 
N1  - E13. Independent variables include compressor speed, circulation pump speeds, ground loop return temperature, and building circuit return temperature.. 
N1  - E14. The IMST-ART software package was used to develop a performance map for a heat pump system, describing the sensitivity of heat pump capacity and power consumption to variations in external and internal water mass flow rates and return temperatures [19, 20].. 
N1  - E15. The performance map was developed for five different pump speeds and both heating and cooling modes.. 
N1  - E16. Polynomial correlations were established for heating and cooling modes, with regression values R2 equal to or better than 0.998.. 
N1  - E17. Control Strategies. 
N1  - E18. Research concerning control issues related to vapour compression systems has largely focused on refrigeration and air-conditioning, and to a lesser extent on heat pumps [6,7].. 
N1  - E19. The most common type of capacity control currently deployed in commercial ground source heat pumps appears to be ON/OFF compressor cycling [2].. 
N1  - E20. Other studies have examined variable-speed control, intermittent control, and supplemental heating [10,11].. 
N1  - E21. The validated model is used to examine system capacity and performance sensitivity with different control strategies, including set-point control of room air temperature, room air temperature bandwidth, building loop return water temperature, and building loop return temperature bandwidth [12,13,14].. 
N1  - E22. System Performance. 
N1  - E23. The GSHP system utilized in this research is located in an institutional building at the Universidad Politécnica de Valencia, Valencia, Spain [16].. 
N1  - E24. The overall heat pump system consists of a heat pump, an indoor circuit, and an outdoor circuit.. 
N1  - E25. The heat pump is a water-to-water reversible GSHP, which uses propane (R290) as its primary refrigerant [17].. 
N1  - E26. The system level mathematical model was developed from the GSHP heat pump system using Engineering Equation Solver (EES) [17].. 
N1  - E27. The model was validated against data from the full UPV GSHP system, and sensitivity studies were conducted to examine system performance with different control strategies [18].. 
N1  - E28. The system performance of a ground source heat pump is affected by various control variables, including building hydronic circuit set-point temperature, building circuit hydronic temperature bandwidth, building space set-point temperature, and building space temperature bandwidth [1].. 
N1  - E29. The dominant factor affecting system power consumption is the building space set-point temperature, which directly affects heat gains to the building and the cooling load [2].. 
N1  - E30. As the set-point temperature increases from 21 to 25 ◦C, the daily system power consumption decreases significantly from 67.54 to 48.23 kWh [3].. 
N1  - E31. System Components. 
N1  - E32. The system mathematical model incorporated each of the system components, including fan coils, circulation pumps, hydronic pipe network, storage tank, and building conditioned space.. 
N1  - E33. Experimental characterization of the fan coil units was carried out to establish performance maps, and empirical polynomial correlations were used to map the performance of the internal and external pumps.. 
N1  - E34. The hydronic pipe network was modeled using the Lax–Wendroff model for 2D conduction transport [21].. 
N1  - E35. Validation And Results. 
N1  - E36. Validation of the system model was undertaken by comparing model predictions and experimental data taken from the UPV GSHP installation.. 
N1  - E37. The results showed that the model predictions were in good agreement with the experimental data, with a maximum mean deviation of approximately 5% for cooling capacity and 2.4% for power input [17].. 
N1  - E38. However, a maximum mean deviation of approximately 13.8% was observed for heating mode, which was attributed to transient conditions at start-up.. 
N1  - E39. Control Variables. 
N1  - E40. The effect of varying building return water temperature has less influence than space temperature set-point on system power consumption [4].. 
N1  - E41. As building water set-point is increased from 10 to 14 ◦C, compressor power consumption decreases from 30.19 to 27.07 kWh, while compressor ON time is also observed to decrease from 42.33 to 37.13% [5].. 
N1  - E42. Building water return bandwidth has a negligible effect on system and compressor power consumption, and daily SPF values for the system and the heat pump are not significantly affected by water return bandwidth [6].. 
N1  - E43. Space temperature bandwidth also has a negligible effect on compressor and system performance [7].. 
N1  - E44. Energy Consumption. 
N1  - E45. The daily SPF of the heat pump remains practically constant, whereas the daily SPF of the system decreases as the space set-point temperature increases from 21 to 25 ◦C [8].. 
N1  - E46. The total system power consumption decreases as the space set-point temperature increases, due to the decreased compressor ON time and its associated power requirement [10].. 
N1  - E47. Factors. 
N1  - E48. The current paper did not explore the influence of ground loop design [1], building hydronic loop design [2], and building efficiency [3] on overall system performance.. 
N1  - E49. Funding. 
N1  - E50. This work was supported under the FP7 programme “Advanced ground source heat pump systems for heating and cooling in Mediterranean climates” (GROUND-MED FP7-ENERGY-2007-2TREN-218895) [no citation marker in original text]. 
