AU  - Noyé, Sarah 
AU  - Mulero, Rubén 
AU  - Carnieletto, Laura 
AU  - De Carli, Michele 
AU  - Castelruiz, Amaia 
TI  - A review of advanced ground source heat pump control: Artificial intelligence for autonomous and adaptive control
T2  - Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews
VL  - 153
PY  - 2021
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2021
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.rser.2021.111685
AB  - Geothermal energy has the potential to contribute significantly to the CO2 reduction targets as a renewable source for building heating and cooling but is yet under exploited, mostly due to its high initial investment cost. A lot of research is being carried out to optimise Ground Source Heat Pump (GSHP) systems' design, but a good control strategy is also fundamental to achieve long-term performance and reduced payback time. GSHP control optimisation is a non-linear dynamic optimisation problem that is influenced by multiple parameters. It can thus not be fully optimised with traditional methods. Artificial Intelligence, and in particular Machine Learning, is suited for this type of optimisation as it can learn implicit relations between parameters and can address non-linearity. This paper reviews the challenges of GSHP control and the strategies for control optimisation found in the literature, from basic rule-based system to artificial neural network-based strategies. Two principal uses of Artificial Intelligence for ground source heat pump control are identified: building a predictive model of the system that reflects its real performances and optimising the control decision in real time. However, the examples found in the literature are limited and the need to further explore the benefits of Machine Learning is identified. The latest developments in the field are reviewed to explore their potential to further improve GSHP control. The challenges of the full implementation of such algorithms are also discussed.
KW  - Control
KW  - Heat Pump
KW  - Ground Source Heat Pumps
KW  - Machine learning
KW  - artificial intelligence
KW  - Reinforcement Learning
KW  - Artificial Neural Networks
KW  - Deep Learning
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. In Europe, all new buildings are required to be Nearly Zero Energy Buildings since 2020, roadmaps are implemented to convert existing buildings to nZEBs [1], and the trend is towards positive energy buildings [2]. nZEB, having high energy performance and sourcing the little energy they require mostly from renewable energies, are necessary to achieve the 2050 CO2 reduction targets.Geothermal energy is a key source of renewable energy for nZEBs, but is still largely underexploited [3]. 
N1  - C2. When focusing on Artificial Intelligence (AI) for Ground Source Heat Pumps (GSHP) control, this paper identifies three applications, which are reviewed : (1) parameter optimisation; (2) system modelling; (3) control optimisation. 
N1  - C3. This paper reviews the state of the art of GSHP control and the potential of Artificial Intelligence to reduce operating cost and improve energy efficiency. 
N1  - C4. GSHPs are complex system and require controls that can adapt to their operating environment so that maximum performance can be achieved, making online optimisation better adapted for their control. 
N1  - C5. The field of Artificial Intelligence provides algorithms that can learn complex patterns from data and work with a great number of parameters without the need of explicit knowledge to link them. These algorithms can provide autonomous and adaptive control to better control GSHP for energy, comfort and cost optimisation, but long-term performance need to be better included in their learning objectives. 
N1  - C6. The focus in the literature so far has been on using AI to build predictive models of GSHP systems. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. Yang et al used RL agents trained on a Simulink model to control an HGSHP system with PVT panels. They first used a RL controller for the PVT system, leading to an increase in performance of 10% compared to the rule-based alternative after 3 years. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. Ground Source Heat Pumps are complex system and require controls that can adapt to their operating environment so that maximum performance can be achieved, making online optimisation better adapted for their control. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. When focusing on Artificial Intelligence (AI) for Ground Source Heat Pumps (GSHP) control, this paper identifies three applications, which are reviewed : (1) parameter optimisation; (2) system modelling; (3) control optimisation. 
N1  - E5. This paper reviews the state of the art of GSHP control and the potential of Artificial Intelligence to reduce operating cost and improve energy efficiency. 
N1  - E6. GSHPs are complex system and require controls that can adapt to their operating environment so that maximum performance can be achieved, making online optimisation better adapted for their control. 
N1  - E7. The field of Artificial Intelligence provides algorithms that can learn complex patterns from data and work with a great number of parameters without the need of explicit knowledge to link them. These algorithms can provide autonomous and adaptive control to better control GSHP for energy, comfort and cost optimisation, but long-term performance need to be better included in their learning objectives. 
N1  - E8. The focus in the literature so far has been on using AI to build predictive models of GSHP systems. 
N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. A lot of studies focus either on cooling or heating, not considering the full-year performance and even fewer studies look at performances beyond a year, unless the main objective of the study is to compensate for load imbalance. 
N1  - E11. GSHP Control. 
N1  - E12. GSHP control optimisation is a non-linear dynamic optimisation problem that is influenced by multiple parameters, and Artificial Intelligence, particularly Machine Learning, is suited for this type of optimisation [1].. 
N1  - E13. The challenges of GSHP control and the strategies for control optimisation found in the literature are reviewed, from basic rule-based systems to artificial neural network-based strategies.. 
N1  - E14. Two principal uses of Artificial Intelligence for ground source heat pump control are identified: building a predictive model of the system that reflects its real performances and optimising the control decision in real time [2].. 
N1  - E15. AI Applications. 
N1  - E16. The field of Artificial Intelligence provides algorithms that can learn complex patterns from data and work with a great number of parameters without the need of explicit knowledge to link them [3].. 
N1  - E17. These algorithms can provide autonomous and adaptive control to better control GSHP for energy, comfort and cost optimisation.. 
N1  - E18. The focus in the literature so far has been on using AI to build predictive models of GSHP systems.. 
N1  - E19. The progress on deep learning could further improve predictive tasks when large datasets are available.. 
N1  - E20. Future Research. 
N1  - E21. Examples of artificial intelligence applied to GSHPs have been limited to date, and further research is needed to streamline the transition between virtual and real environments [4].. 
N1  - E22. Metaheuristic algorithms have been used with success, and progress in the field of deep reinforcement learning are promising.. 
N1  - E23. The literature shows the relevance of optimising GSHP controls to reduce costs and increase energy efficiency, which in turn can facilitate a greater market penetration of geothermal energy [13]. 
