N1  - E9. Introduction. 
N1  - E10. The PhD dissertation focuses on the control and energy optimization of an experimental ground source heat pump (GSHP) system installed at the Universitat Politècnica de València [16].. 
N1  - E11. The system was built in the framework of the GEOCOOL project, which aimed to develop a commercial-size, economic, energy-efficient, and environmentally friendly GSHP system for cooling and heating [16].. 
N1  - E12. The dissertation proposes, describes, develops, implements, and evaluates a new integrated optimization strategy to make the GSHP system operate at its optimal point.. 
N1  - E13. Control Strategies. 
N1  - E14. Different control strategies have been proposed and tested in the system, including the management of air flow in the fan coil, water mass flow in the internal and external hydraulic systems, and setpoint temperature in the heat pump [46].. 
N1  - E15. Pardo et al. tested a control strategy that estimated energy savings of around 30% [46].. 
N1  - E16. Del Col et al. developed an integrated control strategy that included the variation of compressor speed, water pumps speed, fan coils speed, and heat pump temperature setpoint [48, 49].. 
N1  - E17. The dissertation proposes a new integrated optimization strategy that has been tested in situ in the experimental GSHP installation, obtaining significant energy savings of up to 35% in the summer season and 53% in the winter season [51].. 
N1  - E18. The temperature compensation strategy developed in this research work helps to save energy by varying the temperature setpoint according to the ambient temperature [no specific citation].. 
N1  - E19. The strategy is combined with another optimization strategy consisting of varying the frequency of the water circulation pumps as a function of the thermal load of the building [no specific citation].. 
N1  - E20. Energy Optimization. 
N1  - E21. The energy optimization analysis was carried out by comparing the optimized control to a standard control, which consisted of a temperature setpoint of 7°C (45°C for heating mode) and 50 Hz of frequency for both circulation pumps.. 
N1  - E22. The results showed that the optimized control achieved significant energy savings while keeping the user comfort [71].. 
N1  - E23. The performance of the system was thoroughly analyzed through the study of typical days of operation for both cooling and heating modes, and along several years of operation.. 
N1  - E24. The study included the analysis of the power consumption of each component and their contribution to the total energy consumption, the heat generated, the operating temperatures, and the energy efficiency parameters [13, 14, 15].. 
N1  - E25. Optimization. 
N1  - E26. The energy optimization methodology developed in this PhD work improved the seasonal performance factor of the system by 35% in cooling mode and 53% in heating mode [no specific citation].. 
N1  - E27. The methodology is applicable to any other ground source heat pump installations of the same type.. 
N1  - E28. The standard control considered in the first approach was more restrictive due to the requirements of the GROUND-MED project [no specific citation].. 
N1  - E29. The energy optimization algorithm consists of calculating the internal circulation pump frequency as a function of the ambient temperature and the partial load ratio, then determining the supply building temperature to meet user comfort [3].. 
N1  - E30. If the calculated building supply temperature is out of the feasible range, it is set to the limit value and the internal frequency is corrected to meet user comfort [4].. 
N1  - E31. System Design. 
N1  - E32. The sizing of the buffer tank volume is crucial, and several criteria were considered, including the minimum OFF time, minimum ON time, and maximum number of starts per hour of the compressor [no specific citation].. 
N1  - E33. The most restrictive approach appears to be the minimum OFF time criterion, but this may not be important for scroll compressors [no specific citation].. 
N1  - E34. The minimum volume will be provided by the worst-case compressor time, and Eq 3.22 provides the final design guideline recommended by the authors [no specific citation].. 
N1  - E35. Control Strategy. 
N1  - E36. The new optimized control strategy was implemented and programmed in the control board of the system, with the main target of meeting user comfort while saving as much energy as possible [71].. 
N1  - E37. The strategy was tested during the cooling season (May-October 2015) and the heating season (December 2015 - February 2016).. 
N1  - E38. The results showed that the building supply temperature did not reach the limit value of 45◦C at any time, and the users' thermal comfort was maintained.. 
N1  - E39. Energy Savings. 
N1  - E40. The optimized control was compared to a standard control, and the results showed an improvement in the system performance factor (DPF) [71].. 
N1  - E41. The alternation of standard and optimized days can be clearly observed in Fig. 3.55, with an improvement of 33% in the cooling mode and a similar improvement in the heating mode.. 
N1  - E42. The Seasonal Performance Factor (SPF) was also calculated, showing an improvement of 35% in the cooling mode and 53% in the heating mode.. 
N1  - E43. User Comfort. 
N1  - E44. The comfort conditions were defined according to Spanish regulations [71], with acceptable ranges of indoor temperature (T) and relative humidity (RH).. 
N1  - E45. The results showed that the indoor temperature and relative humidity were within the comfort range when the new optimized control algorithm was working, with values of T = [24 − 25]◦C and RH = 50%.. 
N1  - E46. No complaints were received from the users regarding their thermal comfort when the optimized algorithm was used, and the comfort conditions were always met.. 
N1  - E47. Methodology. 
N1  - E48. The methodology developed to obtain optimal performance was reviewed and upgraded, based on coupling the estimation of the optimal values of the internal circulation pump frequency and the building supply temperature to minimize energy consumption [1].. 
N1  - E49. The values of both controlled variables are calculated as a function of the ambient temperature and the partial load ratio of the system [2].. 
N1  - E50. Results. 
N1  - E51. The new optimized control was tested during one whole cooling season and the current heating season, resulting in an improvement in the seasonal performance factor of the system of 35% in cooling mode and 53% in heating mode [5].. 
N1  - E52. The feedback from users about comfort has been positive, with measured values of temperature and relative humidity within the acceptable comfort range [6]. 
