N1  - E9. Objectives. 
N1  - E10. This paper aims to provide recent progress and advances in the research work carried out on design optimization and optimal control of GSHP systems. 
N1  - E11. Optimization. 
N1  - E12. The development of an optimization strategy for GSHP systems involves defining appropriate optimization objectives, identifying key variables, formulating the optimization problem, and solving it using an appropriate method [1].. 
N1  - E13. Sensitivity analysis is often used for dimension reduction to determine the major design parameters that significantly influence the performance of the GSHP system [31].. 
N1  - E14. The control optimization problem for GSHP systems aims to minimize system power consumption or operational cost by optimizing operating variables such as the operating number of heat pumps and temperature settings [13].. 
N1  - E15. Design Methods. 
N1  - E16. Design optimization problems for GSHP systems can be formulated in different ways, including rule-of-thumb methods and model-based approaches [42, 43].. 
N1  - E17. Rule-of-thumb methods are simple and easy to implement but may not provide optimal results, while model-based methods rely on component or system models for performance prediction and can offer a more reliable solution [21].. 
N1  - E18. Model-based methods often require a good understanding of system dynamics and behaviors, as well as a set of performance data for model training and parameter identification [44].. 
N1  - E19. Control Strategies. 
N1  - E20. Optimal control can provide better operating performance of GSHP systems compared to rule-based control methods [92, 93].. 
N1  - E21. Model predictive control can play an essential role in renewable integrated GSHP systems, and nonlinear model predictive control has been shown to exhibit better performance than other strategies [93].. 
N1  - E22. However, studies on optimal control of GSHP systems are insufficient, and the development of energy-efficient control strategies and evaluation of their control reliability, effectiveness, and long-term performance are needed [1]. 
