TI  - Second-order Multipole Formulas for Thermal Resistance of Single U-tube Borehole Heat Exchangers
T2  - 
VL  - 
PY  - 2017
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2017
UR  - 
DO  - 10.22488/okstate.17.000535
AB  - The borehole thermal resistance is both an important design parameter and a key performance characteristic of a borehole heat exchanger.Another quantity that is particularly important for deep borehole heat exchangers is the internal thermal resistance between the upward-flowing and downward-flowing fluid channels in the borehole.The multipole method is a well-known and robust method to compute both these thermal resistances.However, it has a fairly intricate mathematical algorithm and is thus not trivial to implement.Consequently, there is considerable interest in developing explicit multipole formulas.So far zeroth-order and first-order multipole formulas have been derived for cases where the two legs of the borehole are placed symmetrically in a borehole.This paper presents new explicit second-order multipole formulas, which provide significant accuracy improvements over the previous formulas. INTRODUCTION, SCOPE AND RATIONALECompared to ambient air, ground, in general, is a far superior source or sink of thermal energy because of its relatively stable temperature levels over the year.Since the turn of this century, the use of heating and cooling systems utilizing the ground as a heat source or a heat sink has grown at a remarkable rate (Lund and Boyd, 2016), stimulated by energy prices, technology advances and environmental concerns.A typical ground source heating or cooling system consists of a heat pump, a ground heat exchanger, and auxiliary systems for storage and distribution of thermal energy.The ground heat exchanger can be of open or closed type.In an open system groundwater is directly used as the heat carrier fluid, whereas in a closed system the heat carrier fluid is circulated in a closed loop, which can be horizontal or vertical.Various heat exchanger configurations can be used in closed-loop vertical systems, including single or double U-tubes, and simple or complex coaxial pipes.Among all types, a borehole heat exchanger with a single U-tube is by far the most commonly used ground heat exchanger in practice because of its low cost, small space requirements, and ease of installation.The scope of this paper is also limited to the application of single U-tubes in borehole heat exchangers.Ground thermal conductivity (λ) and borehole thermal resistance (Rb) are the two principal parameters that govern the heat transfer mechanism of a borehole heat exchanger.The heat transfer outside the borehole boundary is dictated by the thermal conductivity of the ground, whereas the heat transfer inside the borehole is characterized by the borehole thermal resistance between the heat carrier fluid and the borehole wall.A high ground thermal conductivity is beneficial for the ground heat transfer.However, being an intrinsic property of ground, the ground thermal conductivity cannot be controlled in practice.On the other hand, a low borehole thermal resistance is desirable for better heat transfer inside the borehole.The borehole thermal resistance depends upon the physical arrangement and the thermal properties of borehole components including grouting, ground heat exchanger, and the heat carrier fluid.Its value can be engineered to a certain extent by optimizing the geometry and layout of the ground heat exchanger and by choosing appropriate materials for the borehole components.
KW  - formulas
KW  - U tube
KW  - borehole heat exchanger
KW  - Ground heat exchanger
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. INTRODUCTION, SCOPE AND RATIONALECompared to ambient air, ground, in general, is a far superior source or sink of thermal energy because of its relatively stable temperature levels over the year. 
N1  - C2. The second-order multipole formulas for borehole thermal resistance and total internal thermal resistance are compared with previously published results. 
N1  - C3. The presented formulas can be used for all single U-tube applications where the two legs of the U-tube are symmetrically placed in the borehole. 
N1  - C4. The newly-derived formulas have been compared with the original multipole method, as well as the previously-derived zeroth-order and first-order explicit multipole formulas. 
N1  - C5. The thermal resistance values calculated from the second-order multipole formulas are always within 1 % of the original tenth-order multipole method. 
N1  - C6. The results shown in these figures are for a single ground thermal conductivity of 4.0 W/m-K. 
N1  - C7. The presented formulas may be used to estimate the effective borehole thermal resistance from the expressions defined by Equations 3 and 4. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. The results shown in these figures are for a single ground thermal conductivity of 4.0 W/m-K. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. The thermal resistance values calculated from the second-order multipole formulas are always within 1 % of the original tenth-order multipole method. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. The presented formulas can be used for all single U-tube applications where the two legs of the U-tube are symmetrically placed in the borehole. 
N1  - E5. The newly-derived formulas have been compared with the original multipole method, as well as the previously-derived zeroth-order and first-order explicit multipole formulas. 
N1  - E6. The thermal resistance values calculated from the second-order multipole formulas are always within 1 % of the original tenth-order multipole method. 
N1  - E7. The results shown in these figures are for a single ground thermal conductivity of 4.0 W/m-K. 
N1  - E8. The presented formulas may be used to estimate the effective borehole thermal resistance from the expressions defined by Equations 3 and 4. 
N1  - E9. Introduction To Borehole Heat Exchangers. 
N1  - E10. The use of heating and cooling systems utilizing the ground as a heat source or a heat sink has grown at a remarkable rate (Lund and Boyd, 2016), stimulated by energy prices, technology advances and environmental concerns.. 
N1  - E11. A typical ground source heating or cooling system consists of a heat pump, a ground heat exchanger, and auxiliary systems for storage and distribution of thermal energy.. 
N1  - E12. The ground heat exchanger can be of open or closed type, with a borehole heat exchanger with a single U-tube being the most commonly used ground heat exchanger in practice.. 
N1  - E13. Borehole Thermal Resistance. 
N1  - E14. Ground thermal conductivity (λ) and borehole thermal resistance (Rb) are the two principal parameters that govern the heat transfer mechanism of a borehole heat exchanger.. 
N1  - E15. The borehole thermal resistance depends upon the physical arrangement and the thermal properties of borehole components including grouting, ground heat exchanger, and the heat carrier fluid.. 
N1  - E16. The multipole method is a well-known and robust method to compute the borehole thermal resistance, but it has a fairly intricate mathematical algorithm and is thus not trivial to implement (Claesson and Hellström, 2011).. 
N1  - E17. The concept of thermal resistances in a borehole is best discussed with the help of a thermal resistance network, with several representations possible (Hellström, 1991; Liao et al, 2012; Spitler et al, 2016b).. 
N1  - E18. Multipole Formulas. 
N1  - E19. The multipole method uses a combination of line heat sources and so-called multipoles to determine thermal resistances for any number of arbitrarily placed pipes in a composite region.. 
N1  - E20. This paper presents newly derived explicit second-order multipole formulas for calculating the borehole thermal resistance and the total internal thermal resistance resistances.. 
N1  - E21. The accuracy of the presented formulas is established by comparing them to the original multipole method (i.e. the tenth order multipole calculation) (Bennet et al, 1987; Claesson, 2012).. 
N1  - E22. The superiority of the explicit second-order multipole formulas over the existing zeroth-order and first-order formulas is also demonstrated, with the general expressions for borehole thermal resistance and total internal thermal resistance derived from the thermal networks of even and the odd cases (Hellström, 1991; Javed and Spitler, 2016).. 
N1  - E23. The second-order multipole formulas for borehole thermal resistance and total internal thermal resistance are compared with previously published results using a reference dataset provided by Javed and Spitler (2017).. 
N1  - E24. The comparison shows that the second-order multipole formulas provide significant accuracy improvements over the zeroth-order and first-order multipole formulations (Javed and Spitler, 2017).. 
N1  - E25. The thermal resistance values calculated from the second-order multipole formulas are always within 1% of the original tenth-order multipole method.. 
N1  - E26. Thermal Resistance. 
N1  - E27. The total internal thermal resistance Ra between the two pipes consists of a pair of equal series resistances connected in parallel to another pair of equal series resistances (Claesson, 2016).. 
N1  - E28. The relationship between network resistances R1−b and R1−2, and even and odd thermal resistances RRJ+J and RRJ−J can be obtained from Figure 2 and Equations 6 and 7.. 
N1  - E29. The borehole thermal resistance Rb and the total internal thermal resistance Ra can be expressed in terms of thermal resistances RR2+ and RR2− using Equation 10.. 
N1  - E30. Accuracy And Implementation. 
N1  - E31. The second-order multipole formulas are recommended for calculation of borehole thermal resistance and total internal thermal resistance due to their excellent accuracy and relative ease of implementation (Paul, 1996; Javed and Spitler, 2017).. 
N1  - E32. The implementation of the second-order multipole formulas requires approximately 10 lines of coding, which is a significant improvement over the original implementation of the Multipole method (Claesson, 2016). 
