TI  - Entropy scaling framework for transport properties using molecular-based equations of state
T2  - Journal of Molecular Liquids
VL  - 395
PY  - 2023
SP  - 
EP  - 
DA  - 2023
UR  - 
DO  - 10.1016/j.molliq.2023.123811
AB  - Modeling transport properties is important for many applications. In this work, an entropy scaling framework for modeling transport properties using molecularbased equations of state (EOS) is presented. It can be applied for modeling the viscosity, thermal conductivity, and self-diffusion coefficients. The framework is formulated in a general way such that it can be coupled with various EOS. It is demonstrated that the model, when coupled to molecular-based EOS, provides not only good descriptions of existing data but also reasonable predictions in a wide range of states covering liquid, gaseous, supercritical, and meta-stable regions. Moreover, the model can be used for reliably predicting transport properties of mixtures. The universal parameters of the model were fitted to computer experiment data of the Lennard-Jones fluid. This procedure provides inherently a robust form of the basic scaling function. Thereby, only few data points are required for the determination of the component-specific model parameters. The applicability of the developed framework is demonstrated for model fluids as well as for a wide variety of real substances including non-polar, polar, and associating pure fluids and mixtures.
KW  - equations of state
KW  - thermal conductivity
KW  - PC-SAFT
KW  - dynamics
KW  - molecular dynamics
N1  - C. Highlights. 
N1  - C1. Transport properties of pure fluids and fluid mixtures are important in many disciplines of science and engineering [1,2]. 
N1  - C2. We demonstrate the flexibility of the entropy scaling framework by applying it to nine different molecular-based equations of state (EOS). 
N1  - C3. Entropy scaling requires a suitable model for calculating the entropy, which is usually accomplished by an EOS. 
N1  - C4. The entropy scaling framework proposed is designed to be coupled with molecular-based EOS, which provide in many cases robust extrapolation capabilities to conditions not used in the model development. 
N1  - C5. The EOS are integrated in the entropy scaling framework model in a consistent way by adapting the second virial coefficients, the critical point parameters, molecular property parameters, and the configurational entropy from the EOS. 
N1  - C6. The entropy scaling framework can be flexibly used in conjunction with practically any molecular-based EOS. 
N1  - D. Findings. 
N1  - D1. For the viscosity and the thermal conductivity, the vast majority of the data points is described by the entropy scaling model with a deviation below 5%; that number is 2% for the selfdiffusion coefficient. 
N1  - D2. For the vast majority of the studied pure components, a mean relative deviation below 6% is obtained. 
N1  - D3. The vast majority of predicted data points yields a deviation well below 10%. 
N1  - D4. For the data points at p &gt; 500 MPa (where also experiments are challenging to carry out), a larger deviation up to 38% is obtained. 
N1  - E. Summary. 
N1  - E1. Snapshot. 
N1  - E2. An entropy scaling framework was developed for modeling transport properties of pure fluids and mixtures. 
N1  - E3. Key findings. 
N1  - E4. We demonstrate the flexibility of the entropy scaling framework by applying it to nine different molecular-based equations of state (EOS). 
N1  - E5. Entropy scaling requires a suitable model for calculating the entropy, which is usually accomplished by an EOS. 
N1  - E6. The entropy scaling framework proposed is designed to be coupled with molecular-based EOS, which provide in many cases robust extrapolation capabilities to conditions not used in the model development. 
N1  - E7. The EOS are integrated in the entropy scaling framework model in a consistent way by adapting the second virial coefficients, the critical point parameters, molecular property parameters, and the configurational entropy from the EOS. 
N1  - E8. The entropy scaling framework can be flexibly used in conjunction with practically any molecular-based EOS. 
N1  - E9. Framework. 
N1  - E10. The entropy scaling framework is a general method for modeling transport properties, including viscosity, thermal conductivity, and self-diffusion coefficients, using molecular-based equations of state (EOS) [1,2].. 
N1  - E11. It can be coupled with various EOS and provides good descriptions of existing data and reasonable predictions in a wide range of states.. 
N1  - E12. The framework is designed to be flexible and can be used with practically any molecular-based EOS.. 
N1  - E13. Applications. 
N1  - E14. The entropy scaling framework has been demonstrated to be applicable for modeling transport properties of pure fluids and mixtures, including non-polar, polar, and associating substances.. 
N1  - E15. The framework can predict transport properties of mixtures without any additional information, based on the component-specific parameters for the pure substances.. 
N1  - E16. The model performance was evaluated using data at high pressures, up to 1000 MPa, and showed good agreement with experimental data.. 
N1  - E17. Advantages. 
N1  - E18. The entropy scaling framework has several advantages, including the ability to predict transport properties with few adjustable parameters and the ability to extrapolate to conditions not used in the model development.. 
N1  - E19. The framework can also be used to predict transport properties of mixtures using simple mixing rules, and it has been shown to yield excellent predictions for states that were not considered for the parametrization, including metastable states and extreme pressure and temperature conditions. 
